AU  - Kohno, T.
AU  - Nagano, M.
AU  - Kikunaga, S.
PY  - 2020
DO  - 10.21037/vats.2019.12.05
AB  - Background: According to the annual report of the Japanese Association for Thoracic Surgery, 67% of primary lung cancers were resected thoracoscopically in 2016. Approaches and procedures for video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) vary between institutions. We examined long-term outcomes of 3-port VATS lobectomy with selective lymphadenectomy for clinical stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Methods: Between January 2005 and December 2013, a total of 816 patients with clinical stage I NSCLC underwent 3-port VATS lobectomy with selective mediastinal lymphadenectomy and were followed-up for 5 years after surgery. Morbidity, mortality, and 5-year overall survival (OS) and disease-free survival (DFS) were reviewed and the patterns of recurrence were investigated. Results: Clinical stage was IA in 661 cases and IB in 155 cases. Three procedures (0.4%) were uneventfully converted to thoracotomy. No re-thoracotomy was performed for hemostasis and no intraoperative deaths were encountered (30-day mortality rate, 0%). Postoperative complications were identified in 84 patients (morbidity rate, 10.3%). Pathological stage was IA in 533 cases, pIB in 173, pIIA in 33, pIIB in 21 and pIIIA in 56. Median number of dissected lymph nodes was 19. Pathological nodal upstaging was noted in 101 patients (12.4%). The 5-year OS rate was 94.2% in pIA, 86.1% in pIB, 69.7% in pIIA, 81.0% in pIIB and 66.1% in pIIIA. The 5-year DFS rate was 90.8% in pIA, 78.0% in pIB, 54.5% in pIIA, 57.1% in pIIB and 39.3% in pIIIA. Recurrence of the primary disease occurred in 114 cases (33 locoregional only, 63 distant only, 18 both). Conclusions: This report suggests that 3-port VATS lobectomy with selective mediastinal lymphadenectomy represents a feasible approach for primary lung cancer, with long-term outcomes comparable or superior to those from conventional approaches. © Video-Assisted Thoracic Surgery. All rights reserved.
