AB  - Early-stage lung cancer is composed of relatively smaller tumors with at most intraparenchymal or ipsilateral hilar lymph node involvement. According to the eighth edition of the TNM stage classification, stage I represents a primary tumor ≤4 cm in size without nodal involvement or distant metastases. Stage II represents any tumor ≤5 cm with intraparenchymal or ipsilateral hilar node involvement and the absence of distant metastasis. The treatment of early-stage non-small cell lung cancer is evolving. Although the mainstay of therapy remains surgical lobectomy with lymph node sampling or dissection, parenchymal-sparing resections such as segmentectomies and wedge resections, as well as alternative therapies such as stereotactic body radiotherapy, have shown promising results in select groups of patients unable to tolerate lobectomy because of prohibitive surgical risk. Minimally invasive resections have increased in use substantially and are associated with shorter hospital stay, fewer complications, and low perioperative mortality. The use of adjuvant chemotherapy may be associated with improved survival in node-positive tumors. Although the therapeutic options for early-stage lung cancer are evolving, the basic principles of locoregional control continue to remain the same. © 2018 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
