AB  - In experienced centers, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS lobectomy) is accepted as the new standard in the treatment of stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). For higher stages with a greater chance of (unforeseen) nodal disease, the experience of the surgical team is even more important as it appears to correlate with the quality of lymph node clearance. In patients with borderline lung function who are considered not to tolerate a lobectomy, a thoracoscopic anatomical sublobar resection might be an option. Different factors have to be estimated: the diameter, the margin, the technical feasibility, the function of the residual lobe and the pathology. In patients with tumors smaller than 2 cm and a lung function allowing a lobectomy, the indications for a sublobar resection are investigated in prospective trials. Today, a (thoracoscopic) lobectomy is still considered the gold standard.
