AB  - Between July 2003 and July 2010, 397 patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) underwent lobectomy and segmentectomy with systemic lymph node dissection via video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS, n = 193) or open thoracotomy (Open, n = 204) at our institution. We retrospectively investigated postoperative recurrence, in particular, locoregional recurrence, and long-term prognosis in these two groups of patients. For clinical stage I disease, for which VATS was frequently used, there was no significant difference in the time to postoperative recurrence, frequency of postoperative recurrence, or number of recurrence sites (local/distant) in the VATS and Open groups. However, long-term prognosis was significantly better in the VATS group than in the Open group (p = 0.05). Locoregional recurrence of NSCLC postoperatively may be related to the surgical technique used. Notwithstanding, our investigation showed that both VATS and open thoracotomy can be considered to have similar efficacy.
