TI  - Large-cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the lung: A clinicopathologic study of eighteen cases and the efficacy of adjuvant treatment with octreotide
AU  - Filosso, P.L.
AU  - Ruffini, E.
AU  - Oliaro, A.
AU  - Rena, O.
AU  - Casadio, C.
AU  - Mancuso, M.
AU  - Turello, D.
AU  - Cristofori, R.C.
AU  - Maggi, G.
PY  - 2005
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtcvs.2004.05.023
AB  - Objective: This study was undertaken to evaluate the efficacy of a new adjuvant protocol with octreotide, alone or in combination with radiotherapy, in radically resected large cell neuroendocrine carcinomas of the lung. Methods: Between 1990 and 2001, a total of 18 consecutive patients affected by large cell neuroendocrine carcinomas of the lung were operated on. Lobectomy and systemic lymphadenectomy were performed in all cases. Postoperative radiotherapy was performed when stage was higher than Ib. Ten patients with positive results of preoperative indium In-111 pentetreotide scintigraphy received octreotide after the operation. Results: Nine patients (50%) had local recurrences or distant metastases (mean recurrence time 14 months); palliative chemotherapy was given, but all patients died. In 10 cases (55.5%) octreotide alone or in combination with radiotherapy was administered as adjuvant treatment; 9 of these patients (90%) are alive and free of disease (P = .0007), and the other had liver and brain metastases 21 months after surgery. Conclusions: Our preliminary results seem to demonstrate the efficacy of octreotide as adjuvant therapy in large cell neuroendocrine carcinomas of the lung when results of preoperative indium In-111 pentetreotide scintigraphy were positive. Further study are required to assess the utility of octreotide in patients with negative results of indium In-111 pentetreotide scintigraphy. Copyright © 2005 by The American Association for Thoracic Surgery.; Indications:10 patients with large-cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the lung with positive preoperative OctreoScan results.Patients:18 outpatients, 11 men and 7 women, aged 48-71 (mean 63) years. Sandostatin (with or without radiotherapy) group: 10 patients, 7 men and 3 women, aged 48-71 years. Non Sandostatin (with or without radiotherapy) group: 8 patients, 5 men and 3 women, aged 52-70 years. Duration of follow-up: 11 to 61 (mean 42) months.TypeofStudy:The efficacy of a new adjuvant protocol with Sandostatin, alone or in combination with radiotherapy, in radically resected large cell neuroendocrine carcinomas (LCNC) of the lung was evaluated. An open, comparative, controlled clinical study.DosageDuration:20 mg (long acting release) every 28 days. Duration: 11 to 61 months.Results:Nine of 10 patients (90%) treated with Sandostatin are alive and do not show tumor recurrence (P = .0007;); the other had tumor recurrences in the liver and in the brain 21 months after a left upper lobectomy for pT2 N2 LCNC, and she survived 32 months after surgery. Sandostatin was well tolerated in all cases. No patients stopped the therapy. Follow-up was planned with chest radiographs every 3 months and thoracic, upper abdomen, and brain computed tomographic scan every 6 months. For the whole study population, tumor relapses occurred in 9 patients: 8 of these (88.8%) had not received Sandostatin as adjuvant therapy. Mean recurrence time was 19 months after surgery (range 9-41 months). OctreoScan was effective in detecting the recurrence sites in all patients. Palliative chemotherapy was proposed for all 9 patients with tumor recurrence, including 1 receiving Sandostatin therapy. Overall, median survival was 42 months. The 3- and 5 year survival rates were 63% and 35%, respectively.AdverseEffects:Unspecified number of patients developed pain at the site of injection, transient nausea, abdominal cramps and diarrhea.AuthorsConclusions:In conclusion, LCNC is a relatively uncommon high-grade tumor of the lung, characterized by poor prognosis and high rates of recurrences even in early stages. Surgery alone is ineffective in prolonging survival, and an optimal adjuvant therapy has still not been identified. This is the first description of the effectiveness of octreotide, alone or in combination with radiotherapy, as adjuvant treatment in radically resected LCNC of the lung. According to our preliminary results, and despite the limitations of this study (small number of cases and retrospective design), we propose the routine use of this protocol as postoperative treatment, even if surgery has been radical.FreeText:All patients underwent radical tumor resection with lobectomy and systemic lymphadenectomy. Patients with a TNM pathologic stage higher than Ib received postoperative radiotherapy. Bronchoscopy was performed in all patients; the diagnosis of NSCLC was made in 5 (27.7%), and the diagnosis of atypical bronchial carcinoid was made in 1 (5.5%). In-111 pentetreotide scintigraphy was routinely performed in the preoperative assessment and follow-up of all patients with suspected or proven neuroendocrine tumor of the chest. Ten patients (55.5%) were submitted to OctreoScan because of high NSE and chromogranin A serum levels or preoperative diagnosis of neuroendocrine tumor. Preoperative OctreoScan showed pathologic uptake corresponding to the tumor only. All patients were directly followed up with periodic outpatient clinics. Somatostatin receptors (sst2 and sst5) were detected by polymerase chain reaction technique in all patients with positive results of preoperative OctreoScan. Adjuvant therapy (radiotherapy) was administered to al patients with a cancer stage higher than Ib. Patients received Sandostatin 1 month after surgery. Concomitant drugs: cisplatin (or carboplatin), etoposide and taxol (1).
