AB  - Background Surgery is the gold standard to cure early-stage lung cancer. However, even in stage I lung carcinoma, 5 years recurrence rate is around 25%. Factors associated with recurrence are largely unknown; especially in patients operated on according to the recommendations of lobe-specific lymphadenectomy. Objective To examine factors associated with local and metastatic recurrence, and those linked to 3-years disease-free survival (DFS), and overall survival (OS) following complete resection in early-stage pathological node-negative (pN0) non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC) without adjuvant therapy. Methods We performed a retrospective analysis of a single-center prospectively maintained database of patients with pT1-2aN0M0 non-small cell lung cancer between 2015 and 2017. We did not consider patients with adjuvant therapy. We excluded patients with other metachronous neoplasm. The primary outcome was distant or local recurrence. Secondary outcomes were 3-years DFS and OS. Statistical significance for survival was assessed using the Fine and Gray subdivision hazard for competitive risk analysis; cumulative incidences were estimated with Gray’s test. Results Of 237 patients identified with pT1-2aN0M0 lung carcinoma in the interest period, 170 were included in the statistical analysis. 36 patients (21%) showed recurrence on the follow-up and 29 (17%) within the 3 years after surgery. Lymphovascular invasion, visceral pleural invasion, PET-CT SUVmax (SUVmax > 4.2) and the time from pre-operative PET-CT to surgery (> 45 days) were strongly associated with early recurrence in univariate and multivariate analysis. Conclusion In stage I NSCLC patients, efforts are required to speed up the initial work-up to quickly operate this population.
