AB  - Objective. We aimed to clarify the surgical outcomes of localized lymph node recurrence after non-small cell lung cancer resection. Methods. We retrospectively reviewed seven cases in which surgery was performed for hilar or mediastinal lymph node recurrence after non-small cell lung cancer resection. Results. All patients were male. The median age was 66 years. The patients underwent lobectomy at the initial surgery. Lobe-specific mediastinal lymph node dissection was performed in six cases and systematic lymph node dissection was performed in one case. The histology was squamous cell carcinoma in four cases, and large cell carcinoma, large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma, and adenosquamous cell carcinoma in one case each. The pathological stage was stage I in four cases, II in one cases, and III in two cases. The median disease-free interval was 12 months. The recurrence site was mediastinal in six cases and hilar in one case. In six of the seven cases, the histology of the resected lymph node was the same as that in the first surgery. In the adenosquamous case, the squamous cell component metastasized. The final outcome was lung cancer death in three cases and long-term survival in four cases. All long-term survivors experienced recurrence of the squamous cell carcinoma component. The 5-year overall survival rate after lymph node resection was 57.1%. Conclusion. More than half of the patients had long-term survival after surgery for lymph node recurrence. It is important to consider surgery for localized lymph node recurrence, which may result in favorable outcomes, especially in squamous cell carcinoma cases.
