TI  - Era of Immunotherapy for Early Stage Non-small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC): Pathologic Complete Response in Stage II NSCLC With Neoadjuvant Chemoimmunotherapy
AU  - Vasic, I.
AU  - Velotta, J.B.
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.Abstracts.A2767
AB  - Introduction: Lung cancer remains the top cancer killer worldwide, primarily due to advanced presentation. However, up to 40% of cases still present with early-stage disease. Previously, standard management for resectable stage II NSCLC typically involved surgery followed by adjuvant chemotherapy, however recent interest has grown around neoadjuvant therapies, particularly the combination of chemotherapy and immunotherapy. Although neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy is not yet a universal approach for early-stage NSCLC, it is under active investigation. This case of pathologic complete response following neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy demonstrates the potential benefits of such an approach for early-stage NSCLC, especially in patients with high PDL1 expression, and supports the evolving role of immunotherapy in early stage NSCLC.Case: A 74- year-old Asian male with no history of smoking presented with shortness of breath and underwent a routine chest X-ray, which revealed a 6 cm mass in the left upper lobe (LUL). A follow-up chest CT scan confirmed a 5.6 x 5.2 x 6.4 cm mass in the LUL with associated ground-glass opacity. Imageguided biopsy confirmed lung adenocarcinoma, and next generation sequencing (NGS) testing revealed no targetable mutations and a PD-L1 expression of 100%. PET scan showed an increased SUV uptake in the primary tumor with no evidence of lymph node or distant metastases, and endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) demonstrated negative mediastinal lymph node biopsies. The patient received three cycles of neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy with Nivolumab, Cisplatin, and Pemetrexed per the CheckMate 816 protocol. Following neoadjuvant treatment, he underwent a left video-assisted thoracoscopic (VATS) left upper lobectomy with mediastinal lymph node dissection of levels 5-12. Final surgical pathology showed no evidence of viable tumor cells, with all 21 lymph nodes negative for adenocarcinoma. His post-operative recovery was unremarkable and was discharged on POD 2. He is currently on surveillance CT scan protocols every 6 months with no evidence of disease recurrence. Discussion: This case highlights the efficacy of neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy in achieving a complete pathological response in early-stage NSCLC. The combination of Nivolumab, an immune checkpoint inhibitor, with Cisplatin and Pemetrexed, a platinum-based chemotherapy regimen, capitalizes on the synergy between immune activation and cytotoxic effects, improving the likelihood of complete tumor eradication prior to surgery. This approach is particularly relevant for patients who lack any actionable mutations which underscores the importance of expediting early preoperative NGS results. Our patient's response highlights the importance of providing more routine preoperative chemoimmunotherapy for earlier stage NSCLC patients to improve survival.
ID  - 2280
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Remote Lung Cancer Recurrence in Surgical Staple Site Successfully Diagnosed With Endobronchial Ultrasound and Treated With Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy Alone
AU  - Teves, J.
AU  - Villanueva, C.G.
AU  - Cereno, R.P.
AU  - Villalobos, R.
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.Abstracts.A4842
AB  - Introduction: Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related mortality in the Philippines and worldwide. Endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) has emerged the procedure of choice in diagnosing lung and mediastinal masses, with its minimally invasive approach in sampling mediastinal structures, peripheral nodules and endobronchial lesions smaller than 2cm. This negates the need for an invasive surgical approach, decreasing operative complications and hospital costs. At early stages, lung resection surgery is first-line; however, for those unable to tolerate due to poor lung reserve or other medical conditions, stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) as part of the standard of care, is becoming increasingly accessible. Case description: A 68 year old female who previously underwent curative surgery for Stage 1 lung adenocarcinoma came in due to possible tumor recurrence. Four years into her disease surveillance, Chest CT and PET scan revealed a right hilar soft tissue density with 0.7 x 0.5 cm size adjacent to the metallic clips of the previous lobectomy site. After 1 year, it increased to 1.1 x 1.2 cm and became FDG-avid, hence tumor recurrence was considered. Due to the technical difficulty of accessing the lesion percutaneously, its small size, hilar location and proximity to vessels, she was referred to our institution for EBUS-guided biopsy. Bronchoscopy showed intact right lower lobectomy stump with mucosal irregularities and grossly irregular surgical staples (Figure 1). Linear EBUS of the right hilar lesion showed heterogeneous echogenicity, and transbronchial needle aspiration biopsy was positive for non-small cell carcinoma. With poor calculated lung function post-surgery, she was referred to radiation oncology and received a total dose of 6,000 cGy in fifteen (15) fractions to the right hilar mass as curative treatment. All procedures were successfully done as outpatient, obviating risk of an expensive, morbid open thoracic surgery. Discussion: Staple-line thickening adjacent to postsurgical sites in NSCLC is frequent, but tumor recurrence is rare. This report highlights the value of EBUS not just in early detection of cancer, but also its recurrence in rare sites inaccessible by conventional bronchoscopic modalities. With limitations of age and comorbidities, local recurrences can be treated with SBRT over salvage surgery, which offers equivalent local disease control.
ID  - 2281
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Valve-ued Decisions: Bronchoscopic Lung Volume Reduction in a Patient With Squamous Cell Carcinoma
AU  - Hurwitz, A.
AU  - Moore, B.
AU  - Ahmad, S.
AU  - Hoy, S.L.
AU  - Bechara, R.I.
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.Abstracts.A4844
AB  - Introduction: Bronchoscopic lung volume reduction (BLVR) is generally contraindicated in patients with active malignancy. We present a case of a patient with severe emphysema who underwent BLVR and was subsequently diagnosed with squamous cell carcinoma (SCC), ultimately requiring management with photodynamic therapy (PDT) without removal of the valves. Case: A 68-year-old male with severe emphysema and a 25-pack-year smoking history underwent placement of four endobronchial valves in the left lower lobe. During the procedure, an unexpected superficial abnormality was identified in the bronchus intermedius, which could not be seen on prior imaging. Later, biopsy confirmed invasive SCC. Invasive mediastinal staging using endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) revealed no signs of lymphangitic spread. PET and EBUS staging confirmed that the malignancy was confined to the endobronchial region. Given the patient's poor surgical candidacy in the setting of stage I SCC, lung resection was not offered. However, the patient experienced a remarkable improvement in quality of life immediately post BLVR, and the decision was made to keep the valves in place during treatment. Local treatment with endoscopic ablation and multiple rounds of PDT was initiated for the primary SCC. Post bronchial biopsies thus far have been negative for malignancy. Discussion: The gold standard for stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is surgical resection. If he was a candidate, the valves would have been removed prior to surgery. As this was not an option and he had such a profound symptomatic improvement, we decided to proceed with staging and treatment of his lung cancer without removal of the valves. The valves did not lose effectiveness despite multiple bronchoscopies that could have dislodged the valves. Conclusion: This case illustrates that, with a multidisciplinary approach, BLVR can be safely maintained in select patients with emphysema even after the discovery of concurrent early-stage malignancy. Despite concerns regarding cancer diagnosis post-BLVR, effective local cancer control was achieved through ablation and PDT. This case underscores the crucial role of individualized decision-making in complex patients, empowering clinicians to consider concurrent management of emphysema and early-stage lung cancer.
