TI  - Comparison of outcomes between anatomical segmentectomy and stereotactic body radiation therapy in early-stage non-small cell lung cancer: A single-center retrospective analysis
AU  - Costa, R.
AU  - Elzen, C.V.D.
AU  - Kostopanagiotou, K.
AU  - Sousa, F.
AU  - Lopes, J.C.
AU  - Moreira, A.L.
AU  - Osório, L.
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.1016/S1556-0864(25)00354-5
AB  - Background Early-stage lung cancer management has evolved beyond traditional lobectomy. Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) offers a non-surgical approach for medically inoperable patients and segmentectomy provides lung preservation, direct outcome comparisons between these modalities remain controversial. This study evaluated clinical outcomes of SBRT versus segmentectomy in stage IA-IB NSCLC patients. Methods A retrospective analysis was conducted of stage IA-IB NSCLC patients treated with either segmentectomy or SBRT at our institution (2020–2023). Treatment selection was determined by a multidisciplinary tumor board. Primary endpoints were overall survival, disease-free survival, and local recurrence rates. Baseline characteristics were adjusted using multivariate analysis. Multivariate analysis was performed to adjust for baseline differences between groups. Results Eighty-three patients were analyzed (22 segmentectomy, 60 SBRT), with a median follow-up of 22.1 months. The segmentectomy group was younger (median age 68.4 vs. 72.1 years, P = 0.030) and had better performance status (ECOG 0–1: 90.9% vs. 82.8%, P < 0.001). SBRT patients presented a more severe COPD grade, a higher prevalence of cardiovascular diseases and other respiratory diseases (P < 0.001). At 18-months, OS favored segmentectomy group (85.7% vs. 77.1%, P = 0.045), while DFS was superior in SBRT (89.2% vs. 76.2%, P = 0.031). Locoregional recurrence rates were 0% and 6% in the segmentectomy and SBRT groups, respectively (P = 0.051). Treatment-related toxicity analysis revealed grade 1/2 acute adverse events in 15% of SBRT patients with no grade ≥3 complications, whereas 9.1% of surgical patients experienced grade ≥3 complications. Conclusions SBRT demonstrated comparable oncological outcomes to segmentectomy with an acceptable safety profile, particularly in medically compromised patients. While segmentectomy achieved better overall survival, SBRT’s favorable disease-free survival and toxicity profile support its role as a viable alternative, suggesting treatment selection should be individualized based on patient characteristics and comorbidities. Legal entity responsible for the study The authors. Funding Has not received any funding. Disclosure All authors have declared no conflicts of interest.
