AB  - Introduction The landscape for early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) has changed following landmark studies such as Checkmate 816, Keynote 671 and AEGEAN. We aim to review the outcomes of patients treated with neoadjuvant systemic therapy (NACT) in the East Midlands. Methods A retrospective regional review was carried out (registered in Leicester and Nottingham). Patients with NSCLC who were treated with NACT between March 2023 to November 2024 were identified using an electronic database (ChemoCare). Demographic, clinical, toxicity and surgical outcome data were collected via retrospective review of electronic patient records. Seven patients were excluded; one did not receive NACT and six had ongoing treatment at time of cut-off. Results A total of 24 patients were included in analysis. There were 15 males (63%), median age was 69 (range 52–84), 23 (96%) identified as white and 16 (67%) were ex-smokers. Performance status was either 0 (14, 58%) or 1 (10, 42%) and Charlson Comorbidity Index ≥5 in 15 patients (63%). 15 patients (63%) had nodal involvement, 10 (42%) had N2 disease. There was an almost equal split between squamous (10, 42%) and non-squamous (11, 46%); the remaining 3 (13%) not otherwise specified. All were ALK and EGFR negative and PDL1 expression was typically either less than 1% (9, 38%) or 1–49% (9, 38%). 20 patients (83%) completed three cycles of NACT, and 11 (46%) received carboplatin and paclitaxel. 15 (63%) had partial or complete radiological response. Grade 3–4 toxicities were observed in 4 patients (17%) and one patient died due to toxicity. 18 patients (75%) had surgery, of whom 15 had a lobectomy, and R0 resection was achieved in all. Pathological complete response was observed in 7 (29%). Conclusions Our real-world data shows NACT is feasible within NHS centres, however patient selection is key to improving patient outcomes. Disclosure No significant relationships.
