TI  - Spread through airway spaces (STAS): fact or an artifact in non-small cell lung cancer?
AU  - Jethani, M.
AU  - Mistry, R.
AU  - Safi, C.
AU  - Deshkulakarani, S.
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejso.2024.109205
AB  - Background: In Lung cancer, tumor invades through stroma, vessels or pleura. Tumor spread through air spaces is recently considered by WHO as a novel pattern of invasion. STAS in lung cancer has been reported to have its association with poor prognosis. The aim of this study was to evaluate the correlation between STAS & clinicopathological features and its prognostic implications in patients with NSCLC. Material and methods: This is a single institute retrospective observational study of all patients of resectable NSCLC from Jan 2017 till Dec 2022. Data regarding clinical and demographic characteristics, type of resections and histopathology with its molecular markers were obtained from electronic medical records. Results (For Surgical Trial Proposals fill in ‘the Feasibility’, for Surgical Trial in Progress fill in the ‘Current status’): A total of 113 patients were included in this study. 65.5% were male, median age being 65 years. 73.5% were adenocarcinoma, 15.9% SCC, 8% Adenosquamous and 2.7% other histology. Out of 113 patients, STAS was observed in 50.5 % of patients who underwent lobectomy and 58.3% of patients who had sublobar resections. According to histological subtype, STAS was observed in 56.6% (47/83) of total adenocarcinoma and 38.8% (7/18) of total squamous cell carcinoma patients. In Adenocarcinoma, STAS was associated with wild type of EGFR. Median follow up was 27.4 months (6 to 60 months). The median disease-free survival (DFS) was 15.8 months whereas overall survival (OS) was 22.4 months. Considering all stages that were included in the study, OS difference, though not statistically significant was there in first two years (p< 0.07) but not in DFS. For stage I tumors, statistically significant difference was there in OS (p< 0.041) but not in DFS (p< 0.065). Conclusions (For Surgical Trial in Progress and Surgical Trial Proposals fill in ‘NA’): STAS is a biological phenomenon rather than an artifact. STAS is a risk factor for recurrence, mainly exists for stage I Adenocarcinoma. STAS may help clinicians for decision making in adjuvant therapy. Thus, STAS is one of the emerging prognostic factors in NSCLC management.
