TI  - CHALLENGES OF INTERVENTIONAL PULMONOLOGY: NEUROENDOCRINE TRANSFORMATION AND NEED OF COLLABORATIVE CARE
AU  - Razia, D.
AU  - Ali, E.
AU  - Khokar, A.I.
AU  - Saeed, A.I.
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.1016/j.chest.2024.06.2625
AB  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Cancer Case Reports Posters (J) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Histologic spectrum of lung neuroendocrine tumors (LNETs) determines their biologic behavior; typical LNETs are well-differentiated, low-grade tumors with low mitotic rate, and atypical LNETs are intermediate-grade tumors with the high mitotic rate and/or necrosis. Typical LNETs commonly present as stage I tumors, whereas atypical LNETs are stage II (bronchopulmonary nodal involvement) or III (mediastinal nodal involvement) at presentation.Neuroendocrine transformation is a resistance mechanism reported in patients with non-small cell lung cancer. We describe a unique patient who experienced neuroendocrine transformation from typical to atypical LNET in 8 months, requiring multidisciplinary tumor board discussion. CASE PRESENTATION: 48-year-old never-smoker gentleman was referred to our interventional pulmonology (IP) clinic in February 2023 due to a 2.5x2.3x1.8cm endobronchial mass occluding the right main stem and right paratracheal nodes, associated with cough and weight loss. Outside pathology from right mainstem lesion was unremarkable. He underwent bronchoscopy with electrocautery snare for resection, argon photocoagulation for tumor destruction and sampling of node stations 11L, 4L, 7 and 4R. Pathology of mainstem bronchus lesion demonstrated typical LNET with MIB–1 proliferation of 1.2% and mitotic rate 0–1 mitosis/2mm²; lymph node samples were benign. Outside hospital dotatate PET showed SUV of 25.7 in right mainstem lesion. In April 2023, patient underwent repeat bronchoscopy with endobronchial tumor debridement; pathology was congruent with initial findings. Thoracic surgery had recommended lobar sleeve resection with regional lymphadenectomy, and oncologist had recommended radiation. However, patient refused both treatments. He was referred back to IP in November 2023. Surveillance bronchoscopy at 8-month interval in December 2023 was performed. Tissue pathology from right mainstem lesion demonstrated atypical LNET; neoplastic proliferation was noted with MIB–1 proliferation of 5.9% and mitotic rate 5–6 mitoses/2mm². Patient is awaiting his oncologist appointment and will be followed by IP for surveillance in 3 months. DISCUSSION: Typical LNETs have excellent prognosis following surgical resection with 5- and 10-year survival of 87-100% and 82-87% respectively.[1] Atypical LNETs have 5- and 10-year survival of 30-95% and 35-56% respectively[2], with greater tendency to metastasize (liver/bone) and recur locally. Standard oncologic resection including lobectomy or pneumonectomy may be necessary for atypical LNETs.There is no consensus on optimal approach to adjuvant therapy. The National comprehensive cancer network (NCCN) recommends consideration of adjuvant cisplatin or carboplatin plus etoposide with/without radiotherapy for patients with completely resected, stage-IIIa or N2 atypical LNETs. For patients with unresectable or margin positive atypical LNETs, recommendation is radiotherapy with/without adjuvant chemotherapy. CONCLUSIONS: Bronchoscopic management of LNETS may be suboptimal method of definitive treatment, and is best reserved for palliation in poor surgical candidates with typical and atypical LNETs. For typical LNETs, guidelines recommend no surveillance for node-negative disease except in those with tumor size>3 cm, close margins on resected tumor, or multifocal disease. For atypical LNETs, the surgical guidelines for non-small cell lung cancer are followed, and surveillance is recommended every 6 months for 2 years and then annually for life based on ACCP guidelines 6.4.2. In the current report we describe transformation of typical carcinoid tumor into atypical carcinoid 8 months after resection. REFERENCE #1: Ferguson MK, Landreneau RJ, Hazelrigg SR, et al. Long-term outcome after resection for bronchial carcinoid tumors. Eur J Cardiothorac Surg 2000; 18:156. REFERENCE #2: Gustafsson BI, Kidd M, Chan A, et al. Bronchopulmonary neuroendocrine tumors. Cancer 2008; 113:5. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Emaan Ali No relevant relationships by Abid Khokar No relevant relationships by Deepika Razia No relevant relationships by Ali Saeed
ID  - 2322
