TY  - JOUR
TI  - Comparative Effectiveness of Sub-Lobar Resection vs Lobectomy for Medically Operable Stage I NSCLC: A VACCR Analysis
AU  - Snider, M.
AU  - Salama, J.K.
AU  - Boyer, M.J.
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2024.09.423
AB  - Introduction: Lobectomy is standard of care surgical management for early-stage, non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), with sub-lobar resection reserved for patients lacking sufficient pulmonary reserve or who have small, peripheral tumors. Outside of a clinical trial, the role of sub-lobar resection for early-stage NSCLC irrespective of tumor location is not well defined. This study sought to compare overall survival (OS) and lung cancer-specific survival (LCSS) following lobectomy or sub-lobar resection in patients treated for Stage I NSCLC in an open access healthcare setting. Methods: Patients diagnosed with Stage I NSCLC between 2000-2020 from the Veterans’ Affairs Health System with an FEV1 or DLCO > 60% of predicted treated with lobectomy or sub-lobar resection were identified. OS and LCSS were compared using log-rank and Kaplan-Meier analyses, with hazard ratios calculated. Multivariable Cox regression analysis via stepwise approach included age, diagnosis period, sex, race, smoking status, FEV1, DLCO, tumor histology, and clinical T stage as covariates. Surgery type was included ad hoc for clinical suspicion. Results: Overall, 1,267 patients were included. Patients were predominantly male (97%) and white (75%). 73% of patients had a Charlson comorbidity index of 0. Most sub-lobar resection patients underwent wedge resection (79%). Sub-lobar resection patients were older (mean age 68 vs 66, p < 0.001) and had worse lung function (mean predicted FEV1: 84% vs 91%; DLCO: 77% vs 85%, p < 0.001 for both) compared to lobectomy patients, respectively. Median follow-up was 5.6 years and 5-year OS was 58% (95% CI 56-61%). 90-day mortality rates were equivalent between lobectomy and sub-lobar resection (2.6% vs 2.4%, p > 0.05). Multivariable analysis showed no significant difference in OS and LCSS between surgery types (Table 1). Age, diagnosis period, and DLCO were significant predictors of OS and LCSS. Sex and smoking status were additionally found to be significant predictors of OS. When considering surgery type and these demographic factors, DLCO appears to be a predictor of mortality after surgery, while FEV1 does not. Conclusions: In medically operable patients undergoing sub-lobar resection or lobectomy for Stage I NSCLC, there is no difference in OS and LCSS on multivariable analysis, indicating the two are equivalent in this real world, open access healthcare setting. With recent phase III trials JCOG0802/WJOG4607L and CALGB 140503 supporting the use of sub-lobar resection for small peripheral tumors, this data supports future studies investigating the role of sub-lobar resection in an expanded population of medically operable patients. Keywords: Sub-lobar Resection, Lobectomy, Survival
