DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2024.05.202
AB  - Background: The standard of care for resectable, early-stage NSCLC includes biomarker testing for EGFR genomic alterations and PD-L1 expression following the recent FDA approvals of adjuvant osimertinib (2020) and adjuvant immunotherapies (atezolizumab, 2021; pembrolizumab, 2022). Our aim was to describe management of resected NSCLC before and concurrent with evolving recommendations for biomarker testing and adjuvant therapy. Methods: Using the nationwide Flatiron Health deidentified, longitudinal EHR-derived database, we selected patients with stage II-IIIB(N2) NSCLC diagnosed beginning 1-Jan-2019, who received no neoadjuvant therapy and had tumor resection by 31-Jan-2023, with data cutoff on 31-July-2023. Results: Of 1050 eligible patients (57%/40% stage II/III, 3% unknown), 51% were women. Median age was 70 years (range 29-85); 88% had smoking history; 91% with known performance status had ECOG 0-1 (808/885); 71% of tumors were nonsquamous. Tumors tested for PD-L1 rose from 40% (2019) to 75% (2022); EGFR testing rose from 34% to 68%. Overall, PD-L1 and EGFR testing were conducted a median of 32 (range 0-227) and 40 (range, 0-228) days after NSCLC diagnosis, respectively. Lobectomy was the most common surgery (Table). A slight upward trend in use of adjuvant therapy was observed (from 62% to 70%, 2019 to 2022). Overall, 624 of 960 patients with complete resection received adjuvant therapy (65%); the majority (91%) received platinum-based chemotherapy. Among 170/624 patients (27%) who then received subsequent adjuvant treatment, over one-half (64%) received PD-(L)1-inhibitor-based regimens (Table). Conclusions: We observed increasing rates of PD-L1 and EGFR testing from 2019 to 2022 among patients with resected NSCLC; however, approximately one-quarter were still not tested in 2022. Only approximately two-thirds of patients received adjuvant therapy following complete resection. Platinum chemotherapy remained the most common initial adjuvant therapy, while PD-(L)1 inhibitor-based regimens were most common as subsequent therapy. [Formula presented]
