AB  - Background: Lung cancer (LC) is the primary cause of US cancer deaths, with varied survival rates based on the diagnosis stage. The 5-year relative survival for localized LC in the SEER database is 62.8% (CI 62.3, 63.3). However, there is limited research on LC outcomes in SNH. Out of 282,011 LC patients undergoing elective lung lobectomies nationwide, 14.5% were treated at SNH, such as John Peter Smith (JPS) hospital, which serves Tarrant County in Texas. We present early-stage NSCLC patient outcomes at JPS and evaluate the impact of low-dose CT scan (LDCT) screening. Methods: A retrospective chart review was conducted on patients diagnosed with early-stage NSCLC at JPS from 2013 to 2021. Data on demographics, treatment, vital status, cause of death, and LDCT status were collected. Survival probabilities were assessed using Kaplan Meier, log-rank tests, and Cox-proportional hazards (CPH) models. Results: Analysis included 123 early-stage NSCLC patients, excluding cases with favorable histology (11 carcinoids, 3 acinar cell tumors). Definitive treatment was surgery in 68 pts (50 lobectomy, 14 wedge resection), radiation in 23 patients, and no treatment in 32 patients. Among biopsy-confirmed cancer patients, the median time to surgical treatment was 66 days (IQR 53-101). Of the 52 deceased patients, 40 died from LC, 6 from a different cancer, and 6 from other causes. The overall survival rates were 84% at one year and 46% (CI 36.1, 57.7) at five years, favoring females and Asians. Surgery resulted in a higher survival rate (74%) compared to radiation (43%, p=0.0066). LDCT showed higher survival probabilities but lacked statistical significance. Conclusions: Patients treated for early-stage NSCLC at JPS have lower 5-year survival rates than SEER. Survival rates are improving for LDCT recipients. Almost a third of patients did not receive LC treatment, partly due to socio-economic factors and comorbidities associated with SNH. [Formula presented]
