TI  - PP01.01 High-risk histopathological correlates of stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) outcomes
AU  - Locke, M.
AU  - Aung, W.Y.
AU  - Seetharamu, N.
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2024.05.250
AB  - Background: Lung cancer is one of the leading causes of cancer-related mortality. Risk stratification of early-stage lung cancers is thus essential to management, and histopathological features can be powerful prognostic tools. While the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) 8th edition has recognized and upstaged visceral pleural invasion (VPI) to Stage IB, other histological features such as lymphovascular invasion (LVI) are not accounted for. The objective of this study is to evaluate LVI, VPI micropapillary pattern, and spread through airspaces (STAS) as histopathological correlates to outcomes in stage 1 non-small cell lung cancers (NSCLC). Methods: Retrospective chart review was conducted on 740 patients who underwent lung resection at a single academic center between 2015-2019 with pathology confirming NSCLC. A total of 597 patients with resected stage I NSCLC were included in the analysis. Patients treated with neoadjuvant chemotherapy or with stage II or above cancers were excluded. Medical records were reviewed for demographics, smoking history, histopathological features, TNM staging (AJCC 7th edition), recurrence, and death. Charlson Comorbidity Index (CCI) was calculated, and Recurrence Free Survival (RFS) was estimated via the Kaplan-Meier Method. Correlation between STAS, VPI, LVI, micropapillary pattern, and patient outcomes was examined via multivariate Cox proportional hazards regression. All statistical analyses were performed in R and visualized in GraphPad Prism 10.0.2. Results: Mean age at diagnosis was 69.9 years (range 40-93). Most patients were female (62.3%) and smokers (71.7%). Adenocarcinoma was the most common pathology (82.7%), and most specimens were obtained via lobectomy (51.4%). Median CCI was 6. 44 patients (7.3%) had eventual recurrence on follow-up. 33 patients had LVI, 47 patients had VPI, 38 patients had STAS, 1 patient had perineural invasion, and 75 patients had micropapillary involvement. Median RFS at 3 years was 88.2%. STAS, VPI, and micropapillary pattern had non-significant associations with RFS. In multivariate analysis, LVI was an independent predictor for shorter RFS (HR 3.325, p-value 0.0009, 95% CI 1.503 to 5.711). Conclusions: LVI was a strong independent predictor associated with postoperative recurrence in stage I NSCLC. LVI and other histopathological features can be powerful tools in risk stratification of early-stage lung cancers.
