DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2024.05.326
AB  - Background: Sublobar resection has been established as standard of care for early-stage lung cancer. Randomized trials have demonstrated that anatomic sublobar resection (segmentectomy) may be superior to lobectomy with regard to overall survival among patients with tumors ≤2cm. However, whether patients with tumors >2cm-3cm are at increased risk of upstaging and worse overall survival if treated with segmentectomy is unknown. Therefore, the purpose of this study is to investigate the association between upstaging and overall survival for patients with tumors >2cm-3cm who received segmentectomy or lobectomy. Methods: The National Cancer Database (NCDB) was used to identify patients who underwent segmentectomy or lobectomy for clinical stage I (tumor >2cm-3cm) non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC) between 2010 and 2017. Patients who underwent wedge resection, pneumonectomy or had missing stage were excluded. Bivariable analysis was used to evaluate differences between the extent of surgical resection and patient, clinical, hospital, and sociodemographic characteristics. Propensity-score matching (1:1, nearest-neighbor) was used to create a cohort comparing patients who were upstaged within each extent of surgical resection. Cox Proportional Hazards models and Kaplan-Meier curves were used to evaluate the association between extent of surgical resection, pathological upstaging, and overall survival. Results: The study identified 25,844 patients with surgically treated clinical stage I (tumor >2cm-3cm) NSCLC, with 1,291 (5.0%) segmentectomies and 24,553 (95.0%) lobectomies. Overall, 13.3% and 18.1% of patients who received segmentectomy and lobectomy were upstaged to pathological stage II or greater, respectively (p<0.001). The propensity-score matched cohort was well-balanced and included 2,580 patients (50% segmentectomy) with 172 (6.7%) upstaged after segmentectomy and 235 (9.1%) after lobectomy. Upstaging after segmentectomy was associated with similar overall survival compared to upstaging after lobectomy in both unadjusted (HR 1.19, 95% CI 0.95-1.50) and adjusted (aHR 0.90, 95% CI 0.66-1.23) Cox models as well as Kaplan-Meier curves (log-rank, p=0.31). Conclusions: Upstaging from clinical stage I (tumor >2cm-3cm) to pathological stage II or greater is more commonly reported for patients who received lobectomy compared with segmentectomy. Patients who are upstaged after segmentectomy have similar overall survival compared to patients upstaged after lobectomy.
