TI  - Surgery versus stereotactic radiotherapy in patients over 75 treated for stage IA-IIA NSCLC
AU  - Oliver, G.
AU  - Couderc, A.-L.
AU  - Boucekine, M.
AU  - Zacchariotto, A.
AU  - Kaeppelin, B.
AU  - Alex, F.
AU  - Thomas, P.-A.
AU  - Padovani, L.
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.1016/S0167-8140(24)03082-2
AB  - Purpose/Objective: Lobectomy with lymph node dissection remains the standard treatment for IA-IIA NSCLC1,2. Stereotactic ablative radiotherapy (SABR) is now a standard of care for inoperable early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)3. Its effectiveness, and noninvasiveness have led to make SABR a standard treatment for patients considered at high surgical risk. Taking into account the increased risk of early mortality after surgery, the decision making process regarding elderly patients remains challenging because of the high level of comorbities4-7. Our objective is to assess and compare the overall survival and locoregional recurrence for elderly patients treated by surgery or SABR for localized NSCLC using a prospective surgical national data base. Material/Methods: We conducted a single-center retrospective study between January 2012 and December 2022 including patients over 75 years of age who received surgery or SABR for NSCLC less than 5cm in size, N0, M08. Data for surgery cohort were collected from the EPITHOR® database of the French Society of Thoracic Surgery. Data for SABR cohort were collected from patient files. Patients' comorbidities were collected by an oncogeriatric assessment if the ONCODAGE score was < 149. A cumulative comorbidity index was calculated for each patient, considering severity and impact on treatment. We performed subgroup analyses using the CART method to identify factors impacting survival and early death. Results: 389 patients were included in the study, 288 of whom underwent surgery and 101 received SABR. After propensity score matching for age, gender, tumor size and comorbidity index, 127 operated patients were matched with 85 patients treated with SABR. In the matched population overall survival at 1 and 5 years in the was 83.87% and 47.30% versus 88.8% and 31.5% respectively in the surgery and SABR cohort p=0.068. Patient survival was better in SABR-treated patients in the first year, due to the strong impact of early surgery-related mortality. In the matched population, the number of deaths within 90 days of treatment was 8 in surgical patients (6.3%) and 1 in SABRtreated patients (1.2%). In multivariate analysis, only WHO status was significantly associated with survival p=0.02 HR 1.47 (1.37-4.47). Subgroup analysis of overall survival highlighted the major impact of DLCO and comorbidity index (figure 1). We performed a decision making score to improve the selection of patients eligible for surgery, in a goal to reduce the early post-operative morality and finally improve the overall survival of elderly population treated for early NSLC (figure 2). Conclusion: Surgery seems to remain the standard of treatment in terms of overall survival and locoregional recurrence. Nevertheless, SABR offers excellent local control and short- and long-term tolerance. To improve patient selection, we propose for the first time a tool to aid therapeutic decision making. Keywords: Elderly, stereotactic radiotherapy, early stage References: 1. Ettinger DS, Wood DE, Aisner DL, Akerley W, Bauman JR, Bharat A, et al. Non–Small Cell Lung Cancer, Version 3.2022, NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology. J Natl Compr Canc Netw. mai 2022;20(5):497-530. 2. Howington JA, Blum MG, Chang AC, Balekian AA, Murthy SC. Treatment of Stage I and II Non-small Cell Lung Cancer. Chest. mai 2013;143(5):e278S-e313S. 3. Timmerman R. Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy for Inoperable Early Stage Lung Cancer. JAMA. 17 mars 2010;303(11):1070. 4. Pezzi CM, Mallin K, Mendez AS, Greer Gay E, Putnam JB. Ninety-day mortality after resection for lung cancer is nearly double 30-day mortality. J Thorac Cardiovasc Surg. nov 2014;148(5):2269-78. 5. Thomas PA, Couderc AL, Boulate D, Greillier L, Charvet A, Brioude G, et al. Early-stage non-small cell lung cancer beyond life expectancy: Still not too old for surgery? Lung Cancer. févr 2021;152:86-93. 6. Brooks ED, Sun B, Zhao L, Komaki R, Liao Z, Jeter M, et al. Stereotactic Ablative Radiation Therapy is Highly Safe and Effective for Elderly Patients With Early-stage Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer. Int J Radiat Oncol. juill 2017;98(4):900-7. 7. Palma D, Visser O, Lagerwaard FJ, Belderbos J, Slotman B, Senan S. Treatment of stage I NSCLC in elderly patients: A population-based matched-pair comparison of stereotactic radiotherapy versus surgery. Radiother Oncol. nov 2011;101(2):240-4. 8. Goldstraw P, Chansky K, Crowley J, Rami-Porta R, Asamura H, Eberhardt WEE, et al. The IASLC Lung Cancer Staging Project: Proposals for Revision of the TNM Stage Groupings in the Forthcoming (Eighth) Edition of the TNM Classification for Lung Cancer. J Thorac Oncol. janv 2016;11(1):39-51. 9. Soubeyran P, Bellera C, Goyard J, Heitz D, Curé H, Rousselot H, et al. Screening for Vulnerability in Older Cancer Patients: The ONCODAGE Prospective Multicenter Cohort Study. Williams BO, éditeur. PLoS ONE. 11 déc 2014;9(12):e115060.
