TI  - Trends in Station-Based Lymph Node Sampling for Early-Stage Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer From 2009 to 2019
AU  - Tupper, H.
AU  - Sakoda, L.C.
AU  - Sarovar, V.
AU  - Banks, K.C.
AU  - Sun, A.
AU  - Wile, R.
AU  - Barnes, K.
AU  - Ashiku, S.K.
AU  - Patel, A.R.
AU  - Velotta, J.B.
PY  - 2024
AB  - Rationale: Surgical resection offers an unrivaled opportunity to accurately stage a patient's cancer. However, accurate staging requires adequate lymph node assessment. In 2021, the American College of Surgeons Commission on Cancer (CoC) updated their minimum lymph node sampling recommendation for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) from 10 nodes to “3N2 1N1” (3 mediastinal nodes and 1 hilar node from discrete stations). Preliminary data suggests early-stage NSCLC survival is superior with station-based sampling compared to count-based sampling, but data on adherence are sparse. This study evaluates adherence to 3N2 1N1 sampling recommendations over time in a large, integrated health system. Methods: We evaluated a retrospective cohort of adult patients with clinical stage I-II NSCLC who underwent surgical resection from 2009 to 2019 using institutional electronic health records. We performed structured electronic record review to validate and supplement institutional cancer registry data and to capture surgical lymph node sampling data from pathology reports. Covariates included patient sociodemographic and clinical data. The primary outcome was the proportion of patients with resections meeting the 3N2 1N1 criterion total and by year. We also performed a sub-analysis excluding patients with non-anatomic resections to assess if this impacted adherence. Although CoC recommendations apply to all curative-intent resections, there is often decreased nodal sampling adherence in non-anatomic parenchymal-sparing resections. Results: Among the 2577 patients analyzed (median age at surgery [interquartile range]: 70.2 [64.1-75.8] years; 59% female), most had a smoking history (77.5%) and important comorbidities (Charlson Comorbidity Index >1: 83.9%; BMI >30: 27.8%). The majority were stage I (87.6%) with adenocarcinoma histology (72.7%). Patients predominantly underwent lobectomy (83.2%) or wedge-resection (12.8%). Other resection types were rare. Most (92.4%) patients had at least one lymph node sampled, although sampling improved considerably from 2009 (74.8%) to 2019 (96.0%) (Table 1). Among patients with a nodal specimen, the proportion who met the 3N2 1N1 criterion improved from 26.4% in 2009 to 58.3% in 2019. When excluding 331 patients with non-anatomic resections, the overall proportion with at least one nodal specimen was slightly higher (93.1% vs. 92.4%), as was the proportion meeting the 3N2 1N1 criterion (46.3% vs. 44.1%), with similar improvements over time. Conclusions: The CoC has recommended a 70% adherence to 3N2 1N1 by 2022. Our data highlights that, although there is room for improvement, adherence to these standards were already improving before CoC implementation. Further standardization of nodal staging and reporting will enhance NSCLC treatment and outcomes. (Figure Presented).
