TY  - JOUR
TI  - Comparative Effectiveness of Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy Versus Wedge Resection in Long-term Health Outcomes in Early-stage Lung Cancer
AU  - Mudd, J.
AU  - Mhango, G.
AU  - Zafar, H.
AU  - Wisnivesky, J.P.
PY  - 2024
AB  - Rationale: Many early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients are considered high-risk for complications after lobectomy due to older age, comorbid conditions, or other factors. The therapeutic options for these patients include sub-lobar resection, such as wedge resection, or stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT). While prior studies showed comparable survival outcomes between sub-lobar resection and SBRT up to 5 years post-treatment, patients undergoing SBRT may have higher risk of long-term recurrence. In this study, we assessed the long-term outcomes of patients undergoing SBRT vs. wedge resection. Methods: We identified patients with clinical stage I-IIA NSCLC and tumor size <5 cm who were treated with SBRT or wedge resection between 2006 and 2017 in the Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results-Medicare database. Unadjusted overall survival for patients treated with SBRT vs. wedge resection were calculated using Kaplan-Meier curves. We used multiple propensity score methods to compare overall survival up to 10 years post-treatment between patients that underwent SBRT vs. wedge resection after controlling for potential confounders. Results: We identified 6,439 stage I-IIA NSCLC patients, of whom 3,741 (58%) were treated with SBRT. Patients treated with SBRT were significantly older, had lower income, larger tumor size, and a higher proportion required home supplementary oxygen (p<0.0001 for all comparisons). In unadjusted analysis, patients receiving SBRT had significantly higher overall mortality than those undergoing wedge resection (5-year survival: 32.4% vs 50.4%, respectively; 10-year survival: 7.4% vs 23.0%, respectively; p<0.0001 for both comparisons). After adjusting for propensity scores, patients undergoing SBRT had significantly higher mortality (hazard ratio [HR]: 1.37; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 1.26- 1.49) than those receiving wedge resection in analyses using inverse probability weighting. Similar results were found in analyses adjusting for propensity scores or stratified by propensity score quintiles. Sensitivity analysis showed worse mortality in patients of age < 80 years old (HR: 1.50; 95% CI: 1.42-1.59) and tumor size <20 cm (HR: 1.58; 95% CI: 1.47-1.69) treated with SBRT compared to wedge resection. Conclusions: SBRT is associated with worse long-term survival compared with wedge resection in patients with early-stage NSCLC, particularly in younger patients and those with smaller tumor size. These findings should help to inform patient-provider discussions of treatment options for early-stage lung cancer patients who are not candidates for lobectomy.
ID  - 2379
