AU  - Lyberis, P.
AU  - Ruffini, E.
AU  - Bille, A.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.1000
AB  - Introduction: The prevalence of obesity has dramatically increased worldwide in the past few decades. Moreover, obesity has important implications in terms of post-operative complications, surgical-related risk and technical issues. Our study aims at comparing the Robotic-assisted (RATS), Video-assisted (VATS) thoracic surgery approaches, and the open approach (OA) in terms of morbidity and mortality rate, technical feasibility and short terms oncologic outcomes in obese patients undergoing an anatomical pulmonary resection for early stages Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC). Methods: We retrospectively analyzed all consecutive patients with Body Mass Index (BMI) ≥ 30 kg/m2 undergoing anatomical lung resection for stages I-II NSCLC between 2013 and 2022 in two high volume thoracic surgery centers. We divided them in three groups according to the surgical approach: RATS, VATS, and OA. We analyzed as primary outcomes: post-operative complication rate, length of hospital stay (LOS), length of chest drain (LOD), in hospital, 30- and 90-days mortality, and estimated intraoperative blood loss (eIBL). Secondary outcomes were conversion rate, overall, N1 and N2 lymph node stations harvested, nodal upstaging rate, and operating time. Results: We collected a total of 279 patients, 102 in the RATS group, 120 in the VATS group and 57 in the open group. The results are shown in Table 1. RATS and VATS approaches were associated to a significantly lower post-operative complication rate, median LOS, and median LOD compared with OA. There was no significant difference between the groups regarding hospital, 30- and 90-days mortality. The median eIBL was significantly lower in the RATS group compared to VATS and OA. The median overall, N1 and N2 lymph node stations harvested was significantly higher in the RATS group than in both OA and VATS. The nodal upstaging rate did not significantly differ between RATS and VATS, while the OA had a higher nodal upstaging rate than both VATS and RATS. Finally, RATS was associated with a significantly lower operating time than VATS and OA. [Formula presented] Conclusions: In our series, VATS and RATS were associated with significantly lower post-operative complications, LOS and LOD compared to OA in obese patients after anatomic lung resection for Stage I-II NSCLC. RATS had a significantly fewer eIBL and LOD compared to the other approaches. Mortality rates did not differ between the approaches. RATS was associated with a significant reduction of operating time, and a higher median overall, N1 and N2 lymph node stations harvested. Keywords: Obese patients, anatomical lung resection, Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer
ID  - 2409
