TI  - EP07.01-30 Predictors of Poor Quality of Life Following Lung Cancer Surgery
AU  - Diab, M.
AU  - Choi, J.J.
AU  - Grant, K.
AU  - Yee, J.
AU  - McGuire, A.L.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.976
AB  - Introduction: There is paucity of research on preoperative predictors of quality of life (QOL) in patients undergoing lung resection and its impact on patients different domains of QOL. This study aims to shed light on predictors of poor QOL following lung cancer resection for stage I-IIIa Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC). Methods: A prospective observational single-institutional prospective cohort study was conducted (May 2022- April 2023) at a high volume thoracic surgical centre of excellence. QOL was assessed with the European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer (EORTC)QLQ-C30 and Lung cancer module LC29 tools: at baseline, 2-week, 6-week, 3,6,12 and 18 months post-operatively.Univariate analysis and multivariable linear regression analyses were performed. The QOLscales were treated as continuous variables in our analysis for all domains (Global, cognitive, emotional and physical). We also took into account, and calculated the minimal important difference (MID) Of 0.5 SD for all the time points. Values were were reported as mean, SD, median, IQR for the whole study cohort. A mixed linear regression model was conducted to evaluate the impact of all peri-operative variable on QOL domains. Results: A total of 120 patients were included in the study. The median(IQR) age was 69, 55% were female, 62% Caucasian and 17% Asian. 62% of resections were VATS. 55% lobectomy and 45% sublobar resection. Obstructive sleep apnoea was the only statistically significant variable to impact Global QOL domain (p=0.036). However, education level, tumour size and FVC had a more prompt recovery in that domain (Fig.1a). When examining the physical domain, the advanced tumour TNM staging (Stage I vs I-IIIa groups) were the only statistically significant predictors of poor QOL (p=0.042). Notable, worse OSA, COPD, DLCO and FVC had poorer physical QOL domain recovery at all time points. Again, OSA had a statistical significance on poor cognitive QOL recovery (p=0.042). It also revealed the poor FEV1 and FVC values correlated with worse recoveries, but reaching statistical differences (Fig.1b). Multivariable analysis outcomes will be reported in a subsequent larger cohort. Conclusions: Sleep apnoea was a common denominator in poor QOL recovery at multiple domains. We also note that worse FVC values demonstrated superior correlation better with QOL outcomes versus FEV1. Understanding predictors of poor QOL and further study on this topic is essential to inform postoperative recovery outcomes. [Formula presented] Keywords: Lung Cancer, Quality of Life, Thoracic Surgery
