DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.1450
AB  - Introduction: The proliferation of nodules identified across a spectrum of clinical indications has created a need for improved diagnostic interventions. Ion Navigational Bronchoscopy has emerged as an effective and safe biopsy platform for identifying malignancy and proving benign disease. Navigational bronchoscopy allows stratification of patients to observation, resection subsequent to biopsy, synchronous resection as single episode of anesthesia, or other definitive therapy. We have evaluated the impact of Ion bronchoscopy on surgical stage and the management of NSCLC patients in an academic community medical center thoracic oncology program. Methods: We reviewed 455 patients managed with Ion Navigational Bronchoscopy since July 2020. Patients entered through lung screening, lung nodule program, or cancer surveillance. Patients were evaluated by thoracic surgery and pulmonary medicine. All patients had CT chest for navigation planning. Only NSCLC patients were included in this analysis. All patients had PET scans. Surgical staging with thoracic lymphadenectomy was performed in all patients. The majority of patients had robotic resection with MIDAS (Minimally Invasive Diagnosis and Surgery) which includes robotic navigational bronchoscopy and robotic assisted pulmonary resection under single episode of anesthesia. Ion pathology, surgical stage, type of pulmonary resection, complications and outcomes were assessed. Results: Median nodule size: 13mm; range 5 - 35 mm. Patient age: median 70 years; range 48 - 86 years. We performed robotic assisted resection on 101 patients with NSCLC; 85 patients had MIDAS (single episode of anesthesia). Resections included: lobectomy 65, segmentectomy 10, sublobar 26. Surgical staging in Table 1. Mortality was zero. Conclusions: Improving long-term outcomes for patients with NSCLC begins with earlier diagnosis and initiation of definitive therapy. Our multidisciplinary thoracic oncology team evaluates patients at first identification of a new nodule and leverages the capabilities of robotic technology to establish diagnosis and implement therapy. In our series, 88% of patients were stage I and 95% were stage I or II. Early intervention facilitates pulmonary parenchymal sparing procedures such as segmentectomy and sublobar resections. Incorporating navigational bronchoscopy and minimally invasive surgery into the lung nodule algorithm will shift our patient population to early stages and optimize oncologic outcomes. [Formula presented] Keywords: robotic bronchoscopy, lung nodule, early stage lung cancer
