TY  - JOUR
TI  - OA11.06 Sublobar Resection vs. Lobectomy for Non-Small-Cell Lung Cancers 2cm or Less in Size with High-Risk Features
AU  - Mathey-Andrews, C.A.
AU  - Venkateswaran, S.
AU  - Abruzzo, A.
AU  - Potter, A.
AU  - Haridas, C.
AU  - Kumar, A.
AU  - Raman, V.
AU  - Yang, C.-F.J.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.067
AB  - Introduction: Recently, the randomized prospective trial CALGB 140503 demonstrated the non-inferiority of sublobar resection to lobectomy in the surgical management of early stage non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). It remains unknown, however, whether sublobar resection is oncologically sufficient for treating small tumors with high-risk features (e.g. visceral pleural invasion (VPI), lymphovascular invasion (LVI), and high grade). Methods: Patients in the National Cancer Data Base (NCDB) diagnosed with clinical T1a-b N0M0 NSCLC who underwent sublobar resection (i.e., wedge resection or segmentectomy) or lobectomy with intraoperative lymph node evaluation between 2010-2020 were identified. Only patients with tumors characterized by VPI, LVI, and high grade were included for further analysis. Patients with major comorbidities were excluded in order to decrease selection bias and capture patients undergoing intentional sublobar resection. Overall survival of patients who underwent sublobar resection vs. lobectomy was evaluated using multivariable-adjusted Cox proportional hazard modeling and propensity-score matched analysis, matching for 10 common prognostic covariates including age, tumor size, and grade. Short-term and pathological outcomes were also evaluated. Results: Of the 278 patients with clinical T1a-bN0M0 NSCLC and all three high-risk features identified, 68 (24%) patients underwent sublobar resection and 210 (76%) underwent lobectomy. There were no significant differences in 5-year overall survival between patients undergoing sublobar resection and lobectomy in unadjusted analysis of the overall cohort (67% [95% CI: 52%-78%] vs 70% [95% CI: 61%-77%], p=0.96). Furthermore, in both multivariable-adjusted and propensity-score matched analysis, there were no significant differences in survival between patients undergoing either surgical approach (aHR 1.22, [0.60-2.50, p=0.58; 5-year survival: 69% [95% CI: 50%-82%] for sublobar resection vs. 70% [95% CI: 51%-82%] for lobectomy, p=0.82; Figure I). There were also no significant differences in surgical margin positivity, 30-day readmission, and 30- and 90-day mortality in propensity-score matched analysis. Conclusions: In this national analysis, we found that there were no significant differences in survival or in short-term outcomes between patients undergoing sublobar resection vs. lobectomy for early-stage NSCLC with high-risk pathologic features. These findings suggest that if high-risk features are detected on final pathology after sublobar resection for clinical N0M0 tumors sized 2 cm or less, completion lobectomy is unlikely to confer an additional survival advantage. [Formula presented] Keywords: non-small-cell lung cancer, sublobar resection, lobectomy
