TI  - EP01.01-14 Gender Differences in Clinical Course and Patient-Reported Outcomes among Patient with Early-Stage Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer
AU  - Li, C.
AU  - Zhou, H.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.753
AB  - Introduction: Previous studies have illustrated significant biological and survival-related differences in gender for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). However, gender differences in perioperative clinical course and patient-reported outcomes (PRO) have not been well investigated. This study aimed to analyze the gender differences in the perioperative period among patients who underwent surgery for early-stage NSCLC. Methods: Patients who underwent surgery and had a histological diagnosis of stage I-II NSCLC at Guangdong Provincial People’s Hospital between October 2021 and September 2022 were identified. Among those who had completed the PRO assessment at baseline and at least twice after surgery were enrolled for final analysis. Propensity score matching (PSM) was used to minimize the bias of this retrospective research. Generalized estimating equation models (GEE) were applied to assess PRO between groups over time. All analysis was conducted using the R studio v. 4.2.2 and SPSS v. 26.0. Results: A total of 828 patients (351 males, 477 females) was included in this study. Females were more likely to present at a younger age (p < 0.001), be married (p < 0.05), be non-smoker (p < 0.001), have satisfying American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) classification (p < 0.001), have adenocarcinoma with stage IA1-IA2 (p < 0.001), and be treated with a minimally invasive approach (p < 0.001). After PSM for confounding factors, 118 case-matched pairs were identified. Females were significantly associated with less N2 lymph nodes (station) dissection (both p < 0.05), shorter operative time (p < 0.001), less intraoperative bleeding (p < 0.001), shorter length of postoperative hospital stay (p < 0.05) and less postoperative pulmonary complications (p < 0.05). No statistically significant differences were found although males experienced longer chest tube duration, more lobectomy and more invasive histology (all p > 0.05). Regarding to PRO, females were more likely to report moderate-to-severe pain, disturbed sleep, shortness of breath and fatigue during the 3-day postoperative hospitalization (all p < 0.001). During the 4-week post-discharge, females reported more moderate-to-severe disturbed sleep, shortness of breath, cough and annoyance (all p < 0.001) while pain between gender was comparable (p = 0.052). Conclusions: Among patients with early-stage NSCLC who underwent surgery, females demonstrated an increased likelihood of having minimally invasive treatment and being discharged without postoperative complications. However, females had a higher proportion of moderate-to-severe symptom burden and function status compared to males, indicating perioperative management might be adjusted according to gender. Further research on gender differences in surgical treatment and outcomes is necessary to improve tailored management. Keywords: Patient-Reported Outcomes, Gender difference, Early-Stage Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer
