TI  - EP04.07-01 Message from the Patients: What If I Wasn't COVID 19
AU  - Suleymanov, E.
AU  - Melek, H.
AU  - Gebitekin, C.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.865
AB  - Introduction: The COVID-19 pandemic has changed the routine of life all over the world and together caused the death of millions of people. The aim of this study is to investigate the positive and negative effects of the covid 19 pandemic on the diagnosis and surgical treatment of lung cancer. Methods: The data of 1606 patients who underwent segmentectomy and superior lung resection for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) between January 1996 and March 2022 in our clinic were prospectively recorded and analyzed retrospectively reviewed retrospectively. Patients were divided into two groups, before and after COVID. The surgical and oncological results of the patients were compared. Results: 1387 of the patients were male, 218 were female, and the mean age was 60.73 (19-87). In group 1 (pre-pandemic) 1420 patients, 827 (58.2%) underwent direct surgery, and 593 (41.8%) underwent after neoadjuvant therapy. Segmentectomy was performed in 98 (6.9%) patients, lobectomy in 1140 (80.3%), and pneumonectomy in 182 (12.8%) patients. Pathological stage I patient were 38.04%. While 16 (8.4%) of 185 patients in Group 2 (pandemic period) were asymptomatic, surgery was performed when a nodule was detected in the Thorax CT performed due to COVID/pneumonia. Of the patients in Group 2, 97 (52.4%) underwent direct surgery and 88 (47.6%) underwent after neoadjuvant therapy. Segmentectomy was performed in 19 (10.3%) patients, lobectomy in 159(85.4%), and pneumonectomy in 7 (3.8%) patients. Pathological stage I patient were 41.3%. Postoperative mortality was detected in 40 (2.49%) patients (Group 1 n=36 (2.5%), Group 2=4 (2.1%). Complications were detected in 585 patients postoperatively (Group 1, n= 521(36,6%), Group 2,n= 64 (35,3%), p>0.005. Conclusions: In our clinic, there was no increase in mortality and morbidity after anatomical resections for lung cancer during the pandemic period. However, covid infection caused a significant increase in the rate of early diagnosis. These results support the importance of lung cancer radiological screening. [Formula presented] Keywords: early-stage lung cancer, COVID-19, Screening
