TI  - EP07.02-05 A Three-Dimensional Computed Tomography Analysis of Residual Lung Volume Changes after Segmentectomy for Non-small-Cell Lung Cancer
AU  - Abe, M.
AU  - Osoegawa, A.
AU  - Karashima, T.
AU  - Takamori, S.
AU  - Takumi, Y.
AU  - Sugio, K.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.984
AB  - Introduction: After lobectomy, the residual cavity is filled by mediastinal and diaphragmatic deviation and compensatory volume changes in the residual lungs. Based on the results of JCOG0802, the number of cases of segmentectomy for early-stage lung cancer is increasing. In the present study, we analyzed three-dimensional computed tomography (3D-CT) data to examine the volume changes in the lung before and after segmentectomy. Methods: We included 58 cases of segmentectomy (limited to one lobe) as initial surgery for primary lung cancer at our institution between June 2015 and December 2020. The mean age was 69.3 (range: 40-86) years old, with 28 male patients and 30 smokers. Volumes (ml) were measured by CT using the SYNAPSE VINCENT® preoperatively and one year postoperatively. Predicted residual volume (ml) was calculated by subtracting the volume of the resected segments from the preoperative total lung volume. These values were used to calculate the rate of increase in the postoperative lung volume ([postoperative total lung volume / predicted residual volume] × 100) (%). The compensated group comprised 35 patients with a postoperative total lung volume of ≥100%, and the uncompensated group comprised the remaining 23 patients. Results: The right lung was affected in 31 cases, while the left lung was affected in 27 cases. The resected segments were as follows: right S6 (14 cases), right basal segment (11 cases), other segments of the right (lower lobe: 4 cases, upper lobe: 2 cases), left S1+2 (4 cases), left upper segment (5 cases), left S6 (13 cases), left basal segment (3 cases), and other segments of the left (upper lobe: 1 case, lower lobe: 1 case). The intersegmental plane was divided using only a stapler (39 cases) or mainly with electrocautery (19 cases). The average rate of change in the postoperative lung volume was 103.3% (range: 72.4%-143.2%) in the 58 total cases. The compensated group was significantly more likely to have undergone thoracoscopic surgery (p=0.031), division of the intersegmental plane by electrocautery (p<0.001), and basal segmentectomy (p=0.038) than the uncompensated group. The postoperative lung volume increase rate in the compensated group was compared between preoperative and postoperative volumes of (1) lobes including the resected segment, (2) total volume of ipsilateral lobes excluding the lobe with resected segments, and (3) total volume of the contralateral lung. No significant difference was found in lobes including the resected segment (1). A comparison of the total volume of ipsilateral lobes excluding the lobe with resected segments (2) showed a significantly increased postoperative volume for right S6 segmentectomy (103.2%-115.5%: p=0.008) and right basal segmentectomy (109.8%-160.5%: p=0.004). On comparing contralateral lungs (3), the postoperative volume was significantly increased in right S6 segmentectomy (98.8%-114.4%: p=0.023). Conclusions: Although the analysis focused on segmentectomy in the lower lobes, there was no significant decrease in volume in any of the residual segments compared to the preoperative volume. Compensation for increased postoperative volume in lobes other than the residual segment was also suggested. Keywords: segmentectomy, three-dimensional computed tomography, residual lung volume
