AB  - Introduction: About 20% of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) are diagnosed at an early stage, allowing an effective pulmonary resection, but many patients still experience disease recurrence. Thus, biomarkers able to stratify patients with a different prognosis are needed. This study aims to investigate 1) the prognostic potential of free circulating (FC) and extracellular vesicles (EVs)-derived microRNA (miRNAs), and 2) the added of prognostic capacity of the combination of biomarkers and known prognostic factors (PFs). Methods: The RESTING study is a multicentre prospective observational cohort study on resected stage IA-IIIA NSCLC patients. The main end-point was disease-free survival. A peripheral blood sample for plasma collection was taken pre-surgery from each patient. MiRNA-specific libraries were prepared with the QIAseq miRNA Library Kit and sequenced on Illumina NextSeq 550. Data were normalized using the TMM method. To reach the study aims, three models were specified: one using the miRNA data only; one using both miRNAs and known PFs (age, gender, and pathological stage), and one using the PFs only. Five-fold cross-validation (CV) was applied to assess the predictive performance of the models. Separate analyses for FC and EV-derived miRNA were performed. Results: A total of 222 patients were enrolled. Median age was 70 (range 44-85) years. Ninety-nine (46.5%) patients had stage IA, 38 (17.8%) had stage IB, 9 (4.2%) had stage IIA, 27 (12.7%) had stage IIB and 40 (18.8%) had stage IIIA tumors. 179 (81%) had adenocarcinoma histology, 42 (19%) squamous cell carcinoma. The median follow-up time was 26.3 (95% CI 25.4-27.6) months. A total of 52 events (relapse or death) were observed. From the multivariable Cox models on DFS, three FC-miRNAs and 21 EV-miRNAs resulted associated with DFS. After including the PFs into the model, two FC-miRNAs (miR-29c-3p and miR-877-3p) and five EV-miRNAs (miR-181a-2-3p, miR-182-5p, miR-192-5p, miR-532-3p and miR-589-5p) remained associated with prognosis. The model combining EV-miRNAs and PFs showed similar discriminatory and stratification capacity as compared to the PFs only model whereas in the case of FC-miRNA, the combined model performed significantly worse than the PFs only model. Conclusions: Results of the RESTING study highlighted that two FC-miRNAs and five EV-miRNAs have a potential role in the identification of early-stage NSCLC patients with a higher risk of relapse or death after surgery. This could represent a non invasive, quick and low-cost approach to better identify patients with a more aggressive disease, permitting a more accurate patient stratification and helping clinician in the therapeutic decision making. The study was supported by ERA-NET TRANSCAN-2 (JTC 2016)-RESTING project Keywords: miRNA, prognosis, early-stage NSCLC
