TI  - MA18.06 Reassessing Sublobar vs. Lobar Resection for Early-stage Small Cell Lung Cancer in the Wake of CALGB 140503
AU  - Haridas, C.S.
AU  - Papneja, S.
AU  - Potter, A.
AU  - Andrews, C.M.
AU  - Kumar, A.
AU  - Martin, L.
AU  - Yang, C.-F.J.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.248
AB  - Introduction: The standard of care for stage I small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) is lobectomy followed by chemotherapy. Recently, the results of CALGB 140503 demonstrated that sublobar resection was associated with similar overall survival compared to lobectomy for stage IA (<2 cm) non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). It is unclear whether similar findings would be observed among patients diagnosed with stage IA (<2 cm) SCLC. The objective of this study was to compare the overall survival of sublobar resection vs. lobectomy for stage IA (<2 cm) SCLC. Methods: Patients who underwent lobectomy or sublobar resection (including segmentectomy and wedge resection) for clinical stage IA (<2 cm) SCLC from 2010-2015, with follow-up up through December 2020, in the United States National Cancer Database were identified for analysis. The year 2015 was selected as the study cutoff to ensure that patients would have adequate follow-up time. Rates of adjuvant chemotherapy use were assessed. Among patients who underwent adjuvant chemotherapy, we compared overall survival between patients who underwent lobectomy vs. sublobar resection using 2:1 propensity score-matching; a similar analysis was performed comparing overall survival between patients who underwent lobectomy vs. wedge resection followed by adjuvant chemotherapy. Results: A total of 439 patients met study inclusion criteria, of which 309 (70.4%) underwent lobectomy and 130 (29.6%) underwent sublobar resection (104 wedge resections; 26 segmentectomies). Rates of adjuvant chemotherapy use were 65.4% (n=202) and 72.3% (n=94) among patients undergoing lobectomy and sublobar resection, respectively. In a propensity score-matched analysis of 146 patients who underwent lobectomy followed by adjuvant chemotherapy and 73 patients who underwent sublobar resection followed by adjuvant chemotherapy, there were no significant differences in 5-year overall survival between the groups (48.0% [95% CI: 39.5%-56.0%] vs. 52.8% [95% CI: 40.6%-63.5%], P=0.41). Additionally, in a propensity score-matched analysis of patients who underwent wedge resection vs. lobectomy, followed by adjuvant chemotherapy, there were no significant differences in 5-year overall survival between the two groups (50.8% [95% CI: 40.9%-59.8%] vs. 48.4% [95% CI: 34.7%-60.9%], P=0.96). Conclusions: In this national analysis of U.S. patients diagnosed with clinical stage IA (<2 cm) SCLC, sublobar resection followed by adjuvant chemotherapy was associated with similar overall survival compared to lobectomy followed by adjuvant chemotherapy. These findings suggest that the recent results of CALGB 140503 among stage IA (<2 cm) NSCLC patients may be generalizable to patients diagnosed with stage IA (<2 cm) SCLC who undergo surgery followed by adjuvant chemotherapy. [Formula presented] Keywords: Wedge resection, Sublobar resection, Segmentectomy, Small Cell Lung Cancer, Early stage
