AB  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Cancer Posters 1 SESSION TYPE: Original Investigation Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/10/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm PURPOSE: Technological advancement in navigational/robotic bronchoscopy increased the detection of less than 15 mm peripheral lung nodules of non–small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). This early detection of lung cancer at an early stage has raised the need for sub-lobar resection instead of lobectomy for better patients outcome and fewer complications. METHODS: A retrospective review of a single center for patients with primary or metastatic peripheral lung nodules. Those patients underwent a discussion in a multidisciplinary clinic by thoracic surgery and interventional pulmonology. The evaluation includes selection criteria for the patient and presurgery evaluation with proper mediastinal staging and pulmonary function test. Those patients underwent the same anesthesia event with robotic bronchoscopy marking and sub-lobar lung nodule resection. The data was collected between January 2023 and March 2023. Twelve patients were selected. The outcome was monitored for the length of the same-day event anesthesia, the lung nodule marking time, surgery time, LOS, and any reported complications. RESULTS: Out of the twelve patients, Ten nodules were resected after marking from the upper lobe of the lung, and two nodules were removed from the lower lobes. The average time for lung nodule marking was 27.75 Min; the average resection time was 43.56 min; the LOS post-surgery was 1.58 days, No significant complication was reported. CONCLUSIONS: Combining sub-lobar resection with robotic lung nodule marking can result in more precise removal of the cancerous tissue, minimizing the amount of healthy tissue removed. This can improve the patient's overall lung function and reduce the risk of complications associated with more extensive lung surgery. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Combining robotic lung nodule marking and sub-lobar resection can be an effective treatment option for patients with early-stage lung cancer. However, the suitability of this procedure will depend on various factors, such as the size and location of the nodule, Location of the nodule, and the multidisciplinary presurgery discussion DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Abdul Hamid Alraiyes No relevant relationships by Alex Cedeno-Rodriguez No disclosure on file for Gary Chmielewski No relevant relationships by Axel Joob
