AU  - Miller, P.
AU  - Burk, J.R.
AU  - Vigness, R.
AU  - Hollingsworth, J.W.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm-conference.2023.A30
AB  - Background: Early identification, diagnosis, and treatment of lung cancer is associated with improved clinical outcomes. Robotic-assisted bronchoscopy has improved the ability to safely diagnose early staged lung malignancies. Combining robotic-assisted bronchoscopy and roboticassisted lobectomy under single-anesthesia could reduce the time from identification of a pulmonary nodule to therapeutic intervention in early staged lung cancer in a select patient population. Methods: We performed a retrospective case-control single-center study to compare patients with radiographic Stage I non-small cell lung cancer undergoing robotic navigational bronchoscopy and surgical resection with historical controls. Primary outcome was time from initial radiographic identification of a pulmonary nodule to therapeutic intervention. Secondary outcomes included times between identification of nodule to biopsy, time between biopsy and surgery, and complications related to procedures. Historical controls and cases were compared by two-tailed unpaired student t-test for continuous variables and Chi-square analysis for categorical data. P<0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results: Historical controls (N=63) and identified cases (N=22) demonstrate similar demographics. Patients with suspected Stage I NSCCA whom received single anesthesia for diagnosis and intervention with robotic-assisted bronchoscopy and robotic-assisted lobectomy had shorter times between identification of a pulmonary nodule and intervention, when compared to historical controls (65 vs 116 days, P=0.005). Single-anesthesia cases had very low rate of complications (0% vs 5%) and shorter hospitalizations after surgery (3.6 vs 6.2 days, P=0.017). Conclusion: Our findings support that the use of a multidisciplinary thoracic oncology team and single-anesthesia biopsy-to-surgery approach in management of stage I lung cancer significantly reduced the time from identification to intervention, biopsy to intervention, and length of hospital stays in the management of care for lung cancer patients. The potential implication is that adoption of combined robotic-assisted bronchoscopy with robotic-assisted surgical lobectomy may have benefit to patient clinical outcomes in a select patient population.
ID  - 2445
