TI  - Surgery versus stereotactic ablative radiation therapy for stage I lung cancer: a real-world series
AU  - Josephides, E.
AU  - Farinelli, E.
AU  - Fabbri, G.
AU  - Volpi, S.
AU  - Routledge, T.
AU  - Karapanagiotou, E.
AU  - Ahmad, S.
AU  - Smith, D.
AU  - Bille, A.
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1016/S0169-5002(23)00613-X
AB  - Introduction: For patients with Stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), standard of care is lobectomy for standard-risk medically operable patients or sub-lobar resection for high-risk patients. Stereotactic ablative radiotherapy (SABR) is standard for inoperable patients but its role in operable patients is unclear. Prospective randomised trials comparing surgery and SABR in early-stage NSCLC have been unable to accrue. Randomising to these treatments is challenging, so we compared outcomes of patients undergoing either treatment using real-world data. Methods: We reviewed data from patients with clinical stage I NSCLC treated with surgery or SABR between 2015-2021 within a single UK centre. Overall survival (OS) and progression free survival (PFS) were compared with Kaplan-Meier method and Cox proportional hazards model. Results: 894 patients with stage I NSCLC treated with surgery (n=624) or SABR (n=270) between 2015-2021 were included. Median age was 72 (range 32-94) in the surgery group and 74 (45-93) in the SABR group. 155 (24.8%) patients died in the surgery group (8 within 30 days of operation). 152 (56.3%) died in the SABR group (1 within 30 days). Recurrence occurred in 68 (10.9%) patients in the surgery group and 39 (14.4%) in the SABR group. Recurrences were locoregional in 33 (48.5%) and 25 (64.1%) of the surgery and SABR groups, respectively. Surgery was associated with superior OS [hazard ratios (HR) 0.40 (95% confidence interval (CI) 0.32-0.50)] compared with SABR but there was no significant difference in PFS between the groups [HR 0.70 (95% CI 0.49-1.05)]. Conclusion: This Real-World series revealed superior OS for patients undergoing surgery compared to SABR in stage I NSCLC. There was no difference in PFS between the groups. OS outcomes may be influenced by comorbid factors which require further evaluation. Further prospective studies are awaited to clarify the efficacy of both treatments in this population. Disclosure: No significant relationships. [Figure presented]
