TY  - JOUR
TI  - Photodynamic Therapy as Curative Intent for Carcinoid Tumor: A Case Report
AU  - Santillana, E.C.
AU  - Celis, E.A.
PY  - 2022
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm-conference.2022.205.1_MeetingAbstracts.A3349
AB  - Introduction: Photodynamic therapy (PDT) is an established FDA-approved modality for curative intent in early-stage and palliative intent in advanced stage non-small cell lung cancers (NSCLC). However, the use of this modality has also been described in various other off-label indications such as the treatment of bronchial carcinoid tumors. Currently, there is no established maximum amount of light activations that can be performed with PDT. This case report was done to describe the successful treatment of a carcinoid tumor with PDT after undergoing 6 sessions of light activation in a patient who would have otherwise had to have undergone a pneumonectomy. Case Details: 27 year-old woman never-smoker with history of childhood asthma who had symptoms of cough and hemoptysis for about 6 months prior to presenting to the hospital at which time she was found to have a 2.7 x 1.8 cm right hilar/suprahilar mass on imaging which appeared to extend into the right mainstem bronchus. PET-CT at that time only showed FDG-avidity at the right hilar mass. Biopsies were consistent with typical carcinoid tumor. Given concern for involvement of the distal trachea, CT surgery deemed the lesion was inoperable and she was referred to pulmonary for PDT. Patient subsequently underwent PDT with 6 activations sessions with no recurrence of disease on her 6-week follow up bronchoscopy. Follow up forceps biopsies x 4 each were obtained from the right mainstem, right upper lobe and anterior segment of the right upper lobe which were significant for fibrosis/scarring with no evidence of tumor. Repeat PET-CT also showed no FDG-avid lesions. Discussion:PDT has been described as an off-label treatment modality for typical carcinoid with a low complication rate with skin photosensitivity reactions being the most common. From literature review, typically 2-3 light activations are performed. One would expect that increasing activations leads to increased complications, however no maximum number of activations has been set or studied. In our case, our patient did not have any complications despite being in a sunny state after receiving 6 light activations. PDT may be an adequate first line modality with low risk as curative-intent therapy for typical bronchial carcinoid tumors in select patients who are unable to or do not desire to undergo invasive surgical resection. In our patient, therapy showed resolution of her carcinoid at 6 weeks after 6 light activation sessions and it will be interesting to see if this results in a long-term response. (Figure Presented).
ID  - 2515
