TY  - JOUR
TI  - Lobectomy with a Twist: A Case Report of Post-Lobectomy Airway Stenosis Managed Bronchoscopically
AU  - Louie, B.
AU  - Mcbride, C.L.
AU  - Lazarus, D.R.
AU  - Cornwell, L.
AU  - Bandi, V.D.
PY  - 2022
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm-conference.2022.205.1_MeetingAbstracts.A3352
AB  - Introduction:For patients with early stage, non-small cell lung cancer who are surgical candidates, surgical resection remains the preferred treatment modality. Multiple surgical options exist; sleeve lobectomy is frequently performed for lesions near carinae. Lobectomy has well described complications, some of which require additional surgeries. We report a case of bronchial and vascular torsion after sleeve lobectomy managed bronchoscopically. Case Description:A 61-year-old man with extensive tobacco history was noted to have a 10 mm x 5 mm right upper lobe lesion on computed tomography. We performed bronchoscopy for workup. Endobronchial biopsies showed carcinoid tumor; pathologic stage was IIB carcinoid tumor (T2N1M0). The patient underwent right upper lobe sleeve lobectomy without immediate complications. Two months post-op, he presented to the emergency department with one day of acute shortness of breath. Chest radiography revealed right lung volume loss with rightward mediastinal shift and atelectasis. A CT chest elucidated a focal occlusion of the proximal bronchus intermedius and right middle lobe bronchus, a kinked right main pulmonary artery, with concern for bronchial torsion. Bronchoscopy was performed, which found 100% obstruction of the right mainstem bronchus anastomotic site by scar and granulation. A jagwire was successfully advanced then serial balloon dilations were done to allow a 10 mm x 15 mm self-expandable metal stent to be placed at the right mainstem bronchus. Post-procedure, patient reported significant improvement in respiratory symptoms and function, with repeat CXR showing improved positioning of the trachea and aeration of the right lung. We repeated bronchoscopy 1 month later to replace the stent due to mucoid secretions within it. Ten weeks after the initial bronchoscopy, the metal stent was removed and the patient had satisfactory airway remodeling which allowed for independent patency of the bronchi without residual torsion. The patient is asymptomatic on most recent follow-up.Discussion:Our case describes bronchial torsion after sleeve lobectomy. Bronchial torsion is a rare but can be life-threatening when twisting of the anatomy results in airway or vascular occlusion. Incidence may be as low as 0.089-0.4% after lobectomy. Treatment usually involves surgical detorsion and fixation of the lung, or completion pneumonectomy if detorsion fails. Our patient was able to avoid correctional surgery through bronchoscopic dilation and stenting which resolved the stenosis of both the airway and right pulmonary artery branch. To our knowledge, there are no other reports in the literature of correction of combined bronchial and vascular narrowing after sleeve resection which have been managed bronchoscopically. (Figure Presented).
