TI  - Lung resection with underlying UIP: Russian roulette
AU  - Patel, A.
AU  - Alagbe, B.
AU  - Bishay, E.
PY  - 2022
DO  - 10.1016/S0169-5002(22)00194-5
AB  - Introduction: Lung cancer patients who require a curative resection, the incidence of UIP as an associated disease is 3.7% [1]. Acute exacerbation of UIP in lung cancer patients is a critical factor in determining post-operative mortality. Methods: We retrospectively reviewed two patients with a similar degree of background IPF in the presence of early-stage nonsmall cell lung cancer who were put forward for curative surgical resection. We gathered demographic, pre-operative fitness, imaging, pathological and post-operative follow-up data on both patients to compare outcomes between lobar and sub-lobar resection in this complex setting. Results: We operated on two patients both in their 70s with PET avid upper lobe lesions, both long-term ex-smokers, TLco of 55-60% and VO2 max of >15ml/kg/min. Patient A underwent a VATS lobectomy with an R0 resection but suffered a flare-up of UIP post-operatively, admitted to ICU and died on day 44 post-operatively. Patient B underwent a VATS wedge resection and was discharged home well at rest on day 3. Surveillance PET/CT at 12 months post-operatively, has shown features of local recurrence at the resection margin. Conclusions: Post-operative flare-up of UIP is a feared complication by both surgeons and clinical oncologists. There are no predictive factors for outcome in this high-risk group of patients. Sub-lobar resection may be an appropriate alternative for smaller tumours however a delicate balance must be struck in order to ensure these patients have a long disease-free interval without local recurrence at the cost of significant post-operative mortality from IPF flareup. Measures to reduce the acute inflammatory response after lung resection need further exploration.
