TI  - Salvage surgery in patients with locally advanced nonsmall cell lung cancer - outcomes and longtime results
AU  - Furrer, K.
AU  - Werner, R.S.
AU  - Hillinger, S.
AU  - Schneiter, D.
AU  - Inci, I.
AU  - Opitz, I.
PY  - 2021
DO  - 10.1183/13993003.congress-2021.PA2295
AB  - Objective: In patients with stage IIIB or IV non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), current guidelines recommend a combination of systemic treatment including chemotherapy, targeted- or immunotherapy with definitive radiotherapy. Nevertheless, relapses still occur in >30% within 2 years. In selected patients with local tumor recurrence or residual disease, so-called 'salvage resection' can be performed with curative intent. Since previous reports on this approach are scarce and candidate selection remains challenging, we aimed to assess the outcomes of salvage surgery. Methods: We retrospectively identified patients with initial stage IIIB or IV NSCLC who underwent salvage lung resection in curative intent between 2001-2020 and included only if this resection had not been part of the first line treatment approach. Results: Among 30 patients (53% male, aged 60.5 years), 7 had stage IIIB and 23 had stage IV. 21 lobectomies/bilobectomies (with 8 extended), 1 segmentectomy and 8 extended pneumonectomies were performed. R0 resection was achieved in 93% with pathological complete response in 7 cases. Morbidity rate was 36.7%(11/30) with 5/30 minor (Grade II) and 7/30 major (Grade IIIA-V)complications. 30- and 90-day mortality was 0% and 10%. Median overall survival (OS) and progression-free survival (PFS) were 5 years [95%CI:2.83;7.16] and 12 months [95%CI:0;27] respectively. R0 resection of the primary tumor was an independent factor influencing OS and PFS (69 months[95%CI 24.9;113, p=0.024] and 22 months[95% CI 1.45;42.5, p=0.002]) respectively(fig). Conclusion: Salvage surgery is a potentially curative procedure with promising OS and PFS in selected patients with stage IIIB/IV NSCLC.
