TI  - Outcomes of Robotic-Assisted Lobectomy for Stage i Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer in a Veteran Population
AU  - Dang, B.
AU  - Reddy, R.
AU  - Abolhoda, A.
PY  - 2021
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm-conference.2021.TP137
AB  - Rationale:Lobar resection for stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) has trended toward less invasive means. Thoracotomy lobectomy has been traditionally performed, but less invasive techniques include video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery and robotic-assisted lobectomy. The aim of this study is to elucidate the efficacy of robotic assisted lobectomy in stage I NSCLC. Methods:134 patients who underwent robotic-assisted lobectomy for stage IA and IB NSCLC at the Long Beach Veterans Affairs Hospital from February 2012 and April 2018 were identified. Patient data was reviewed up until April 2020, most notably overall survival and recurrence rate based on biopsy or clinical consensus. Results:Patient follow up was variable up to 8 years with median follow up of 3.5 years. Mean age was 68.3 years old, 94% were male, and 6% were female. 55% of patients presented with adenocarcinoma and 32% presented with squamous cell carcinoma. 69% were stage IA and 31% were stage IB. Lung cancer recurrence occurred in 19 patients (14.2%). Progression-free survival was a median of 1.5 years. Overall survival was found to be 93% at 1 year, 76% at 3 years, and 51.8% at 5 years. Conclusions:Overall survival rates in this study group were comparable to those seen in sublobar resections in a similar veteran population (5-year survival 56%), though the overall survival rates were lower than in lobar resections in a similar veteran population (70%). The recurrence rate seen in this population is comparable to other studies in the literature (19% for standard lobectomy at 25 month follow up, 13% in VATS at 23 month follow up), though with longer follow up.
