TI  - Survival outcomes following curative-intent thoracic resection for early stage lung cancer: A 10-year audit
AU  - Lam, S.
AU  - Youn, P.
AU  - Joshi, P.
AU  - Sanders, L.
AU  - Newman, M.
AU  - Phillips, M.
AU  - Thomas, R.
PY  - 2021
DO  - 10.1111/resp.14021
AB  - Introduction: Surgical resection with curative intent is the recommended treatment for early stage non-small lung cancer (NSCLC). There is limited data on survival outcomes following surgery for lung cancer in the Australian population. Aim: To describe the survival rates following curative thoracic surgical resection for early stage NSCLC in a single quaternary centre. Methods: Suitable patients were identified from a prospectively maintained database at Sir Charles Gairdner hospital (SCGH). Consecutive adult patients with a definitive diagnosis of early stage (I-IIIA) NSCLC who underwent curative surgery between January 2009 and September 2019 were included. Following surgery, patients were followed-up for surveillance for five years. Demographics, details of the primary lung cancer and outcomes of surgery were audited. Survival rates were calculated using XLSTAT for Kaplan-Meier analysis. Results: 425 patients (female, 45%; age, 18-89 years) underwent curative-intent surgical resection for early stage NSCLC during the study period. Adenocarcinoma (273 cases, 64%) was the most common cancer type by histology followed by squamous cell carcinoma (113, 27%) and other NSCLC sub-types (39, 9%). Majority of patients had stage 1 cancer (217, 62%); 88 (25%) patients had stage II and 47 (13%) had stage III disease. Most patients (401, 94%) underwent lobectomy with/without additional sub-lobar resection; 24 (6%) patients had pneumonectomy. Resection by video-assisted thoracoscopy was performed in 315 (74%) patients whilst 110 (26%) patients underwent thoracotomy. The median length of hospital stay following surgery was four days. The mean overall survival was 7.9 years (SD 0.25 years) after surgery. Early post-operative mortality was 1.2% at 30 days and 2.4% at 90 days. 94.3% of patients were alive at the end of one year after surgery, 85.8% at two years and 67.1% at five years. Conclusions: In this large single-centre cohort of early stage NSCLC, both early and long-term survival outcomes following curative-intent surgery appear comparable to historical cohorts.
ID  - 2581
