TI  - Long term results of vats lobectomy versus opened lobectomy for early stage non small cell lung cancer
AU  - Do, Q.K.
AU  - Dao, Q.H.
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.1111/resp.13699
AB  - Backgrounds: Lobectomy and lymph node dissection is optimal surgical therapy for early stage NSCLC. Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) approach is less trauma, quickly recovery, whereas those who advocate thoracotomy claim it as an oncologically superior procedure. The aims of study are evaluation the role of VATS lobectomy for early stage NSCLC and compare with opened lobectomy. Methods: All of patient with NSCLC in stage IA-IIB underwent lobectomy and lymph node dissection through VATS or thoracotomy are collected. Patient characteristics were compared. Operative datas, complications were assessed and survival was assessed by Kaplan-Meier and Cox proportional hazards analysis. Follow-up from 14 to 66 months. Results: From May 2008 to August 2016, 57 patients with NSCLC stage IA-IIB underwent VATS lobectomy and 60 patients underwent thoracotomy lobectomy. Mean age is 56.3 range 31-84. The mean operating time in VATS group is 157 minutes longer than thoracotomy was 124 minutes. No statistic difference between 2 group in characteristics of patients. In 57 cases VATS, 5 cases were converted to thoracotomy. There are one patient death in thoracotomy group. Prolonged air-leak were 7 cases (10.7%) in thoracotomy and 2 cases (3.8%) in VATS group. Hospitalization is 18.4 days in thoracotomy group and 12.8 days in VATS group. The 5-year OS rate was 67.8% in the VATS group and 71.6% in the thoracotomy group (P = 0.156). The 5-year DFS rate was 59.6% in the VATS group and 65.9% in the thoracotomy group (P = 0.065). Survival by Cox model was no different for VATS versus thoracotomy (hazard ratio 0.82; P = 0.21) Conclusions: VATS lobectomy is effective and safe for of early stage NSCLC associated with less complications and shorter length of hospital stay. The 5 years survival is similar with thoracotomy approach.
