TY  - JOUR
TI  - The management and outcomes of patients with both interstitial lung disease and lung cancer
AU  - Flack, K.F.
AU  - Chu, S.
AU  - Ciunci, C.
AU  - Berman, A.
AU  - Kreider, M.
AU  - Porteous, M.
PY  - 2019
AB  - Rationale Lung cancer is common and studies suggest that patients with interstitial lung disease (ILD) are at increased risk for developing lung cancer. However, few studies investigate the management and outcomes of patients with both ILD and lung cancer. METHODS This study is a retrospective case series of patients with both lung cancer and ILD evaluated between January 1,2000 and March 1,2013 at two academic lung centers to allow for assessment of 5-year survival. Patients were identified through a database search for ICD-9 codes for both of these diagnoses. Pulmonologists were also asked to identify any additional patients not initially identified. Charts were reviewed by two physicians to confirm eligibility and to extract data related to cancer and ILD management and 5-year survival. An ILD specialist reviewed the clinical data to determine each patient's most likely ILD diagnosis. RESULTS There were 29 patients identified with both lung cancer and ILD. Of these, 18/29 (62.1%) were male, 27/29 (93.1%) were non-Hispanic white, 15/29 (51.7%) had COPD, and 28/29 (96.6%) had a history of smoking, with mean pack-years of 34.7. The ILD and lung cancer characteristics are enumerated in Table 1. Surgery was documented in 11/29 patients (37.9%; 1 pneumonectomy, 6 lobectomies, 1 segmentectomy, 1 wedge resection, and 2 combined surgeries), with only 8/14 patients (57.1%) with Stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) having surgery, the standard of care at this stage. Lung radiation was documented in 9/29 patients (31.0%) and 5/9 (55.6%) developed findings concerning for radiation pneumonitis. Chemotherapy was documented in 13/29 patients (44.8 %). Finally,11/29 patients (37.9%) were placed on hospice and 5-year survival from the time of pathologic cancer diagnosis was 6.9% (2/29 patients) compared to 20% 5-year survival of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis patients at our center and 16.4% and 19% 5-year survival of patients diagnosed with lung cancer from 2001-2003 and 2004-2009, respectively.1 CONCLUSION This study suggests that the treatment and outcomes of lung cancer in the ILD population may differ from the general lung cancer population. While patients in this ILD population were diagnosed with lung cancer earlier than the general lung cancer population, the 5-year survival was decreased.1 Further studies are required to investigate the risk factors and outcomes of patients with both ILD and cancer.
ID  - 2725
