TI  - A clinocopathological study of lung cancer in patients with non-idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) diffuse parenchymal lung diseases
AU  - Fedyna, S.
AU  - Bulman, W.
AU  - Shu, C.
AU  - Saqi, A.
AU  - Sonett, J.
AU  - Stanifer, P.
PY  - 2019
AB  - Rationale: Interstitial lung disease (ILD) is characterized by damage to the lung parenchyma due to inflammation and/or fibrosis1. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) and other diseases that affect the lung interstitium have been associated with lung carcinogenesis2. Approximately 10% of patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) will also have an ILD diagnosis3-4. Lung cancers in patients with IPF predominate in the subpleural lower lobe regions and consist of squamous cell subtypes5-6. The clinocopathological characteristics of lung cancer in non-IPF ILD and other granulomatous diffuse parenchymal lung disease are poorly understood. Methods: Patients with a biopsy-proven diagnosis of an ILD other than IPF who underwent surgical resection of a primary lung neoplasm from January 2000 to May 2018 were included in this study. Patients were classified into three groups: chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP), sarcoidosis, and non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP). Clinical and radiographic information was obtained from their medical records. Results: Twenty total cases of concomitant non-IPF ILD and malignancy were identified with the majority being in patients with confirmed HP. Adenocarcinoma (ADCA) was the most common histology (Table 1) and most were identified as early stage NSCLCs. The majority of tumors in patients with HP originated from a peripheral, subpleural area that on gross inspection correlated with areas of fibrosis on CT scans obtained prior to their malignancy (Table 2). There was no clear anatomic radiologic correlation between the tumors found in the sarcoid and in the NSIP groups. Among the 12 patients with ADCA, a KRAS mutation was observed in 3 (25%) and BRAF in 1 (8%); none were EGFR or ALK positive. Among the patients who underwent curative lung resections for early stage lung cancer, there was not a major decrement in their post-operative lung function as assessed by forced vital capacity (Table 3). Conclusions: In this cohort of patients with early stage lung malignancies and biopsy-proven, non-IPF ILD, ADCA was the most common subtype of NSCLC. Tumors in patients with HP originated in areas with corresponding subpleural fibrosis and were basilar predominant. No such trend was noted in the patients with NSIP and sarcoid. The relatively small decrement in FVC observed following resection suggests that surgery remains a viable option for patients with early stage NSCLC and non-IPF ILD. No definitive conclusion can be made about the mutation status given the small sample size.
ID  - 2726
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - The segment left behind
AU  - Cocciardi, S.F.
AU  - Borah, A.
AU  - Abouzgheib, W.
PY  - 2019
AB  - Introduction: Surgical complications following pulmonary resection with video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) for early stage lung cancer may present initially to the interventional pulmonologist. We present a rare post-VATS bronchial complication diagnosed with bronchoscopy. Case Description: A 65 year-old woman with a history of prior left upper lobectomy for early stage non-small cell lung cancer presented to our hospital with fever, chills and a loculated pleural effusion. One week earlier she underwent elective VATS for a highly suspicious right lower lobe lung nodule. The nodule was wedge-resected and onsite pathology was positive for adenocarcinoma, with subsequent mediastinal lymphadenectomy revealing all negative results. The thoracic surgical team then proceeded with a basilar wedge resection, reportedly sparing the right lower lobe superior segment and its vascular supply, with hopes of preserving all possible lung function given her history of prior left upper lobectomy. She now returns one week later with worsening dyspnea and cough. After careful review of her current computed tomography (CT) chest imaging, we noted consolidative parenchyma of the superior segment distal to a hyperlucency in this location. Diagnostic bronchoscopy revealed a staple line across the right lower lobe superior segment (picture 1). Our findings were discussed with the surgical team and the patient proceeded to the operating room where the right lower lobe superior segment was found to be pus-filled and necrotic. A completion right lower lobectomy with debridement and irrigation was performed and the patient did well post-operatively. Discussion: Catastrophic VATS complications have been described as those that require an additional unplanned surgical procedure. Such complications are uncommon with the literature reporting a less than 2% rate. Vascular and bronchial inadvertent ligation is extremely rare but has been reported and is usually noticed immediately. In our case, the superior segment of the right lower lobe and its vascular supply was unintentionally stapled, leading to the post-obstructive infectious presentation one week later. The diagnosis is made with a careful review of all airways of interest on imaging, as well as a thorough review of the operative report and discussion with the surgical team. This case highlights the importance of a strong working relationship between the interventional pulmonologist and thoracic surgeon that ultimately lead to a successful outcome of a potential fatal complication.
ID  - 2727
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Synapse 3D With Intravascular Indocyanine Green Fluorescence Mapping for Targeted Pulmonary Segmental Resection Trial: A Phase I Safety and Feasibility Trial
PY  - 2019
AB  - Brief Summary With the advent of CT screening for lung cancer, an increasing number of NSCLCs are being detected at very early stages, and the demand for pulmonary segmentectomy is rising rapidly. As such, there is a need to develop new surgical techniques to facilitate minimally invasive pulmonary segmentectomy, as segmentectomy may provide a number of significant advantages over lobectomy for patients presenting with early-stage lung cancer, or for patients unable to undergo a full lobectomy due to existing comorbidities. This study will provide the first case series using preoperative 3D anatomical planning (Synapse 3D) added to ICG and NIF-guided robotic segmentectomy to date and will be the first reported use of Synapse 3D-guided targeted pulmonary segmental resection in Canada. As lung cancer is the most frequently fatal cancer in North America, many thousands of patients will be able to benefit from this operation every year. Detailed Description Lungs are made up of individual lobes. When a lung cancer tumour is found in one of these lobes, the surgeon often performs a Lobectomy. A Lobectomy is the surgery most commonly done to treat early-stage lung cancer and requires removal of an entire lobe of the lung, which removes a large amount of lung tissue For patients with small tumours saving as much healthy lung tissue as possible is important. Each lobe of the lung has smaller sections called segments. When a lung cancer is in one of these segments, it is possible to remove that segment, without removing the entire lobe. This surgery is called a Segmentectomy. Compared to a lobectomy, a segmentectomy saves a larger amount of healthy lung tissue. Research shows that a segmentectomy can result in less blood loss, shorter operation time, less days of having a chest tube, and a shorter hospital stay, compared to a lobectomy. With the advances in screening technology for lung cancer tumours, an increasing amount of very small lung cancer tumours are being found, and the demand for segmentectomy is increasing. A segmentectomy is a hard surgery to perform robotically because it is difficult to view the tissue lines that separate each segment within the lobe. As a result, it is difficult for the surgeon to see exactly which pieces of tissue should be removed in order to safely complete the segmentectomy. Because of these challenges, many patients having robotic surgery will have a lobectomy, even if a full lobectomy is not needed. In response to these challenges, our surgical group has developed the technique of using Near-Infrared Fluorescence (NIF) mapping with intravascular indocyanine green (ICG) dye injection. With the aid of an infrared camera the surgeon is able to see the segment within a lobe of lung after injection of the ICG dye, allowing for a more accurate segmentectomy. We recently reported a 60% success rate of segmental resections with the use of ICG and NIF-guided surgical resection. However, a limitation to this technique is that the segmental anatomy can only be seen during the operation and only after cutting the blood vessels. The introduction of 3D reconstruction and virtual modeling provides a new way to locate lesions accurately within a segment and plan the appropriate operation before the actual surgery occurs. Synapse 3D (Mississauga, Canada) is a 3D modelling technology that is capable of producing a detailed 3D virtual model of a patient's lung based on Computed Tomography (CT) scans. It has been shown to be safe and feasible in performing segmental pulmonary resections on a robotic platform. In this study, we propose a new operation that uses 3D anatomical planning before the surgery (Synapse 3D) and real-time NIF-mapping at the time of surgery using ICG dye, which we believe will greatly increase the likelihood of a successful segmentectomy. If this new operation is successful, it will help patients save more of their healthy lung tissue when they are undergoing surgery for lung cancer.
ID  - 2728
