TY  - JOUR
TI  - Outcomes and Patterns of Failure for Stage I Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer in a Veteran Population
AU  - Kumar, A.
AU  - Kan, C.
AU  - Traughber, B.
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.1016/S0360-3016(19)30498-5
AB  - Background: Lobectomy is the standard of care of Stage 1 non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), however, other local therapies such as wedge resection and definitive radiation have successfully treated early stage lung cancer. Objectives: We examine short-term outcomes and patterns of failure in a veteran population. Methods: Veterans with Stage 1 NSCLC diagnosed from 1/1/2017–12/31/2017 were abstracted from our tumor registrar. Two patients refused any treatment and were excluded. Veterans who were operable were resected with wedge resection with lymph node sampling per thoracic surgeon preference even if they were a candidate for lobectomy. Medically inoperable patients were referred for radiation with dose/fractionation determined by the treating radiation oncologist. All treatments were at the VA except for SBRT that was referred outside of the VA. Outpatient follow up with PET/CT based imaging every 3–6 months was done at our VA medical center. Pathology and imaging were reviewed to analyze for patterns of failure. JMP v12 was used for statistical analysis. Results: 33 veterans were identified. None of the veterans received upfront systemic treatment. All treated lesions were biopsied as squamous cell carcinoma (48%), adenocarcinoma (48%), or large cell carcinoma (4%). 22 veterans had a wedge resection with median follow up 12.8 months and 11 had definitive radiation (6 had SBRT 50–60 Gy in 3–5 fractions; 4 had 60 Gy in 15 fractions; 1 was referred for SBRT but received 70 Gy in 35 fractions) with median follow up 11.4 months. In the surgery group, 22% (5/22) had recurrent disease and 9% (2/22) passed away. One recurrence was intralobar and salvaged with lobectomy; 3 patients had mediastinal nodal recurrences and received salvage chemoradiation/immunotherapy; 1 patient had local and distant recurrence and passed away. In the radiation cohort, 36% (4/11) had recurrent disease. 2 patients treated with SBRT had local recurrence close to or adjacent to the high-dose field, 1 patient (25%) with 60 Gy in 15 fractions recurred with metastatic progression in the setting of a previous lung cancer. 1 patient (100%) with standard fractionation had a local recurrence. The SBRT local recurrences were driven by results from one referral center. Conclusions: Wedge resection and definitive radiation (SBRT and hypofractionated radiation) are effective for Stage I NSCLC with recurrence rates between 20–33%. Interestingly, in our small cohort of veterans, recurrence with wedge resection was more likely to be regional than radiation options which were more likely to be local failures. Certain radiation centers appear to have higher success with lung SBRT. Limiting SBRT to high quality radiation programs may be necessary to reduce local failures.
