TY  - JOUR
TI  - Video assisted thoracoscopic right lower lobectomy for lung cancer
AU  - Lirici, M.M.
AU  - Tierno, S.M.
AU  - Romeo, V.
AU  - Simonelli, L.
PY  - 2018
DO  - 10.1007/s00464-018-6179-z
AB  - Anatomic inferior lobectomy and lymph-node dissection is the standard treatment of early-stage non-small cell lung cancer of the right lower lobe. Traditionally, this procedure is carried out via postero-lateral thoracotomy,and requires division of chest wall muscles and rib spreading. Such an approach is frequently associated with chronic postoperative pain. One of the major potential advantages of video-assisted thoracoscopic (VATS) lobectomy is decreasing the incidence of chronic post-thoracotomy pain. The video shows a VATS inferior lobectomy in an 80 years old man with a 3 cm lesion of the inferior right lobe,addressed to surgical treatment after diagnostic and staging work-up including total body CT scan and bronchoscopy with positive cytology. After proper patient positioning, 3 accesses are created: A 5-cm mini-thoracotomy on the 5th intercostal space at the level of the anterior axillary line and two 10/12 mm incision respectively on the 5th intercostal space at the posterior axillary line and the 7th intercostal space. Gained access to the pleural cavity a careful dissection of the pulmonary ligament and the inferior pulmonary vein is carried out. The vein is dissected free and encircled by tape but not yet divided. Thereafter, the pulmonary arterial branches to the inferior lobe are dissected free, using a 'fissure-sparing' technique whenever possible to decrease the incidence of prolonged postoperative air-leaks. At this stage, arterial node clearance is performed. This maneuver helps completion of arterial dissection: The artery is encircled by tape, as well. Completion of fissure dissection with division of the remaining posterior lung parenchyma is achieved using endoscopic stapler. Blood vessels are divided using endoscopic staplers as well with the following sequence: Inferior lobe artery (branches A7,8,9,10), inferior pulmonary vein, superior segment artery (branch A6). The inferior bronchus is divided after clearance of the peri-bronchial nodes which are removed en-block with the specimen. The remaining lung parenchyma is insufflated to check any air leak. The specimen is retrieved with an endo-bag through the minithoracotomy. Histology showed a pT2, pN0 adenocarcinoma, and an R0 resection. The postoperative course was uneventful, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day 6. VATS with curative intent for cancer of the inferior lobes can be carried out with similar morbidity and similar oncologic outcome to traditional open surgery.
