AU  - Lu, X.
AU  - Yang, H.
AU  - Liu, D.
PY  - 2018
DO  - 10.1200/JCO.2018.36.15_suppl.e20522
AB  - Background: The golden surgery standard of early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is lobectomy with dissection of mediastinal lymph nodes. Due to the risks of reduced pulmonary reserve, comorbidities, and incidences of complications, sublobar resection was demonstracted to be an alternative therapy in elderly patients. Given that the clinical data are limited, the study was designed to evaluate effectiveness and safety of lobectomy and sublobar resection in the target population. Methods: The study data on the elderly NSCLC patients (≥65 years) who received lobectomy vs sublobar resections [segment vs wedge resection]) from 2014 to 2017 were collected from the LinkDoc database. The Kaplan-Meier method and the Log-rank test were performed to evaluate overall survival (OS) based on the method of propensity score matching (PSM). Results: A total of 4490 NSCLC patients were enrolled in this study including 82.8% of patients with lobectomy, 17.2% with sublobar resections (4.9% segment resection and 12.4% wedge resection). After PSM, the 1, 2, and 3-year OS rates in patients with sublobar resection were 97.4%, 93.2%, and 93.2%, which were significantly higher than those with lobectomy (92.9%, 86.2%, and 82.0%). The OS of patients with lobectomy was inferior to that of patients with wedge resection (HR = 1.76, p < 0.05) and segment resection (HR = 4.00, p < 0.05). No significant OS differences was observed between the groups of the patients with wedge and segment resections. The percentage of postoperative mortality rate within 90 days after surgeries was higher in the patients with lobectomy (1.1%) than in those with sublobar resection (0.3%) (p < 0.05). The most common complications were pleural effusion (5.0%), pneumonia (4.9%), and pneumothorax (3.3%). Conclusions: Our results demonstrated that the OS of patients with sublobar resection was superior to that of patients with lobectomy in Chinese elderly patients with NSCLC,. The realworld evidences would offer complementary clinical evidences on the choice of surgical procedures for those elderly patients with early-stage NSCLC.
