TI  - Analysis of recurrence and survival after surgery or sterotactic radiotherapy for early stage NSCLC
AU  - Khemasuwan, D.
AU  - Sorenson, J.S.
AU  - Cannon, G.
AU  - Teerapunchareon, K.
AU  - Griffin, D.
PY  - 2018
AB  - Background: - Lobectomy and mediastinal lymph node dissection is the standard procedure for clinically operable patients with early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). In patients who are not operative candidates secondary to comorbidities, stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) has become the standard for treatment in inoperable NSCLC patients. Methods: - We performed a retrospective study of survival rates and patterns of tumor recurrence in a consecutive cohort of patients with stage I NSCLC (cT1a-bN0M0) with surgery or SBRT. All the patients were followed for three years post-treatment. The primary analysis used a continuous-time Markov multi-state model (MSM) to estimate the effect of surgery vs SBRT on time to death, treating recurrence as a competing risk and adjusting for age, Karnofsky score, Charlson comorbidity index, FEV1, DLCO, tumor staging (cTNM), and a multiplicative interaction between age and cTNM. Four sensitivity analyses were conducted using the Fine and Gray sub-distribution hazards model of competing risks in which death was treated as the event of interest, each utilizing propensity scores (PS) in a different way. PS were computed from a logistic model predicting surgery vs SBRT, with the same covariates as those specified in the primary analysis. Results: - We identified 228 patients of whom 20% (n=45) underwent received SBRT, the remainder (n=183) undergoing surgery. On average, surgery-treated patients were younger (70 vs 79, p<0.001), had lower Charlson comorbidity index (7 vs 9, p=0.002), higher FEV1 (1.9 vs 1.1, p<0.001), and higher DLCO (16.8 vs 9.7, p<0.001). Overall survival at 3 years was 71% (162 patients). The overall recurrence rates (local, regional, and distant) were 21.8% (40/183) in the surgical arm and 22.2% (10/45) in the SBRT arm. The MSM found a significant improvement with surgery on the hazard of death given recurrence (HR: 0.14; 95% CI: 0.03 to 0.65), but showed no significant effect of surgery in terms of hazard of recurrence (HR: 0.63; 95% CI: 0.31 to 1.27) or hazard of death without recurrence (HR: 3.51; 95% CI: 0.02 to 88.08). None of the sensitivity analyses found a significant effect of surgery on the estimated overall hazard of death. (Table1) Conclusion: - Given recurrence, surgical patients had better overall survival than did SBRT-treated patients. However, there was no significant effect of surgery on hazard of recurrence or survival without recurrence in clinical stage I NSCLC compared with SBRT in this cohort.
