TI  - Evaluation of pulmonary function at 24-months post-lobectomy among lung cancer patients with stage I non-small cell lung cancer
AU  - Albano, D.
AU  - Bilfinger, T.V.
AU  - Perwaiz, M.K.
AU  - Nemesure, B.
PY  - 2018
AB  - Background: Lung cancer continues to be the leading cause of oncologic deaths in the United States. In patients with stage I non-small cell lung cancer, resection remains the recommended treatment option. An pre-operative pulmonary evaluation, includes diffusing capacity of the lungs (DLCO) and forced exhalation (FEV1) to assess tolerance for surgery, data are scarce beyond the first 6 months following resection. The purpose of this investigation was to assess changes in pulmonary function test (PFT) outcomes at 24-months post-lobectomy. Methods: This retrospective investigation included a pilot sample of 74 stage I, NSCLC, lobectomy patients. Predicted DLCO% and FEV1% were collected pre op and at 1- and 2-years post op. Univariate regression analyses were used to assess whether tumor location were predictive of post temporal changes in predicted DLCO% and FEV1%. Subsequent multivariate models were used to adjust for age at surgery, gender and smoking status. Results: 74 patients with tumor positions: 6 left lower, 16 left upper, 18 right lower, 7 right middle, 27 right upper. Baseline predicted DLCO% ranged from 40-143% with mean (SD) of 81.7% (20.4%). At Year 2, DLCO% decreased from pre-surgical measures in 44.0% of patients, remained constant in 22.7% and increased in 33.3% of cases. Position of the tumor did not significantly influence changes in DLCO% over time. For FEV1%, pre-surgical values ranged from 41-163% with mean (SD) of 87.1% (22.2%). At 2 years following lobectomy, predicted FEV1% decreased in 53.3%, remained in 24.0% and increased in 22.7% of cases. Patients with tumor locations in the upper quadrants were significantly more likely to have compromised FEV1% at 24-months post-resection (p<0.05), the finding was attenuated after adjustment for age, gender and smoking status (p=0.17). Conclusions: This preliminary investigation suggests that pulmonary function improves in approximately 1/4-1/3 of stage I NSCLC patients during the 2 years following surgery. Additionally, there may be a potential relationship between tumor location and post-surgical pulmonary function (FEV1%), however this finding requires further investigation. This study is ongoing to evaluate these preliminary results in a larger sample. The preliminary investigation suggests that DLCO improves in approximately 1/3 of patients at 24 months while FEV1 improves in only 1/4 of the patients. Close to 1/2 of the patients have not gained back their DLCO or FEV1 at 2 years suggesting that lobectomy in relatively healthy patients has a long term impact on pulmonary performance in about 1/2 of the patients.
