AU  - Hahn, S.M.
AU  - Gomez, D.R.
AU  - Chun, S.G.
PY  - 2017
DO  - 10.1016/j.ijrobp.2017.01.146
AB  - Purpose/Objective(s): The 1998 post-operative radiation therapy (PORT) meta-analysis for lung cancer showed a survival detriment associated with PORT for stage I-II resected nonesmall cell lung cancer (NSCLC), but this analysis is often criticized for including antiquated radiation techniques no longer used in the United States, no CT-planning, and no radiation quality assurance. We aimed to determine the current prevalence and impact of PORT on overall survival after margin negative (R0) resection for AJCC stage I-II NSCLC in the United States. Materials/Methods: All adult (age > 18) patients with stage I-II NSCLC with R0 resection from 2004-2014 were analyzed from the National Cancer Database (NCDB) with respect to receiving radiation as part of their first course of treatment. Results: There were 197,969 patients who underwent R0 resection for stage I-II NSCLC, of whom 4,613 received radiation as part of initial treatment. The median dose was 55 Gy with a 50-60 Gy interquartile range. On adjusted analysis, treatment at a community cancer program, sub-lobectomy, larger tumor size (3-7 cm), and pN1/NX disease were associated with receiving PORT (OR > 1, P<.05). The irradiated group had significantly shorter median survival (45.8 vs 77.5 months, P<.001), and multivariable analysis showed radiation was independently associated with worse overall survival (HR 1.339, 95% CI 1.282-1.399). After propensity score matching, radiation remained independently associated with significantly worse overall survival (HR 1.313, 95 % CI: 1.237-1.394, P<.001). Conclusion: We identified a subset of patients receiving radiation in the NCDB after R0 resection for Stage I-II NSCLC, and that radiation was independently associated with worse overall survival. Despite criticisms of the 1998 PORT meta-analysis, we found no evidence to refute its conclusions from a more modern cohort in the NCDB.
