TI  - 3-year survival among early stage lung cancer patients treated with lobectomy vs SBRT
AU  - Albano, D.
AU  - Bilfinger, T.
AU  - Chokshi, K.
AU  - Nemesure, B.
PY  - 2017
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm-conference.2017.B110
AB  - Introduction: Lung Cancer is the leading cause of cancer deaths worldwide. Lobectomy has traditionally lead to the best survival for fit patients diagnosed with early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), while Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT) has generally served as the suggested treatment option for patients with compromised health status. More recently, SBRT is being offered as a less invasive alternative to surgery for healthier patients, as well. The purpose of this investigation is to compare 3-year survival outcomes for individuals with biopsy-proven stage 1 or 2 NSCLC undergoing surgical resection with lobectomy vs SBRT. Methods: Preliminary data for this retrospective study included 43 patients treated by lobectomy and 39 patients receiving SBRT between 2008 and 2012. Patients were identified through the Lung Cancer Evaluation Center database at the Stony Brook Cancer Center and overall survival at 3-years was determined from the date of diagnosis. Comparisons between those receiving resection vs SBRT were evaluated using Kaplan-Meier curves and log-rank tests. Cox proportional hazard (PH) models were adjusted for gender, age and presence of co-morbidities, with the latter 2 variables serving as a potential proxy for overall health status Results: The 3-year survival rate among patients in the surgery group was 93.0% compared to 76.9% among those receiving radiation therapy. Patients treated by surgical resection had a 3-year mean (+/- sd) survival of 35.0 +/- 0.66 months, whereas the average survival duration for the SBRT group was 32.3 +/- 1.27 months. Overall improved survival outcomes for the surgical group were found to be statistically significant using the log rank test (p=0.04), however, after adjusting for age, gender and presence of co-morbidities, the results were suggestive but did not achieve statistical significance (Cox PH model HR=2.80, 95% CI (0.68, 11.52); p=0.15) Conclusions: Findings from this preliminary investigation suggest that patients with stage 1 or stage 2 NSCLC may benefit from lobectomy compared to SBRT. Although the sample size in the present study is limited, data collection is ongoing to further evaluate overall 3-year survival in a larger cohort of patients, including those with sub-lobe resections, as well as 5-year survival among similar groups of early stage NSCLC patients. The results may assist in guiding treatment practices in the future.
ID  - 2992
