TY  - JOUR
TI  - BILATERALVIDEO-ASSISTED THORACOSCOPIC LUNG RESECTIONS: IS ITA SAFETY PROCEDURE?
AU  - Hernandez-Arenas, L.
AU  - Yang, Y.
AU  - Zhu, Y.
AU  - Wang, H.
AU  - Gonzalez-Rivas, D.
AU  - Jiang, G.
PY  - 2016
DO  - 10.1093/icvts/ivw260.228
AB  - Objectives: To audit our experience regarding the safety and the clinical and surgical outcomes of bilateral video-assisted thoracoscopic (VATS) procedures. Shanghai Pulmonary Hospital performs more than 8000 VATS procedures per year. Most of the cases are lung cancer in early stage disease (accuracy more than 94%) thanks to the screening program. Methods: Between January 2010 and March 2015, 311 selected patients underwent bilateral VATS lung resection with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) early stage disease and benign disease. Indications for bilateral approach include patients with a T-status of tumour <5 cm (T1, T2) N status for tumour N0, probed synchronic tumours, bilateral ground glass opacity (GGO) >0.8 cm. Exclusion criteria include: chest wall involvement, central masses, previous thoracic surgery, adhesions and N1-N2 disease. Perioperative variables and outcomes were collected prospectively and analysed retrospectively. Results: A total of 311 patients underwent the bilateral VATS procedure, 220 cases of lung cancer and 91 benign disease (bullectomy and lung repair) were noted. Bilateral multiport VATS were 242 cases (77%). Bilateral uniportal VATS were 42 cases (14%) and bilateral subxiphoid VATS were 27 cases (9%). Wedge-wedge (47%) and lobectomy-wedge (24%) were the most common procedures. In patients with lung cancer the total number of lymph node stations sampled or dissected were 2.8 ± 0.3. Prolonged air leak (PAL) was the most common complication. Two cases were converted to thoracotomy for technical difficulties. There were no deaths 30 days after surgery or readmissions. All cases presented a rate of complete cancer resection R0. Conclusion: Bilateral VATS procedure for benign diseases, specially pneumothorax and lung bulla repair is well described with acceptable clinical outcomes. For malignant disease it is not performed routinely but with a good selection of the patients the bilateral VATS procedure for NSCLC early stage disease (GGOs, synchronic lung cancer, oligometastasis cancer) has a longer operating time, longer hospital stay but with safe surgical and medical outcomes thus avoiding the need for subsequent operations.
ID  - 3008
