TY  - JOUR
TI  - IS DEFINITIVE SURGICAL RESECTION ACCEPTABLE AS ATREATMENT STRATEGY FOR CLINICAL-STAGE I RADIOLOGICAL PURE-SOLID LUNG CANCER SATISFIED WITH CURRENT MEDICALLY INOPERABLE CRITERIA?
AU  - Hattori, A.
AU  - Matsunaga, T.
AU  - Takamochi, K.
AU  - Oh, S.
AU  - Suzuki, K.
PY  - 2016
DO  - 10.1093/icvts/ivw260.46
AB  - Objectives: Radiological pure-solid lung cancers are considered to be invasive despite their small tumour size. Pulmonary lobectomy is a favourable surgical procedure to prevent locoregional recurrence. However, treatment strategy for patients defined as medically inoperable is still controversial. Methods: We reviewed 500 surgically-resected c-stage I radiological puresolid lung cancer. Pure-solid tumour was defined as a tumour without ground glass opacity (GGO) component on thin-section CT, i.e. consolidation tumour ratio equal to 1.0. Based on the medically inoperable status currently used in clinical trials of stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) for non-small cell lung cancer, high-risk group was defined as follow: (i) preoperative FEV1% or DLCO% < 50%, (ii) 50% < FEV1% or DLCO% <60% with age >75 years, (iii) Three or more severe comorbidities. The prognostic value and surgical outcomes were evaluated between the high-risk (n = 184) and normal-risk group (n = 316). Results: The proportion of males, elderly subjects and smokers were higher among the high-risk groups than the normal-risk group (P < 0.001). Lobectomy was performed in 148 (80%) among the high-risk group. A multivariate analysis revealed that maximum tumour size and CEA titer were clinically significant prognostic factors (P = 0.002, 0.004), while medically high-risk criteria was not associated with poor survival (P = 0.454). The 30- and 90-day mortality rates among the high-risk group were 1.6% and 3.8%, and those among the 427 (85%) patients undergoing lobectomy were 2.0% and 4.0%, respectively. Although overall survival was significantly worse in the high-risk groups (59.4% vs 73.1%, P = 0.004), there were no significant differences in cancer-specific survival between the high-risk and normal-risk arm (74.5% vs 79.2%, P = 0.569), which was also proven in the patients undergoing lobectomy (72.7% vs 81.5%, P = 0.481). Conclusion: Current medically inoperable criteria are not always appropriate prognostic variable. Surgical management including lobectomy for clinical stage I radiological pure-solid lung cancer defined as high-risk could be safely performed with acceptable perioperative mortality and equivalent cancer-specific survival.
ID  - 3012
