TI  - An audit of relapse rate in early stage non-small cell lung cancer treated with surgical resection
AU  - Woolcock, S.
AU  - Quinn, R.
AU  - Pollock, S.
AU  - Chamberlin, N.
AU  - Kersbergen, J.
AU  - Murphy, S.
AU  - Sridharan, K.
AU  - Stamenkovic, S.
AU  - Taylor, I.
PY  - 2016
AB  - Introduction: Survival rates from non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) are strongly related to stage of disease at diagnosis. Patients presenting with Stage I disease (T1a/bNo, T2aNo) have highest survival rates at 1 year (71%) and 5 years (35%). Current guidance recommends that patients who are suitable for treatment with curative intent, should be offered surgery as treatment of choice. Post-operative chemotherapy is recommended for those with good performance status (WHO 0 or 1) and T1-3N1-2M0 disease. Aims: To retrospectively review relapse rates for early stage NSCLC following surgical resection. Methods: In a single centre study we retrospectively reviewed all patients with early stage NSCLC undergoing surgical resection between January 2012 and January 2015. Suitable patients were identified from the lung cancer registry and relevant demographic and individual disease specific information was collected. Results: 666 patients were diagnosed with NSCLC (all stages). 79 patients underwent surgical resection. 13/79 (16%) patients relapsed. Average time from surgery to relapse was 8.2 months. Staging: Radiological staging (pre-operatively): IA 3/13 (23%), IB 8/13 (62%), IIB 2/13 (15%). Pathological staging (post-operatively): IA 2/13 (15%), IB 9/13 (69%), IIA 2/13 (15%). Average SUV PET: 14.1 (3.1-32.9). Histological subtype: Adenocarcinoma 8/13 (61%), Squamous cell 4/13 (31%), Neuroendocrine 1/13 (8%). Type of surgery: Lobectomy 11/13 (85%), Wedge resection 1/13 (8%), Lobectomy and chest wall resection 1/13 (8%). Adjuvant treatment: 6/13 (46%) met criteria for adjuvant treatment; 3/13 (23%) had incomplete tumour resection. 2/6 (33%) had adjuvant radiotherapy (for incomplete resection), 1/6 (17%) received adjuvant chemotherapy, 3/6 (50%) were too unwell for adjuvant treatment. Conclusion: Surgical resection is recommended for patients with early stage NSCLC. This should be followed by adjuvant chemotherapy in suitable patients. Our data shows that 18% of patients relapsed within 3 years of surgery with average time between surgery and relapse 9-10 months. Correlation between radiological and pathological staging appear accurate and no intra-operative surgical issue was identified. Suitable patients for adjuvant treatment were identified and treated appropriately. The presence of occult micro-metastatic disease may precipitate relapse. Whilst tumour subtype and SUV PET values can offer prognostic value, the absence of a suitable biomarker makes micrometastatic disease difficult to predict. Our data suggests that the role of adjuvant chemotherapy to treat micro-metastatic disease needs further consideration and review.
ID  - 3039
