AB  - Background: Exon 19 deletion (Del19) and exon 21 L858R mutation (L858R) are the most common EGFR mutations in non-small cell lung cancer. The purpose of this study was to investigate the difference of clinicopathological features between Del19 and L858R in EGFR-mutant completely resected invasive lung adenocarcinoma. Methods: We identified 195 patients with stage I-III invasive lung adenocarcinoma harboring common EGFR mutations, who underwent complete resection at our institution from 2004 to 2011. Clinicopathological features between Del19 and L858R were retrospectively compared for histologic subtypes by IASLC/ATS/ERS classification and clinical outcomes. Results: Among 195 patients with common EGFR mutations, 79 patients had Del19, whereas 116 had L858R. There was no significant difference in pathological stage between the two groups. The most predominant histologic subtype of Del19 was papillary (49%) followed by acinar (18%), lepidic (14%), solid (16%), and micropapillary (3%). In subtypes of L858R, there were papillary (37%), lepidic (30%), acinar (22%), solid (9%), and micropapillary (2%). The proportion of lepidic in L858R was higher compared with Del19 (P = 0.014). Patients with Del19 tend to have shorter disease-free survival compared with patients with L858R, although this difference was not statistically significant (5-year DFS, 34 vs. 50%; P = 0.080). In addition, there was no significant difference in overall survival (median, 97 vs. 93 months; P = 0.839). At the time of recurrence, 79% of patients with common EGFR mutations were treated with EGFR TKI. No significant differences in response rate (63 vs. 63%) and progression-free survival (17.1 vs. 15.8 months: P = 0.528) by EGFR TKI treatment were observed between the two groups. Conclusions: The proportion of predominant histologic subtype in EGFR-mutant completely resected invasive lung adenocarcinoma was different between Del19 and L858R. There was no significant difference in clinical outcomes.
