DO  - 10.1093/annonc/mdv472.7
AB  - Purpose: Small sized non-small cell lung cancers (NSCLCs) have been increasingly detected due to recent advances in diagnostic modalities. Although limited surgical resection (sublobar resection) offers equivalent outcomes to lobectomy for early stage lung cancer patients, its definitive efficacy is still controversial. Lymph node metastasis can occur even in patients with NSCLCs 20mm or less in size. We investigated the significance of the preoperative SUVmax in patients with c-T1a NSCLC. Patients and methods: We reviewed retrospectively consecutive patients who underwent surgical resection for c-T1aN0M0 NSCLC between 2010 and 2014 who had preoperative PET/CT imaging. SUVmax was correlated with histology, lymphovascular ivasion, pleural invasion, histological grade, lymph node involvement and the incidence of relapse. Results: This study consisted of 80 patients (52 male, 28 female, median age; 70). Of these, 43, 10 and 27 underwent lobectomy, segmentectomy and wedge resection, respectively. The most common histology was adenocarcinoma (78%). The median follow-up time was 23 months and 6 patients (8%) had a recurrence. The median SUVmax was 1.75 (range 0.4-21.2) and the median tumor size was 16mm (range 8-20). Significant differences were observed in SUVmax for histology (adenocarcinoma vs. non-adenocarcinoma, p = 0.02), lympho-vascular invasion ( positive vs negative, p < 0.01), pleural invasion ( positive vs negative, p < 0.01), histological grade (G1 vs G2/ 3, p < 0.01) and lymph node involvement ( positive vs negative, p < 0.01). The incidence of relapse was not correlated with the level of SUVmax (p = 0.07). In this analysis, most patients with SUVmax < 3.0 had p-Stage IA (98%). Conclusion: The preoperative SUVmax is linked to possible malignant features such as lympho-vascular invasion, pleural invasion, histological grade and lymph node involvement and might be useful in considering the indication for applying limited resection in c-T1a NSCLC.
