TY  - JOUR
TI  - Combining gene and miRNA expression for early detection of lung cancer from whole blood sample in paxgene
AU  - Showe, L.C.
PY  - 2015
AB  - Rationale: A blood-based gene expression signature that accurately predicts the presence of a cancer in the lung provides a non-invasive method to assess the malignancy of indeterminate lung nodules. Our previous study using peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC) RNA compared gene expression in early stage NSCLC patients to high risk controls with other smoking related lung disease. We identified a 29 gene signature that classified 137 lung cancers and 91 controls with 86% accuracy (91% sensitivity, 80% specificity). We observed changes in the signature post-lung resection, identified a potential defect in NK cell function and a myeloid signature associated with poor prognosis. The present study determined whether a similar or better classification could be obtained using whole blood collected with the PAXgene RNA stabilization system. Methods: We analyzed gene expression on Illumina human HT12v4 microarrays and miRNA expression on the OpenArray PCR platform. We assessed gene and/or miRNA expression in >600 samples from patients with primarily early stage lung cancer and high risk controls with smoking related lung disease including lung nodules. Samples were obtained from four different clinical sites and included a large cohort from the NYU lung screening initiative. We also assessed a subset of samples from post-lung resection blood and from patients serially imaged over several years. We analyzed gene and miRNA expression separately and combined. The results presented are for 345 samples analyzed on both platforms with 70% used for training (242) and 30% for testing (103). Results: The classifier developed on the training set demonstrated 83% accuracy on the test set using 125 gene probes and 20 miRNAs, an increase compared to using only mRNAs (80% accuracy) or only miRNAs (73% accuracy). A sensitivity of 81% and specificity of 84% was achieved. Based on our power calculation a training pool of 550 samples was needed to achieve 90% accuracy. The results for the 242 sample training set aligned with the estimated power curve are exactly on target with this prediction. Conclusions: PAXgene samples retain gene expression information that can predict the presence of a malignant tumor in the lung. The most accurate classifier in initial studies combines miRNA (non-coding gene) and mRNA (coding gene) expression. More features are required for the PAXgene signature than for PBMC but that number is likely to diminish with a larger training set.
