TI  - STAGING AND SURVIVAL OUTCOME AFTER ANATOMIC SEGMENTECTOMY VERSUS LOBECTOMY FOR CLINCAL STAGE I NON-SMALL-CELL LUNG CANCER
AU  - Beckers, F.
AU  - Werner, I.
AU  - Stoelben, E.
PY  - 2015
DO  - 10.1093/icvts/ivv204.163
AB  - Objectives: Although anatomic segmentectomy has been considered a compromised procedure by many surgeons, there are some series that have demonstrated tumour recurrence and patient survival rates that are equivalent to those achieved by lobectomy. The primary object of this study was to analyse the survival rates of segmentectomy vs lobectomy in clinical stage I lung cancer. Methods: The retrospective data set included 451 patients with clinical stage I lung cancer operated on by anatomic segmentectomy or lobectomy between 2006 and 2011 from a single centre. Results: Between 2006 and 2011, 451 patients with clinical stage I lung cancer received an antomic segmentectomy (n = 173, group 1) or a lobectomy (n = 272, group 2). Perioperative mortality was 0.5% in group 1 and 1.4% in group 2. There was no significant difference in 5-year survival between the groups (68% vs 62%, P = 0.381). Segmentectomy was not found to be an independent predictor of recurrence or overall survival. In the patients with clincal stage cT1b with a tumour size between 2 and 3 cm, the percentage of unsuspected N2 nodal involvement was 9.2%, in the group of clinical stage 1a tumours (<2 cm) the rate of unsuspected N2 was 5.2%. Conclusions: In this large and homogenous collective study, the 5-year survival rate showed no significant difference between anatomical segmentectomy or lobectomy in clinical stage I lung cancer. Anatomical segmentectomy is an oncologically equivalent parenchymal-saving resection type for early stage lung cancer. In contrast to the guidelines, our data suggested that patients with a tumour diameter between 2 and 3 cm (cT1b) should receive an invasive staging of the mediastinum to reduce the rate of unsuspected lymph node involvement.
