TY  - JOUR
TI  - Robotic sublobar resection for the surgical management of isolated pulmonary nodules
AU  - Reis, V.
AU  - Langston, K.M.
AU  - Talsania, A.
AU  - Szwerc, M.F.
AU  - Werner, A.
PY  - 2015
DO  - 10.1097/IMI.0000000000000143
AB  - Objective: The surgical management of early-stage non-small cell lung cancer is in evolution. Open thoracotomy is slowly being replaced by videoassisted thoracoscopic techniques, and more conservative anatomic lung resections are gaining popularity over lobectomy in the management of earlystage lung cancer. This study evaluates the outcomes of combining robotic techniques with pulmonary segmentectomy. This study assesses the efficacy and perioperative outcomes of robotic-assisted pulmonary segmentectomy. Methods: From October, 2011, and June, 2014, 186 robotic anatomic pulmonary resections were performed. Of these, 36 of which were anatomic segmentectomies. Perioperative outcomes were evaluated. Results: A total of 36 robotic-assisted segmentectomies were performed. The most common indication for resection was management of early-stage nonsmall cell lung carcinoma. The median length of stay was 2 days. Median OR time was 121 minutes. There were no deaths within 30 days of surgery. The most common complication was persistent air leak. Conclusions: Robotic-assisted segmentectomy is a safe procedure. Robotic techniques may result in a reduced length of stay and morbidity of sublobar pulmonary resection.
