TI  - Risk factors for delayed recovery of exercise capacity after thoracoscopic lobectomy for lung cancer patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease
AU  - Hamada, K.
AU  - Nakanishi, R.
AU  - Irie, M.
AU  - Yasuda, M.
AU  - Nomura, H.
PY  - 2015
DO  - 10.1016/j.physio.2015.03.3310
AB  - Background: Coexisting of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease have resulted in poor outcomes after lobectomy for lung cancer patients owing to impaired lung function. Many studies demonstrated that minimally invasive approach for lobectomy is associated with better postoperative outcomes than a open thoracotomy approach. However, few studies have investigated the factors related with recovery of exercise capacity in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease underwent thoracoscopic lobectomy for lung cancer. Purpose: The objectives of this study were to clarify the factors related with a delayed recovery of exercise capacity in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease undergoing thoracoscopic lobectomy for early-stage non-small cell lung cancer. Methods: Between June 2005 and October 2013, we retrospectively reviewed 68 consecutive patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease who underwent thoracoscopic lobectomy and the perioperative pulmonary rehabilitation. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is defined when the percentage of forced expiratory volume at 1 second to forced vital capacity is less than 0.70. The severity of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease was graded according to Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung disease criteria. All patients were assessed exercise capacity using six-minute walk distance preoperatively and on postoperative day 7. The patients whose exercise capacity on postoperative restored to less than 80% of their baseline capacity were included in the delayed recovery group. Pre and perioperative variables were analyzed by univariate and multivariate analyses for risk factor of delayed recovery of exercise capacity. Results: The distribution of severity of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease was as follows: 43 cases (63%) for mild, 19 cases (28%) for moderate, and 6 cases (9%) for severe. Twenty patients were included in the delayed recovery groups (29%). Multivariate analyses showed that desaturation of more than 4% during preoperative six-minute walk test (odds ratio, 6.60; P = 0.009) and lower body mass index than 18.5 kg/m2 (odds ratio, 6.84; P = 0.043) were significant factors for the delayed recovery of exercise capacity after surgery in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Conclusion(s): Preoperative evaluation of desaturation during exercise test and nutritional status may be useful to identify those patients who are at increased risk of delayed recovery of exercise capacity in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease undergoing thoracoscopic lobectomy for lung cancer. Implications: Perioperative rehabilitation should be aimed at enhancing the postoperative recovery of exercise capacity, which is an indicator of physical performance after discharge from the hospital, during patients hospitalization in lung cancer patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Our findings will be helpful to planning patientspecific rehabilitation for patients at high-risk for the delayed recovery of exercise capacity in this patients population.
ID  - 3134
