DO  - 10.1111/resp.12494
AB  - Aim: To audit outcome of radical treatment of early stage Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC) in a tertiary teaching hospital. Methods: A retrospective audit of newly diagnosed Stage I to IIb NSCLC patients treated radically with surgery and radiotherapy was undertaken over a period of 2007 - 2012. The Charlson Comorbidity Index was computed for patients at the time of diagnosis to assess if this index could predict outcome of treatment in either modality. Results: 54 patients underwent surgical management and 53 patients underwent radiotherapy with radical intent. Surgical management included lobectomy (52%), wedge resection (42%) and pneumonectomy (6%). Most of the patients treated with radiotherapy were given 60 Gray in 30 Fractions. 88% of patients treated surgically and 76% of patients treated with radiotherapy had Stage I disease. The surgically treated patients were younger (surgical mean age was 69.3 years vs radiotherapy mean age 75.5 years). Patients who were subject to radiotherapy also had more advanced co-existing lung disease. A significant difference in mortality was observed between the two modalities in this audit favouring surgical treatment when compared to radical radiotherapy which carried with it a mortality of 72% during a 5 year period. The mean Charlson Comorbidity Index was computed at the time of diagnosis of lung cancer. The Charlson Comorbidity Index trended higher in patients who had adverse outcomes. Conclusion: There appears to be a significantly favourable outcome for early stage NSCLC patients treated surgically when compared to radical radiotherapy. The Charlson Comorbidity index displayed potential in predicting outcomes.
