TI  - Preoperative serum C-reactive protein predicts longterm outcome in patients undergoing resection for early stage NSCLC
AU  - Marzluf, B.A.
AU  - Krajc, T.
AU  - Möller, M.R.
PY  - 2015
DO  - 10.1007/s00508-015-0873-9
AB  - Background: Inflammation has been shown to contribute to tumor pathogenesis and progression. We hypothesized that elevated preoperative serum C-reactive protein (CRP) is a predictor of worse outcome in patients who underwent anatomical lung resection for NSCLC in stage I. Methods: We conducted a single center retrospective cohort study including patients operated on for NSCLC in stage I between 2002 and 2009. Data recorded apart from preoperative CRP included sex, age, tumor histology, type of surgical procedure, preoperative lung function parameters, preoperative radiation and chemotherapy, surgical and non-surgical complications, and comorbidities. Data on mortality and cause of death up to 2012 were obtained from Statistics Austria. We assessed the influence of CRP on survival by Kaplan Meier and multivariate Cox regression analysis. The study has been approved by the local ethics committee. Results: A total of 684 patients (mean age 62.4 ± 9.4 years; 62.4 % males) were included. The majority of patients had adenocarcinoma (56.0 %) and underwent lobectomy (88.7 %). Overall mean preoperative CRP was 14.2 ± 29.9 mg/l. For CRP = 12.5 mg/l as cutoff value, survival was significantly better for lower (median 3699 days, n = 516) than for higher (median 2022 days, n = 168) CRP by Kaplan Meier analysis and log rank test (p < 0.00001). Multivariate Cox regression analysis identified preoperative CRP as an independent predictor of outcome (HR = 1.004 per increase of 1 mg/l, 95 % CI 1.001-1.008; p = 0.013), in addition to sex, age, and non-surgical complications. Conclusions: Elevated levels of preoperative CRP indicate worse long-Term outcome in patients after radical resection for stage I NSCLC stage.
