AU  - Wong, H.
PY  - 2015
AB  - Introduction: Adjuvant platinum based chemotherapy for Stage II III resected non-small cell lung cancer is considered standard of care. Cisplatin vinorelbine has a robust evidence base to support it, however carboplatin based treatment is used in the UK in patients with borderline performance status. This study aims to compare outcomes in a clinically relevant population. Methods: A retrospective audit of 177 patients who underwent adjuvant chemotherapy for Non small cell lung cancer in Merseyside from 2008 to 2013 was carried out. Data on surgery, performance status, stage, chemotherapy regime and outcomes was gathered from the patient records and analysed. Results: Interim results (from 89 patient records analysed so far) from a group of 89 patients with a median age of 66.5 yrs and a median follow up of 22 months are shown. 72% were PS 0 to 1. 71% had a lobectomy, 27% pneumonectomy, there was a median delay of 58 days from surgery to start of chemotherapy. 58% were Stage I to II, 42% Stage 3A and above. 59% of the patients had cisplatin based chemotherapy, 41% carboplatin based. 53% completed all planned treatment (74% of carboplatin patients and 38% of cisplatin patients); 47% did not complete planned treatment, 72% of these permanently stopped treatment. OS at 6, 12, 24 and 36 months was 98.8%, 93.5%, 81% and 69% respectively with an estimated median survival of 52 months. There was no statistically significant difference in OS for cisplatin, 54.7 months (47-62 months), compared to Carboplatin, 46.7 months (35-58 months) (p value 0.325). Conclusion: Carboplatin based Adjuvant NSCLung cancer chemotherapy does not appear to have significantly different survival outcomes from cisplatin based treatment in a clinically relevant patient group. (Figure Presented).
ID  - 3160
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy in non small cell lung cancer (NSCLC): A review of practice in South East Wales
AU  - Evans, R.
AU  - Johns, E.
AU  - Button, M.
AU  - Brewster, A.
AU  - Hanna, L.
AU  - Lester, J.F.
AU  - Tanguay, J.
PY  - 2015
AB  - Introduction: For patients with resectable non small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) NICE recommends surgical resection as gold standard radical treatment. The LACE meta-analysis suggested that adjuvant Cisplatin-based chemotherapy has an additional beneficial effect on survival. We reviewed current practice to investigate the management and outcomes of this patient group. Methods: A retrospective analysis was undertaken of all patients who received surgery for NSCLC in South East Wales between 2010 and 2013. Data was collated from the Velindre Cancer Centre electronic notes system and LUCADA datasets. Results: 111 patients were identified from 6 South East Wales hospitals. Median age 68 years old, all had a pre-operative WHO performance status ≤ 2 (91.9% PS0/1, 8.1% PS2). The TNM staging: 4.5% IA, 18.9% IB, 28.8% IIA, 21.6% IIB, 24.3% IIIA, 1.8% IIIB, 0% IV. 91.9% of patients underwent a lobectomy, whilst 6.3% had a pneumonectomy and 1.8% had a wedge resection. Resections were classed as R0 in 87.5%, and R1 in 12.5%. Adjuvant radiotherapy was given in 11.7%. 64.9% of patients received adjuvant chemotherapy (of those 84.7% received a Cisplatin doublet, the rest carboplatin). No deaths were identified within 30 days of chemotherapy. Of those who did not receive chemotherapy, 76.9% had a poor performance status or significant co-morbidities and 23.1% declined treatment. The three year overall survival (OS) rate for all patients was 48.6% (three year OS rate for stage I, II and III disease of 56.6%, 47.8% and 42.2% respectively). Conclusion: Appropriate use of adjuvant chemotherapy was observed with a high rate of compliance with Cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy and low morbidity. The observed survival rates are broadly comparable with those seen in the LACE meta-analysis allowing for the unselected patient population.
ID  - 3161
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Complete pathological response after TK-inhibitor therapy in adenocarcinoma of the lung. The role of surgery
AU  - Solli, P.
AU  - Caruana, E.
AU  - Abah, U.
AU  - Granato, F.
PY  - 2015
AB  - Introduction: Epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) (Gefitinib®, Erlotinib®) are effectively used as first line treatment in stage III and IV Non-Squamous NSCLC (NSNSCLC) harbouring the EGFR gene mutation and as second or third line therapy in patients with advanced NSCLC who have failed to respond to conventional chemotherapy. Here we present a case of complete pathological response following Gefitinib® neo-adjuvant treatment in initial Stage IIIA mutated adenocarcinoma of the lung. Methods: We report herein a case of a 59 yrs old lady with a stage IIIA left upper lobe EGFR mutated primary lung adenocarcinoma (T1aN2M0) and previous breast cancer treated with surgical excision and adjuvant chemo-radio-therapy. Results: The patient underwent a standard Cisplatin® + Vinorelbine® protocol with stable disease after 3 cycles. This was followed by a regimen of 250 mg daily administration of Gefitinib® for 4 months. A drastic reduction of the mediastinal tumour burden (T1aN0) was found radiologically and confirmed following invasive (EBUS and subsequent mediastinoscopy) re-staging. A left upper lobectomy with mediastinal nodal dissection was then performed. The pathological report showed a complete response with absence of tumour in the specimen (pT0N0M0) (Figure 1). CT-scan follow-up at 6 months showed no evidence of tumour recurrence. Conclusion: As well as in the present case, EGFR-TKIs are a valid option to treat advanced NSCLC. Little evidence is currently available regarding the use of EGFR-TKIs in locally advanced NSCLC harbouring EGFR mutations. Our report raises multiple questions such as: a) the role of EGFR-TKIs as neo-adjuvant agent in the treatment of locally advanced as well as early stage mutated NSNSCLC; b) the appropriate non-invasive and invasive algorithm of restaging after induction treatment; c) the role and the appropriate timing of surgery after complete response to induction treatment (T0N0M0). We believe that answers could be provided by further prospective and multicentre studies. (Figure Presented).
ID  - 3162
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Are the criteria indicating patients to be "medically inoperable" used in clinical trials of stereotactic body radiotherapy appropriate for patients with early-stage non-small cell lung cancer?
AU  - Takamochi, K.
AU  - Hattori, A.
AU  - Maeyashiki, T.
AU  - Matsunaga, T.
AU  - Oh, S.
AU  - Suzuki, K.
PY  - 2014
DO  - 10.2482/haigan.54.903
AB  - Objective. Stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT) has recently been investigated as an alternative treatment in place of surgery in clinical trials, especially in medically inoperable (MI) patients. However, no clear rationale for the criteria used to determine whether or not a patient is MI has yet been demonstrated. Methods. Between January 2004 and October 2012, 740 patients underwent surgical resection of clinical stage IA non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). In the present study, MI was defined as an FEV1.0 of ≤0.8 l, %DLCO of <40%, PaO2 of ≤70 mmHg, PaCO2 of >50 mmHg or three or more severe comorbidities, based on the criteria of MI frequently used in clinical trials of SBRT for NSCLC patients. The clinicopathological characteristics and surgical outcomes were compared between the MI patients (n=91) and operable patients (n=649). Results. The proportion of males (P=0.002), elderly subjects (P<0.001) and smokers (P<0.001) were higher among the MI patients than the operable patients. Limited surgery (wedge lung resection or segmentectomy) was performed in 37 (40.7%) MI patients and 227 (35.0%) operable patients (P=0.289). Mediastinal lymph node dissection was performed in 48 (52.7%) MI patients and 413 (63.6%) operable patients (P=0.045). The rate of overall morbidity was higher in the MI patients (38.5% in the MI patients vs 23.4% in the operable patients, P=0.002). The 30-day and 90-day mortality rates among the MI patients were 1.1% and 3.3%, respectively. Although overall survival was significantly worse in the MI patients (P=0.002), there were no significant differences in cancer-specific survival between the groups (P=0.293). Conclusions. Surgical resection can be performed safely in MI patients, with an equivalent cancerspecific survival to that observed in operable patients. Therefore, the current criteria used to determine whether a patient is MI for clinical trials of SBRT for NSCLC are not appropriate for evaluating the true degree of operability.
ID  - 3163
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Predictors of imaging surveillance for surgically treated early-stage lung cancer
AU  - Backhus, L.M.
AU  - Farjah, F.
AU  - Zeliadt, S.B.
AU  - Varghese, T.K.
AU  - Cheng, A.
AU  - Kessler, L.
AU  - Au, D.H.
AU  - Flum, D.R.
PY  - 2014
DO  - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2014.06.067
AB  - Background Current guidelines recommend routine imaging surveillance for patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) after treatment. Little is known about surveillance patterns for patients with surgically resected early-stage lung cancer in the community at large. We sought to characterize surveillance patterns in a national cohort. Methods We conducted a retrospective study using the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End-Results (SEER)-Medicare database (1995-2010). Patients with stage I/II NSCLC treated with surgical resection were included. Our primary outcome was receipt of imaging between 4 and 8 months after the surgical procedure. Covariates included demographics and comorbidities. Results Chest radiography (CXR) was the most frequent initial modality (60%), followed by chest computed tomography (CT) (25%). Positron emission tomography (PET) was least frequent as an initial imaging modality (3%). A total of 13% of patients received no imaging within the initial surveillance period. Adherence to National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) guidelines for imaging by overall prevalence was 47% for receipt of CT; however, rates of CT increased over time from 28% to 61% (p < 0.01). Reduced rates of CT were associated with stage I disease and surgical resection as the sole treatment modality. Conclusions Imaging after definitive surgical treatment for NSCLC predominantly used CXR rather than CT. Most of this imaging is likely for surveillance, and in that context CXR has inferior detection rates for recurrence and new cancers. Adherence to guideline-recommended CT surveillance after surgical treatment is poor, but the reasons are multifactorial. Efforts to improve adherence to imaging surveillance must be coupled with greater evidence demonstrating improved long-term outcomes.
ID  - 3164
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Elderly patients undergoing SBRT for inoperable early-stage NSCLC achieve similar outcomes to younger patients
AU  - Mancini, B.R.
AU  - Park, H.S.
AU  - Harder, E.
AU  - Rutter, C.E.
AU  - Corso, C.D.
AU  - Decker, R.H.
AU  - Husain, Z.A.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Purpose/Objective(s): A recent publication demonstrated that lung resection in patients age >65 with early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) was associated with perioperative complications in >50% of cases. This was found to increase with age and was significantly higher for patients age ≥75. It is unclear if older patients undergoing stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) face a similar increased risk. This study aims to address toxicity and survival achieved with SBRT in the elderly compared to younger patients treated at the same institution. Materials/Methods: Records of patients diagnosed with T1-3N0M0 NSCLC treated with SBRT between Sep 2007 and Aug 2013 were reviewed. SBRT was delivered in 3-5 fractions to 40-60 Gy, with a vast majority of patients receiving 54 Gy in 3 fractions. Patients were divided into 2 cohorts, age ≥75 and <75. Kaplan-Meier and Cox proportional hazard analyses were used for univariate and multivariate analyses of survival, respectively. Chi-square and logistic regression analyses were used for univariate and multivariate toxicity assessment, respectively. We compared overall survival (OS), local recurrenceefree survival (LRFS), and distant recurrenceefree survival (DRFS) between cohorts. Results: Two hundred fifty-three patients were identified. Of these, 127 were ≥75 and 126 were <75. Median follow-up was 24.9 mos. There were no differences in gender, ECOG performance status, smoking status, clinical vs pathological diagnosis of NSCLC, histology (adenoca vs all others), T-stage (T1 vs T2-3), peripheral v central tumor, BED, or number of targets between the 2 groups (all P>.05). There was a trend toward less frequent mediastinal staging in the elderly (25.2% vs 34.9%, P=.10). There was no difference between patients ≥75 vs <75 in 2-year OS (48.8% vs 60.4%, P=.95) or LRFS (86.1% vs 88.4%, P=.97). There was a statistically significant difference in 2-year DRFS (89.9% vs 74.9%, HR 0.44, P=.02) favoring patients ≥75. On multivariate Cox proportional hazards analysis, age ≥75 remained associated with improved DRFS (HR 0.40, P=.02). There was no difference in acute or late grade 2-3 toxicity for patients age ≥75 vs <75. Common toxicities included fatigue, chest wall pain, and increased dyspnea/pneumonitis. Rate of grade ≥3 acute toxicity was 5.5% overall (4.7% age ≥75 vs 6.3% age <75). Rate of grade ≥3 late toxicity was 4.0% overall (3.9% age ≥75 vs 4.0% age <75). Most common acute and late grade ≥3 toxicity was increased dyspnea/pneumonitis (3.6% and 2.0%, respectively). Two patients had grade 4 acute, 1 had grade 4 late, and 1 had grade 5 late toxicity in the <75 cohort, with no grade 4-5 toxicity in the >75 cohort. Conclusions: To our knowledge, this is the largest single institution study to date evaluating safety and efficacy of SBRT in an elderly population. In our series, elderly patients undergoing SBRT appear to have achieved similar outcomes and toxicity compared to younger patients.
ID  - 3165
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Predictors of 30-day mortality following resection of early-stage NSCLC: An analysis of the national cancer data base
AU  - Husain, Z.A.
AU  - Kim, A.W.
AU  - Yu, J.B.
AU  - Decker, R.H.
AU  - Corso, C.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Purpose/Objective(s): Studies examining morbidity after lobectomy for early stage NSCLC have shown that patients over the age of 65 have a > 50% incidence of complications, and this risk increases significantly in elderly patients (age ≥ 75). Factors that affect postsurgical 30-day mortality (30-DM), however, are less well defined. Materials/Methods: The National Cancer Data Base (NCDB), was used to identify patients 19 years and older with a diagnosis of stage I NSCLC (cT1-2, cN0, cM0) between the years 2003 and 2011. Data from patients who underwent surgical resection with lobectomy or sublobar resection were abstracted. Univariate and multivariate analysis was performed for predictors of 30-DM. Factors examined included: age, sex, year of diagnosis, tumor size, and Charlson-Deyo (CD) morbidity score. Results: A total of 112,216 patients met the criteria. Of these 71,175 or 64% received surgery, and of this group 57,569 (81%) underwent lobectomy and 13,606 (19%) underwent sublobar resection. Pneumonectomy patients were excluded. The median age was 68 years (53% female). Median tumor size (Tsize) was 2.4cm. CD score was 0 in 49% of patients and 1 or higher in 51%. The overall rate of 30-DM was 2.2%. On univariate analysis, younger age, treatment at a research/academic institution, female sex, Tsize ≤ 3cm, and Charlson-Deyo (CD) score of 0 were protective against 30-day mortality, while treatment at a community cancer center predicted for worse 30-DM. Extent of surgery was not significant. On multivariate analysis, younger age, CD score of 0, female sex, Tsize ≤3 cm, and treatment at a comprehensive community cancer center or an academic/research center were protective against 30-DM (P value <.001 for all). While the overall 30-DM rate was low, an interesting picture emerged when age and comorbidity were examined together (Table). In patients younger than 75 years with medical comorbidities, the 30-DM was 1.8%. However, in elderly patients with comorbidities this rate nearly tripled to 4.6% (P<.01). Much of this difference was driven by elderly patients with comorbidities who underwent lobectomy, in whom the 30- DM climbed to 5.1% as opposed to 3.1% with sublobar resection (P<.01). Conclusions: The overall 30-DM rate following sublobar or lobar resection was quite low. In patients older than 75 years with medical comorbidities, however, this incidence nearly triples; suggesting improved perioperative care, consideration of more limited resection, or consideration of effective nonsurgical therapies may be indicated. (Table Presented).
ID  - 3166
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Comparison of long-term survival outcomes between stereotactic body radiation therapy and sublobar resection for stage I non-small cell lung cancer in high-risk operable patients: Propensity score-match analysis
AU  - Matsuo, Y.
AU  - Chen, F.
AU  - Hamaji, M.
AU  - Kawaguchi, A.
AU  - Ueki, N.
AU  - Nagata, Y.
AU  - Sonobe, M.
AU  - Morita, S.
AU  - Date, H.
AU  - Hiraoka, M.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Purpose/Objective(s): Stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) is being a standard treatment option for medically inoperable patients with stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). However, its role is still unclear in high-risk operable patients who cannot tolerate lobectomy. The purpose of this study is to perform a survival comparison between SBRT and sublobar resection (SLR). Materials/Methods: All patients who underwent SBRT or sublobar resection (SLR) because of medical comorbidity for clinical stage I NSCLC between January 2003 and December 2009 were retrospectively reviewed. Patients with tumor diameter >50 mm or those without histological confirmation were excluded. Propensity score matching (PSM) was performed based on age, sex, performance status (PS), tumor diameter, forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1), and Charlson comorbidity index (CCI). Results: One hundred fifteen patients of SBRT and 65 of SLR were eligible for this study. Median potential follow-up periods for SBRT and SLR were 6.7 and 5.3 years, respectively. Patients with SBRT were elderly, poorer in PS, lower in FEV1, higher in CCI, and larger in tumor diameter than those with SLR. Two patients were upstaged to pathological IIIA after SLR because of mediastinal node metastasis. No patients received adjuvant chemotherapy until disease progression. No treatment-related death was observed either in SBRT or in SLR. Before the PSM, 5-year overall survival (OS) was 40.3% and 60.5% for SBRT and SLR, respectively (P<.01). The PSM picked up 53 patients for each treatment group. After the PSM, difference in OS became insignificant (40.4% and 55.6% at 5 years for SBRT and SLR, respectively; P=.12). Furthermore, cumulative incidence of cause-specific death was similar in the 2 groups (35.5% and 30.3% at 5 years, P=.43). Although local recurrence tended to be higher in SBRT (28.4% and 14.1%, P=.06), regional or distant metastasis was not significantly different between the 2 treatment (14.3% and 9.2% for regional, P=.24; 35.9% and 36.1% for distant, P=.67). Conclusions: SBRT can be an alternative treatment to SLR for those who cannot tolerate lobectomy because of medical comorbidity.
ID  - 3167
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Adjuvant chemotherapy for elderly patients with stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) with tumors ≥ 4 cm: A SEER Medicare analysis
AU  - Malhotra, J.
AU  - Mhango, G.
AU  - Gomez, J.E.
AU  - Smith, C.B.
AU  - Galsky, M.D.
AU  - Strauss, G.M.
AU  - Wisnivesky, J.P.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Purpose/Objective(s): The role of adjuvant chemotherapy for early stage NSCLC stage I patients with tumors size ≥ 4 cm is not well established. Secondary analysis of CALGB 9633, the only randomized clinical trial designed for these cancers did show a potential benefit but these findings have not been validated in the elderly. Materials/Methods: We identified 2923 patients with stage I NSCLC who underwent lobectomy from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER)-Medicare linked database diagnosed from 1992 to 2009. Overall survival and rates of serious adverse events (defined as those requiring admission to hospital) were compared between patients treated with resection alone, platinum-based adjuvant chemotherapy, or adjuvant chemotherapy and radiation. Propensity scores for receiving each treatment were calculated using a logistic model that included socio-demographic characteristics, comorbidities, and cancer-related factors. Survival analyses were conducted with a Cox regression model using inverse probability weights based on the estimated propensity score. Results: Overall, 88.6% patients were treated with resection alone, 10.3% received platinum-based adjuvant chemotherapy, and 1.0% underwent chemotherapy and radiation. Adjusted analysis showed that adjuvant chemotherapy (hazard ratio [HR], 0.82; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.68-0.99) was associated with improved survival compared to resection alone. Conversely, adjuvant chemotherapy and radiation (HR 2.10; 95% CI, 1.40-3.17) were associated with worse outcomes. Patients receiving adjuvant chemotherapy had more serious adverse events compared to those treated with resection alone, with neutropenia being most significant (odds ratio, 11.3; 95% CI, 3.6-36.0). No significant difference was observed in rates of fever, anemia, thrombocytopenia, nausea, and acute renal failure. Conclusions: Platinum-based adjuvant chemotherapy is associated with reduced mortality and increased serious adverse events in elderly patients with stage I NSCLC and tumor size ≥ 4 cm. Our findings are similar to that observed in CALGB 9633, thereby supporting the consideration of adjuvant chemotherapy in this patient group.
ID  - 3168
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Induction of anti-tumor immunity by radio frequency ablation in non-small cell lung cancer: The role of dendritic cells
AU  - Schneider, T.
AU  - Ring, S.
AU  - Mahnke, K.
AU  - Heussel, C.
AU  - Dienemann, H.
AU  - Hoffmann, H.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Introduction: There is growing evidence that RFA-mediated necrosis can modulate host immune responses. In patients with early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCL) we applied radiofrequency ablation (RFA), followed by tumor resection. In this regimen RFA may give rise to necrotic tumor fragments in vivo, which may booster immunity by endogenous DC initiating an in-situ immunization against tumor antigenes. Methods: In a total of 4 patients with solitary pulmonary lesions CT guided biopsy proofed NSCLC and RFA was performed in the same intervention. Radical tumor resection was performed (by VATS-lobectomy, mediastinal and hilar lymph node dissection) 10 days subsequent to RFA. To investigate the role of DC, four blood samples were collected (before RFA, 3 days, 2, 4 weeks subsequent to RFA). DC populations were isolated by magnetic beads and analyzed by flow cytometry. To address the functional changes in DC, cocultures of allogeneic CD4+ T cell with DC were set up and the proliferation was measured 3 days thereafter. Finally we investigated the production of cytokines by DC. Results: The number of CD11c+CD14-CD19- DC significantly increased over the 4 week observation period after RFA. The further dissection of the DC into mDC and pDC subpopulations, respectively, showed that this increase was due to elevated numbers of BDCA-1+ /BDAC-2+ mDC. In contrast the number of BDCA2+ pDC remained unchanged. When analyzing the activation status of the DC it became apparent that after RFA expression of CD83 by DC was upregulated, indicating a maturation of DC. Moreover, the inhibitory molecules B7H2 and B7H3 were clearly down regulated after RFA and remained low, during the observation period. These data indicate that RFA treatment induces a rather mature immunostimulatory phenotype of DC. In the mixed leuocyte reactions we detected enhanced allostimulatory capacity of the DC after RFA, which was apparent during the observation period. When similar co-culture experiments were set up with syngeneic CD8+ T cells, increased proliferation subsequent to RFA was also apparent. Investigating the production of cytokines by DC elevated levels of IFN γ were detected in DC stimulated with TLR ligands such as LPS and PolyI:C. Conclusion: For the first time we could show that RFA of early stage NSCLC results in elevated numbers of activated DC in peripheral blood. Tumor ablation augments the anti-tumor immune responses in vivo and potentially may lead to an in-situ immunization against tumor antigens in this hybrid concept including RFA and subsequent surgical tumor resection.
ID  - 3169
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Outcomes following SBRT vs. limited resection for elderly patients with early stage lung cancer
AU  - Ezer, N.
AU  - Mhango, G.
AU  - Veluswamy, R.R.
AU  - Wisnivesky, J.P.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Background: The standard treatment of early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is lobectomy. However, a considerable number of older patients are not candidates for lobectomy due to comorbidities or limited lung function. While many of these patients are treated with limited resection (wedge resection or segmentectomy), stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT) has emerged as an alternative treatment option. However, there is limited data regarding the effectiveness of SBRT. Methods: We used the Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results registry linked to Medicare claims to compare survival of patients treated with SBRT vs. limited resection. We identified all patients >65years of age with histologically confirmed NSCLC ≤5 cm in size and with negative (N0) lymph nodes. We compared baseline characteristics in each treatment group using the chi-squared test and t-test, as appropriate. We used logistic regression to create a propensity score model predicting SBRT use. We compared survival of patients treated with limited resection vs. SBRT after adjusting for propensity scores. Secondary analyses were performed stratifying the sample by type of limited resection (wedge vs. segmentectomy), age (≤75 vs. >75 years), and tumor size (≥3cm vs. <3cm). Results: We identified 2,243 eligible patients of which 362 (16%) received SBRT. Patients treated with SBRT were older, more likely to be unmarried, to have a higher comorbidity score and smaller tumors (p <0.001 for all comparisons). Adjusted analyses showed no significant differences in survival (hazard ratio [HR]: 1.18; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.97-1.45) among patients treated with SBRT vs. limited resection. While survival of patients who underwent SBRT vs. wedge resection was not significantly different (HR: 1.17; 95% CI: 0.95-1.44), SBRT was associated with worse outcomes when compared to segmentectomy (HR: 1.34; 95% CI: 1.02-1.75). When analysis was restricted to patients ≤75 yrs (HR: 1.08; 95% CI: 0.76-1.53) or >75 yrs (HR: 1.22; 95% CI: 0.96-1.55), and size ≥3cm (HR: 1.20; 95% CI: 0.96-1.50) or <3cm (HR: 1.11; 95% CI: 0.72-1.71) survival following SBRT and limited resection were not significantly different. Conclusion: SBRT is associated with similar overall survival when compared to wedge resection but not to segmentectomy in elderly patients with NSCLC ≤5cm and no lymph node involvement. Our results suggest that SBRT may be considered as an alternative therapy to limited resection in older patients who are not candidates for lobectomy.
ID  - 3170
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Prognostic significance of visceral pleural involvement in early stage lung cancer
AU  - Lakha, S.
AU  - Gomez, J.E.
AU  - Flores, R.M.
AU  - Wisnivesky, J.P.
PY  - 2014
AB  - RATIONALE: Visceral pleural invasion (VPI) has been recognized as a negative prognostic factor in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). According to the most recent American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) Tumor, Node, and Metastasis (TNM) system, VPI leads to upstaging of cancers <3 cm but not of those 3-7 cm in size. However, previous studies have reached mixed conclusions as to whether VPI is an independent prognostic factor in early-stage lung cancer and whether its effect is size-dependent. METHODS: We used nationally-representative population-based data from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End-Results cancer registries. We identified 16,315 patients diagnosed with T1-T2 (i.e., <7 cm), N0, M0 NSCLC between 2004 and 2010 who underwent lobectomy. We compared lung cancer-specific (primary outcome) and overall (secondary outcome) survival in patients with and without VPI using the Kaplan-Meier method. A revised staging classification was derived based on the impact of VPI according to tumor size (<2 cm, 2-3 cm, 3-5 cm, and 5-7 cm). We used Cox regression analysis to assess the impact of VPI on survival after controlling for potential confounders; the c-statistic was used to compare the discrimination of the current AJCC staging system vs. the revised classification. RESULTS: Overall, 3,389 (21%) NSCLC patients had VPI. Unadjusted analyses showed that VPI was associated with significantly worse lung cancer-specific (p<0.0001) and overall (p<0.0001) survival. For each tumor size stratum, survival of patients with VPI more closely tracked that of patients without VPI in the immediately-larger size category (Figure 1). Adjusted analysis showed VPI to be an independent negative prognostic factor for lung cancer-specific (hazard ratio [HR]: 1.37, 95% CI: 1.28-1.46) and overall (HR: 1.23, 95% CI: 1.15-1.30) survival after controlling for potential confounders. A revised system was developed combining size and VPI, in which tumors with VPI were upstaged to the next T status. This revised system had improved discrimination compared with the current AJCC classification (c-statistic: 0.78 and 0.76, respectively). CONCLUSIONS: VPI is a negative prognostic factor in early-stage NSCLC, independent of tumor size. AJCC guidelines may be revised to better incorporate the impact of VPI on survival of patients with early stage NSCLC. Patients with VPI should be considered candidates for more aggressive management. (Figure Presented).
ID  - 3171
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Survival and treatment of non-small cell lung cancer stage I-II : Patient and tumor specific factors affect the prognosis
AU  - Mokhles, S.
AU  - Nuyttens, J.J.
AU  - Maat, A.P.W.M.
AU  - Birim, O.
AU  - Bogers, A.J.J.C.
AU  - Takkenberg, J.J.M.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Rationale The primary objective of this study was to define clinical baseline parameters that can predict survival of patients with stage I or II non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) who underwent surgery or stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT). Methods From January 2001 to January 2011, 425 patients (216 surgery, 209 SBRT) were identified with clinical stage I or II NSCLC. Cumulative survival was analyzed using the Kaplan-Meier method. The Cox proportional hazards regression analyses were used to investigate potential risk factors for mortality caused by tumor and patient specific factors. Results The median age of patients in the surgery and SBRT groups was 65 (range 39-83 year) and 74 (range 51-91) years, respectively. The median follow-up time for the whole group was 2.6 years (range 1-10 years). The types of surgical procedures performed were: wedge resection in 3% (n=4) of patients, lobectomy in 76% (n=163), bilobectomy in 7% (n=16), and pneumonectomy in 14% (n=30). The SBRT group consisted of patients with peripheral tumors. A larger proportion of the surgical group had a Charlson Comorbidity Index score of ≤1 compared to the SBRT group, 48% and 29%, respectively. In the surgery group 65% (n=140) of patients had stage I, 29% (n=62) stage II. In the SBRT group 88% (n=183) of patients had stage I, 12% (n=26) stage II. Median clinical tumor diameter in the surgery and SBRT group was 26 mm (range 5-136) and 30 mm (range 10-100), respectively. SBRT patients were older, had higher Charlson Comorbidity Index score, and lower forced expiratory volume in 1 second. Overall survival of the surgery group was 71% at 3 years and 60% at 5 years. For SBRT group this was 55% at 3 years and 34% at 5 years. We identified that older age, Charlson Comorbidity Index score 4, and clinical stage IIB were associated with higher long-term mortality in patients undergoing surgery. In the SBRT group we identified that Charlson Comorbidity Index score ≥5, and clinical stage >IA were associated with higher long-term mortality. Conclusions Both patient characteristics and survival of NSCLC I and II patients undergoing surgical treatment or SBRT differ considerably. Long-term survival of lung cancer patients can be determined by focusing on patient and tumor specific factors. In addition to TNM the consideration of patient age and Charlson Comorbidity Index may improve prognostication of patients with NSCLC, and assist in selecting an appropriate treatment strategy.
ID  - 3172
