TY  - JOUR
TI  - Central endobronchial stage I non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC) successfully treated with stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) in a non-surgical candidate: A case report
AU  - Cieciuch, A.
AU  - Raval, D.
AU  - Rafeq, S.
AU  - Hayes, G.
AU  - Sioshansi, S.
PY  - 2014
AB  - BACKGROUND: Surgical resection remains the first-line treatment for stage I NSCLC. Therapeutic options are limited for patients with severe lung disease who are deemed medically inoperable. SBRT is a technique utilizing highly conformal, ablative doses of radiation. Phase II trials have shown that SBRT is effective in achieving local control but is associated with toxicity when used to treat central tumors, presumably from injury to proximal airways.1,2 CASE REPORT: 62yo male former heavy smoker with severe COPD on supplemental oxygen was found to have a right upper lobe (RUL) apical segment tumor <1cm in size while undergoing bronchoscopic evaluation for tracheobronchomalacia (TBM) [Image A]. Biopsy revealed invasive squamous cell carcinoma. The tumor could not be visualized on CT or PET/CT. Clinical staging was assessed as T1N0M0. Due to his poor lung function, he was not a candidate for a right upper lobectomy. He opted to undergo SBRT rather than endobronchial brachytherapy. Two coiled metal intralesional fiducials (Visicoil™) were placed bronchoscopically in order to locate the tumor radiographically and guide therapy [Image B]. He received 50 Gy in five fractions over fifteen days and tolerated the therapy well without complications. He was followed bronchoscopically post-SBRT. Initially at 3 weeks, there was a small amount of residual abnormal mucosa in the RUL apical segment [Image C]. Endobronchial biopsy revealed atypical squamous metaplasia. At 15 weeks, scar tissue was present at the takeoff of the RUL apical segment, but there was otherwise no evidence of residual endobronchial tumor [Image D]. After achieving successful local control of his tumor, his respiratory status remains stable. DISCUSSION: This unique case illustrates successful treatment of purely endobronchial T1N0M0 NSCLC with SBRT in a patient with TBM. At four months of follow up, the patient's respiratory status remained stable despite having received high-dose radiation to his proximal bronchial tree. SBRT delivery and subsequent bronchoscopic surveillance were extremely challenging due to the tumor's small size. However, bronchoscopic placement of fiducials guided therapy without causing significant artifact. Using a protocol of 50 Gy in five fractions allowed for safe treatment of this small central tumor, with no noted toxicity at four months of follow up. As our three week bronchoscopic surveillance showed radiation effect, we suggest performing this study 12-16 weeks after completing therapy. (Figure Presented).
ID  - 3173
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Is there a need for fiducial markers in SBRT for lung cancer
AU  - Whitten, P.E.
AU  - Copelen, K.L.
AU  - Stoner, J.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Rationale: Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a treatment option for stage I non-small cell lung cancer in patients who are medically inoperable and/or who refuse to have surgery. According to the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Guidelines (2013), “SBRT has achieved primary tumor control rates and overall survival, comparable to lobectomy and higher than 3D-CRT in nonrandomized and population-based comparisons in medically inoperable or older patients.” SBRT can be achieved by using several methods: respiratory gating, body frame, or target tracking (fiducial markers or fiducial free markers). We report our center's SBRT cone beam CT and orthogonal XR for respiratory gating without fiducial markers and our SBRT toxicity outcomes. Methods: We report back only 9 months of data, annualized to a year-by-year average. January marked our institutions completion of a huge treatment machine upgrade; operating at 60-70% treatment capacity. Comparing 2011 and 2013, there has been a growth of over 20% in the numbers of patients receiving SBRT for lung cancer, due to changes in practice compared to earlier years. From January to September 2013, we have treated 35 patients with Lung SBRT. Two patients from this number received 2 courses of lung SBRT each (to separate lung lesions, not diagnosed at the same time). However annualized, this totals to 46.5 patients (average) per year. Results: To date, the 35 patients (2013) receiving SBRT for lung cancer have not re-presented with excessive early pulmonary toxicity. Conclusion: Fiducials in the lung is a topic that is being explored throughout Radiation Oncology practice. Radiation Oncologists are trying to reach a consensus, whether select patients might benefit from this, with the addition of triggered imaging and arc therapy. 3The necessity of FMs, given the frail patient population, need for general anesthetic, cost, 10% miss rate, and small pneumothorax causes the potential of unnecessary risk. The importance of these risks is magnified because stereotactic treatments are being delivered routinely without FMs on linear accelerators with outstanding oncologic outcomes and very favorable toxicity. Our physicians would agree that the fiducial techniques (even should they be supported in future publications) would not be appropriate for all patients, or all SBRT lung patients. Our center has excellent local tumor control rates with low toxicity with SBRT without the use of fiducial markers. However, fiducial applications in the lung, are under consideration for wider use in the future. (Figure Presented).
