TI  - The impact of histologic subtypes on outcomes following limited resection for early-stage non-small cell lung cancer
AU  - Veluswamy, R.R.
AU  - Ezer, N.
AU  - Mhango, G.
AU  - Beasley, M.B.
AU  - Neugut, A.I.
AU  - Hershman, D.L.
AU  - Wisnivesky, J.P.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Rationale: Limited resection is often used for early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients unable to tolerate lobectomy. Prior studies suggest similar survival rates following limited resection vs. lobectomy for stage I NSCLC ≤2 cm in size. However, the impact of histologic type on outcomes following limited resection is unknown. Methods: We used population-based data from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) registry to identify patients with early-stage NSCLC (T1, N0, M0) ≤2 cm treated with limited resection (wedge resection or segmentectomy) or lobectomy. We grouped these patients based on tumor histology into: adenocarcinoma in situ/minimally invasive adenocarcinoma (AIS/MIA; formerly BAC), invasive adenocarcinoma, or squamous cell carcinoma. We used logistic regression to determine propensity scores for type of resection based on patient's baseline characteristics. Survival of patients treated with limited resection vs. lobectomy was compared within each histological group using Cox regression adjusting for propensity scores. We conducted secondary analysis stratifying the sample by age (≥65 vs. <65 years old) and by type of limited resection (wedge resection vs. segmentectomy). Results: Of the 10,878 patient in the study, 21% underwent limited resection. For AIS/MIA, adjusted analysis showed similar survival (hazard ratio [HR]: 1.15; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.83-1.58) for patients treated with limited resection vs. lobectomy. While limited resection was associated with worse survival (HR: 1.83, 95% CI: 1.04-3.20) for AIS/MIA patients <65 years old, outcomes were similar (HR: 0.95, 95% CI: 0.64-1.39) among patients ≥65 years. Limited resection was associated with worse survival (HR: 1.41, 95% CI: 1.22-1.63) for patients with invasive adenocarcinoma. When stratifying by age, both patients <65 years old (HR: 1.62, 95% CI: 1.23-2.13) and ≥65 years old (HR: 1.35, 95% CI: 1.14-1.59) treated with limited resection had worse survival. Survival was not significantly different (HR: 1.19; 95% CI: 0.99-1.43) for patients with squamous cell carcinoma treated with limited resection vs. lobectomy. Similar results were obtained in analyses stratifying by age. While patients treated with segmentectomy vs. lobectomy had similar survival regardless of histologic type, wedge resection was associated with equivalent survival in AIS/MIA patients but not for those with invasive adenocarcinoma or squamous cell carcinoma. Conclusions: Limited resection is associated with similar survival compared to lobectomy in elderly patients with early-stage AIS/MIA or squamous cell carcinoma. While segmentectomy may be an adequate alternative to lobectomy for NSCLC <2 cm in size, wedge resection is associated with suboptimal outcomes for patients with invasive cell types.
