TY  - JOUR
TI  - The utilization of video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) versus open thoracotomy for stage 1 and stage 2 non-small cell lung cancer in canadian hospitals: A budget impact analysis
AU  - Ondrejicka, D.A.
AU  - Goldstein, L.J.
PY  - 2014
DO  - 10.1016/j.jval.2014.03.430
AB  - Objectives: Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer related death in Canada. Lobectomy is the most common form of treatment for early stage lung cancer and can be performed using an open approach with a thoracotomy incision or as a minimally-invasive procedure using Video-Assisted Thoracic Surgery (VATS). Several recent studies have demonstrated that open and VATS lobectomies achieve onocologically equivalent outcomes, which has lead to rising popularity for VATS. In the Canadian health care environment hospitals are faced with increasingly restrictive budgets, creating a critical need to demonstrate the cost-effectiveness of procedures performed. This study was conducted to determine the budget impact of increasing the proportion of VATS vs. open lobectomies in a Canadian hospital. Methods: We examined the budget impact of increasing the proportion of VATS cases from 25% to 75%, while decreasing the proportion of open cases by the same amount in a hospital that performs 150 lobectomies annually. The model incorporates the costs associated with surgery, length of stay (taking into account facility and staff costs) and common postoperative complications. The cost data used in the model was obtained from peer reviewed literature, the Ontario Case Costing Initiative and case costing from a large Canadian hospital. Data on patient outcomes was obtained from peer reviewed literature. A multivariate sensitivity analysis using a Monte Carlo simulation was completed to ensure scientific rigour. Results: VATS lobectomies are associated with higher procedural costs, but this is offset by a shorter length of stay and a lower postoperative complication rate. The model establishes that for a Canadian hospital performing 150 lobectomies increasing the proportion of VATS cases from 25% to 75% allows for a potential cost savings of CAD $226,066.01 annually. Conclusions: In a Canadian hospital, VATS lobectomy is a more cost-effective procedure than open lobectomy for early stage lung cancer.
ID  - 3200
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Operable lesions: More activities towards new concepts?
AU  - Lagerwaard, F.J.
PY  - 2014
AB  - Stereotactic ablative radiotherapy (SABR) has become an established modality for the curative treatment of early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) in patients who are unfit for surgery. Matured SABR data have confirmed the early reports of high local control with low rates of severe toxicity, while preserving quality of life and pulmonary function. These outcomes have prompted the question whether SABR can also be regarded as an alternative to surgery in operable patients. Onishi et al. described the results of SABR in 87 Stage I NSCLC patients who were medically operable but refused surgery in a multi-institutional setting, reporting a 5-year local control and overall survival rate of 87% and 70%, respectively. A subsequent Amsterdam study reported similar findings in 177 operable patients with local control and survival rates of 93% and 85% at 3-year follow-up. In view of the observation that SABR cohorts generally consist of elderly patients with considerable co-morbidity, these results appear to be similar to those of historical surgical series. However, as only randomized trials can answer the question whether equivalence exists between surgery and SABR, several prospective studies have been initiated in recent years. A lack of patients recruitment, which appears to be prominently due to doctors treatment preference, has led to premature closure of all initiated trials. Therefore, only historical comparisons and data such as from matched pair analyses can be used to determine equipoise between both treatment modalities. A recently published propensity score-matched pair analysis [Verstegen 2013] comparing SABR and video-assisted thoracosopic surgery showed similar overall survival and a slightly better local control for the SABR cohort, although only 64 patients were available in each arm. In order to make an appropriate choice between SABR or surgery for any patient, several parameters need to be considered, and discussion within a multi-disciplinary tumor board meeting is essential. Age and comorbidity are major determinants, because in a recent Dutch national review [unpublished data] the 30-day mortality after surgery for stage I NSCLC increased from 0.6% in patients less than 60 years of age to 3.8% in patients over 80 years of age. Patients need to be optimally staged, including at least FDG-PET-CT scans, and preferably in operable patients some form of minimallyinvasive mediastinal staging through mediastinoscopy, EUS and/or EBUS [Silvestri 2013; de Leyn 2013]. A number of local and international trials evaluation these minimally-invasive staging procedures as a routine procedure in combination with SABR for operable patients are being initiated, among others in the Netherlands. Finally, it is important to realize that salvage surgery remains an option in cases where an isolated local recurrence is diagnosed following SABR. A number of small recent studies have shown that salvage lobectomy is safe and feasible if performed in a timely manner, even in patients initially considered to be medically inoperable.
ID  - 3201
