TI  - Minimally invasive photothermal ablation of lung cancer by low power near-infrared laser and topical injection of indocyanine green
AU  - Hirohashi, K.
AU  - Anayama, T.
AU  - Wada, H.
AU  - Nakajima, T.
AU  - Keshavjee, S.
AU  - Orihashi, K.
AU  - Yasufuku, K.
PY  - 2013
AB  - Rationale Anatomical lobar resection with systematic lymph node dissection is the standard treatment for early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). However, minimally invasive tumor ablation can be an alternative therapy for patients who are unable to tolerate pulmonary resection due to medical comorbidities. We introduce a new minimally invasive photothermal ablation of lung cancer using a combination of indocyanine green (ICG) and low power near-infrared (NIR) laser. Methods [In-vitro study 1]: The 808nm laser with 250mW output was irradiated using different dilutions of ICG and the temporal thermal effect was monitored. [In-vitro study 2]: The cytotoxic effect of hyperthermia on human lung cancer was examined in immortalized human lung cancer cell lines in different temperature and time settings. Cell viability was quantified by MTS assay. [in-vivo study]: Nude mouse subcutaneous human lung cancer Xenograft models were used for the study. After the tumor diameter reached 5mm, 50micro-litres of 0.5g/L of ICG was injected topically into the tumor. The different powers of 808nm laser were irradiated to the tumors for 10 minutes. After treatment, the tumor size was measured daily until the major axis of the tumors in the control group reached 10mm. Finally, all tumors were histologically examined by both H.E. and NADH enzyme staining. Results [in-vitro study 1]: 100mcl of 0.5g/L ICG was heated more than 30' for 6minutes of 250mW power of 808nm laser. Normal saline was used as a control and the temperature did not change significantly in the same setting. [in-vitro study 2]: The temperature dependent and the time dependent cytotoxic effect of thermal therapy was clarified. The viability of cancer cells was completely lost with 59' 5minutes of heating and the cancer cells were immediately lost after 5 minutes of heating compared to using 57' heat, which took 20 minutes to exhibit the complete cytotoxic effect. [in-vivo study] Based on the results of the in-vitro study, the default power setting was set at 300mW. The combination of topical ICG injection and laser irradiation exhibited significant tumor growth suppression. Conclusions Photothermal ablation therapy by combination of low power NIR laser and topical injection of ICG exhibited photothermal ablation effect. Since the low power laser can be irradiated using a thin laser fiber built into the accessory channel of the flexible bronchoscope, the present therapeutic concept may be applied to bronchoscopic minimally invasive photothermal ablation therapy for NSCLC.
ID  - 3253
