AU  - Krievins, D.
PY  - 2013
AB  - Tumor malignancy often correlates with angiogenic activity which can be assessed in order to help to predict tumor behavior. One of key players in tumor angiogenesis is the CXC chemokine network. CXCL4 is a CXC group chemokine with angiostatic properties. Previous studies revealed lower levels of CXCL4 in the microenviroment of tumor compared with systemic circulation in early stage NSCLC patients (Spaks et al., 2012). Molecular mechanisms of this phenomenon are still not clear. Affinity of CXCL4 to sulphated glycosaminoglycans and glycoproteins of vascular endothelium could be the cause of a decrease in the level of CXCL4 in plasma passed through tumor vascular bed resulting in a more prominent CXCL4 plasma level drop in highly vascularized tumors. The aim of the study was to determine CXCL4 plasma levels and tumor vascularity in early stage NSCLC patients. The study involved 20 patients with early stage NSCLC (squamous cell carcinoma (n = 12) and adenocarcinoma (n = 8), TNM stage IA IIB) who underwent radical pulmonary resection. CXCL4 levels were determined by ELISA in paired blood samples [peripheral venous blood (PVB) samples and pulmonary venous blood draining tumor (PVBDT) samples collected during surgery]. Vascular count and CD34 expression were assessed immunohistochemically in tumor tissue samples to evaluate the process of angiogenesis. Mean CXCL4 level in PVB samples was 13 pg/ml and 10.8 pg/ml in PVBDT samples. Mean decrease in CXCL4 level in plasma after passing through tumor vascular bed was 11.2% (p < 0.05). Mean vascular count was 14 blood vessels per mm2. Positive correlation has been found between vascular count and CXCL4 levels in PVB samples (r = 0.65, p < 0.01) and PVBDT samples (r = 0.67; p < 0.01). Plasma CXCL4 levels were found to depend on development of the tumor vascular bed in early stage lung cancer and can be used for noninvasive assessment of tumor angiogenic activity. CXCL4 plasma level drop in blood after passing the tumor vascular bed cannot be explained by nonspecific CXCL4 binding.
