AU  - Moon, Y.K.
AU  - Sung, S.W.
AU  - Park, H.J.
AU  - Wang, Y.P.
AU  - Park, J.K.
PY  - 2013
AB  - Background: Lobectomy and mediastinal lymph node dissection has been the standard treatment in non small cell lung cancer. Recent studies demonstrated limited resection is not inferior to lobectomy in the peripheral early lung cancer with ground opacity dominant lesion. However, the effect of limited resection for the soild type early lung cancers is controversial. The aim of this study was to compare clinical outcomes between limited resection and lobectomy in solid type early stage non small cell lung cancer. Methods: A retrospective review was performed for the patients undergoing lobectomy and limited resection in clinical stage IA non small cell lung cancer for curative intent. The patients with pure ground glass opacity, dominant ground glass opacity lesion and within 30 days death after operation were excluded. Total 164 patients between March 2000 and September 2010 were reviewed in this study. Clinical outcomes including disease free interval, survival and prognostic factors were analyzed. Results: 31 patients underwent limited resection and 133 patients underwent lobectomy. There were significant differences between two groups in age (p < 0.001), cardiovascular disease (p = 0.001) and DLCO (p < 0.001). The mean tumor size was 2.19 cm in the lobectomy group and 1.99 cm in the limited resection group (p = 0.239). The lobectomy group showed a significant improved disease free interval (p < 0.001) and survival (p = 0.001) compared to the limited resection group. In multivariate analysis, Limited resection (p = 0.011), lymphatic vessel invasion (p = 0.006) and number of positive lymph nodes (p = 0.028) were prognostic factors for survival. Limited resection (p < 0.001), visceral pleural invasion (p = 0.002) and lymphatic vessel invasion (p < 0.001) were shown to be related to the poor disease free interval. Conclusions: Limited resection had poor prognosis in solid type clinical stage IA lung cancer. Prognostic factors as lymph node metastasis and lymphatic vessel invasion are associated with clinical outcomes despite of small sized solid type lung cancer. Lobectomy remains standard surgical procedure in solid type clinical stage IA lung cancer.
