AB  - Objectives: Screening studies and new imaging technologies have allowed an increase in the number of diagnosed cases of early stage non-small-cell lung cancers (NSCLC). A minimally invasive robot-assisted approach in major pulmonary surgery is increasingly frequent. This study aims to compare robotic to open thoracotomy lymphadenectomy in stage I NSCLC patients. Methods: From 2007 to 2011 we retrospectively selected c stage I NSCLC patients, who had undergone major lung resections by thoracotomy (group A) and robot-assisted surgery (group B). In both groups age, sex and location of the lesion were homogeneous. For each group, histology, overall number of lymph nodes, nodal stations dissected, sex and location of the lesion were statistically analysed using the software STATISTICA (P-value). Results: Three-hundred and two c stage I patients (224 males, 78 females, mean age 67 years) underwent major lung resections: 203 by thoracotomy (group A), 99 by robot-assisted surgery (group B). Histology was: adenocarcinoma (177 cases), squamous (103), large cell (9), anaplastic (6), sarcomatoid (5), adenosquamous (2). The number of removed lymph nodes was 15.67 (range 4-35) in group A and 14.89 in group B (range 4-27). No statistically significant difference (P=0.316) was observed between them. The overall number of nodal stations examined was slightly but statistically significantly higher in group B (P=0.023): 3.53 in group A (range 1-8) and 4.61 (range 2-8) in group B. Conclusions: For the overall number of lymph nodes, there is no statistically significant difference between thoracotomy and robot-assisted surgery. However, the overall number of nodal stations examined appears to be slightly but significantly higher in the robot-assisted group.
