AU  - Wisnivesky, J.P.
AU  - Mhango, G.
AU  - Carlson, M.
AU  - Herschman, D.
AU  - Neugut, A.
PY  - 2012
AB  - Background: In the past decade, video assisted thoracoscopy surgery (VATS) has been performed with increasing frequency to treat lung cancer patients. However, there is limited data regarding the perioperative and long-term outcomes of stage I-II non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients treated with VATS lobectomy vs. open resection. Methods: Using data from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results database linked to Medicare claims we identified all patients >65 years of age with primary stage I-II NSCLC diagnosed between 1994 and 2005. We compared the perioperative outcomes (surgical complications, extended length of stay [>15 days], readmission within 30 days of surgery, and perioperative mortality) and survival of NSCLC patients treated with VATS vs. open lobectomy. Results: The study included 7,500 patients who underwent lobectomy for stage I-II NSCLC. Of these, 433 (6%) underwent a VATS procedure. Patients who underwent VATS were older (p<0.0001), more likely to be female (p<0.0001), White (p=0.006), unmarried (p=0.0005), have a lower income (p<0.0001), or a have a tumor with squamous histology (p<0.0001). The distribution of comorbidities (p=0.12) and stage (p=0.38) was not significantly different among groups. Stage I patients who underwent VATS had significantly lower rates of extrapulmonary infections (p<0.005) and respiratory complications (p=0.04). Rates of cardiovascular complications (p=0.37), thromboembolic disease (p=0.26), and transfusion requirements (p=0.33) were similar across the two treatment groups. The number of patients with a prolonged length of stay were significantly higher among patients undergoing open lobectomy (14.2% vs. 6.9%; p<0.0001). There were no differences in the rates of readmissions (p=0.28) or need for reoperation within 30 days (p=0.31). Perioperative mortality was significantly lower among patients undergoing VATS (p=0.04). Similar results were obtained in analyses restricted to patients with stage II disease. Overall adjusted survival of stage I patients (hazard ratio [HR]: 0.88; 95% CI: 0.75-1.03) and stage II patients (HR: 0.93; 95% CI: 0.66-1.30) undergoing VATS and open lobectomy was equivalent. Conclusions: These results suggests that VATS lobectomy is associated with better perioperative outcomes, reduced postoperative mortality, and equivalent long-term outcomes among elderly patients with stage I-II NSCLC. Although these findings should be confirmed in prospective trials, our results suggest that VATS lobectomy may be an effective surgical procedure for these patients.
