TI  - Retrospective audit of radically treated non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients at the St James's Institute of Oncology (SJIO), Leeds Teaching Hospital NHS Trust (LTHT)
AU  - Franks, K.
AU  - Rees, A.
AU  - Fleming, C.
AU  - Trigwell, K.
AU  - Snee, M.
PY  - 2012
DO  - 10.1016/S0169-5002(12)70091-0
AB  - Introduction: Compared to other European Countries outcomes for NSCLC in the UK are poorer. We performed a retrospective audit of patients treated with radical intent at SJIO to establish our outcomes. Method: Patients within the LTHT treated with radical surgery or radiotherapy (RT) between 2007 2009 were analysed. A retrospective review of our in-house patients electronic notes system (patient pathway manager) was used to review patient demographics, staging, investigations and clinic letters. Results: 188 patients were analysed. The median age at diagnosis was 70 years (SD±11) of which 44% were female and 66% were male. The stage distribution was; 1A (24.5%), 1B (31.4%), 2A (20.2%), 2B (7.4%), 3A (9.6%) and 3B (6.9%). 86.1% received surgery as their primary treatment and 17.6% received radical RT. Surgery was lobectomy (60.7%), pneumonectomy (20.3%) wedge resection (10.5%) and bilobectomy (8.5%). 3 year OS was 51% for all patients (median OS 37.1 months), with median 46.0 months for surgery and 33.0 for RT. Median OS by stage was 49 (1), 46.0 (2) and 14.0 (3) months. 65.4% patients were recurrence free at the time of analysis with those patients relapsing locally (12.2%), distantly (10%) and both (5.9%). RFS for all patients was 62% at 3 years with a median 46.0 months (surgery 46 months and RT 33 months). Brain RFS was 85% at 3 years with metastases occurring in 4% of stage I, 11.5% of stage II and 12.9% of stage III patients. Conclusions: Our results agree with published series. Outcomes from RT appear inferior for early stage and better for advanced disease. The rates of RT appear low and further analysis of all patients treated within YCN is ongoing. This will form a baseline to assess the impact of the introduction of SBRT for stage I NSCLC and chemo-radiotherapy in stage III during the 2009-2011 period.
