AU  - Margolis, M.
AU  - Strother, E.
AU  - Tempesta, B.
PY  - 2011
DO  - 10.1002/rcs.429
AB  - Introduction: Anatomic segmentectomy has been advocated as a curative procedure in selected patients with non-small cell lung cancer. We investigated the feasibility and suitability of robotic video assisted thoracoscopic segmentectomy and complete mediastinal exeneration as a less invasive alternative to the open procedure. Robotic anatomic segmentectomy and complete mediastinal exeneration was performed in patients with impaired lung function and small periphery located tumors. Methods: We performed a retrospective review of 48 patients who underwent robotic video assisted thoracic surgery segmentectomy for early stage lung cancer in a 60 month period at our institution. All patients underwent robotic dissection of the artery, vein, and the bronchus with division of the respective segmental structures. In addition a complete mediastinal nodal exeneration was performed with removal of subcarinal, paraesophageal, paratracheal, and inferior pulmonary ligament lymph nodes. Inclusion criteria was a preoperative clinical T1 disease in a patient with impaired pulmonary function (FEV1 < 800 or DLCO < 50). Results: There were 26 men, 22 women, mean age 74 years. All patients underwent R0 resection. Operating room time was 3 hours, no statistical difference between robotic segmentectomy and robotic lobectomy. Median hospital length of stay was 6 days. All patients underwent segmentectomy for early stage lung cancer. Histology was squamous cell (11 patients), adenocarcinoma (20 pts), adenosquamous (9), basaloid (1), giant cell (2), and bronchoalveolar (5). Tumor size 2.0 cm or less was 25 patients, 23 were > 2.0 cm. Complications included atrial fibrillation, atelectasis requiring bronchoscopy, and CDoff infection. There were no mortalities. Conclusions: Robotic video assisted segmentectomy is a safe procedure which may represent a less invasive oncologic procedure to patients with small lung cancers and limited cardiopulmonary reserve.
