AB  - The 2009/2010 UK national audit has updated targets for lung cancer management [1]. The corresponding performance of Irish National Cancer Control Program (NCCP) sites is unreported. All consecutive cases of suspected lung cancer discussed at Saint Vincent's Hospital Group (SVHG) multidisciplinary team (MDT) meeting from 2/8/2009 to 2/2/2010 were examined and performance vetted against comparable UK averages. Over the 6 months, 121 cases were discussed, and a convenience sample of 62 cases (51.2% of SVHG MDT cases) was analysed. 13 cases were benign/non-lung primaries, leaving 49 primary lung cancer cases (extrapolatable to 191 incident primary lung cancers in SVHG/year, though more could escape MDT documentation). 14 (29%) were early stage IA-IIB, 12 (24%) were stage IIIA-B, 19 (39%) were stage IV, and 4 were small cell (8%). Surgical resection was undertaken in 17 (35%); (UK 14%). There were 13 lobectomies (76%) and 3 pneumonectomies (18%). There was one open-and-close thoracotomy (6%); (UK 5%). Thirty-day mortality for lobectomy and pneumonectomy were both 0% (UK 2.3, 5.8% respectively). Active cancer treatment was administered in 45 cases (92%); (UK 60%). Chemotherapy rate for performance status 0-1/stage IIIB-IV NSCLC lung cancer was 64%; (UK 55%). SVHG lung cancer service compares well against international standards.
