AB  - Purpose/Objective(s): Accurate staging of mediastinal lymph nodes is a critical factor determining the application of stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) for patients with early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). In this multicenter study the accuracy of 18F-FDG PET/CT to detect mediastinal lymph nodes (MLN) metastases was evaluated for clinical T1-2 NSCLC. Materials/Methods: The data of patients with T1-2 NSCLC who received preoperative 18F-FDG PET/CT staging and radical surgery were retrospectively reviewed in three centers from Oct. 2004 to Jan. 2009. The MLN metastasis was confirmed histopathologically after lobectomy/thoracotomy and lymph node dissection. The specificity, accuracy and NPV were calculated for PET staging. Clinical and pathological factors were analyzed for the correlation between PET findings and pathological MLN metastases. Results: One hundred and sixteen patients were enrolled. The specificity, accuracy and NPV for MLN metastasis on PET were 89.2%, 81.9% and 90.1% respectively. There were 101 patients with clinical T1-2N0 NSCLC. In these patients, the NPV for MLN metastasis with FDG PETwere 90.1% (91 of 101). Only 3 patients were found MLN metastasis in 49 patients with primary tumor FDG PET SUV<6.45, whereas 7 patients had MLN metastases in 52 patients with SUV.6.45 (P = 0.320). There were 4 and 6 cases to be found MLN metastasis with central (n = 41) and peripheral (n = 60) NSCLC (P = 1.000), respectively. And 8 and 2 patients were found MLN metastasis in T1 (n = 78) and T2 (n = 23) patients (P = 1.000), respectively. Conclusions: FDG PET/CTwas specific in N staging for T1-2 NSCLC. However, the NPV was about 90% in clinical N0 patients, suggesting that improvement of accurate N staging is warranted by incorporating PET with other minimal invasive approaches, including EBUS and EUS.
