TY  - JOUR
TI  - Is lobectomy for primary lung tumour in octogenarians worth the risk? A case control study
AU  - Scarci, M.A.
AU  - Attaran, S.
AU  - Haywood, L.
AU  - Lang-Lazdunski, L.
AU  - King, J.
AU  - Routledge, T.
AU  - Harrison-Phipps, K.
AU  - Pilling, J.
PY  - 2011
AB  - Background: For early stage non small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) surgery is the treatment of choice. The aging population will lead to increasing requests for surgical intervention in octogenarians. There are some reservations that this may be excessively dangerous for the survival gained. Methods: We reviewed 41 consecutive octogenarians [24 male, median age 82 years (range 80 - 88) who underwent lobectomy via thoracotomy (36) or VATS (5) for suspected NSCLC over 48 months. These were compared to 41 younger controls [median age 65 years (range 34 -78) matched for incision, lobe resected, histology, TNM stage and sex. Results: Comparison between octogenarians and Controls (Table Presented) Octogenarians made up 7.5% of the 545 patients who underwent lobectomy for suspected NSCLC during the study. In the first three years of the study octogenarians made up 6.1%; 5.6% and 2.5% of lobectomies each year whereas in the final year they comprised 14.6% (p=0.004 Fisher's). Co morbidities were commoner in the control group 58% [11 cardiovascular (CV) and 13 non-CV compared to 27% in the octogenarian group [8 CV; 3 non-CV p=0.007. There were no in hospital deaths in either group. Lung function was significantly poorer in the control group (table). Twelve (29%) octogenarians had post operative complications, six were admitted to intensive care (2 unplanned) compared to 16 (39%) (p=0.4) controls who suffered complications, four of which were admitted to intensive care (3 unplanned). Hospital stay was similar in the two groups (table) In the octogenarian group histology revealed two typical carcinoid tumours; 14 adenocarcinomas; 24 squamous cell carcinomas and one large cell carcinoma. Pathological staging of the NSCLC was IA 12, IB 17, IIA 1, IIB 6, IIIA 2, IIIB 1. Over a median follow up of 637 days (range 22- 1720) seven octogenarians have died; the controls have been followed for a median 1023 days (range 170 - 1722) with nine deaths. Log rank test, Kaplan Meier method shows no significant difference between the two groups (p=0.89). Conclusion: Lobectomy for primary lung tumour in octogenarians is feasible with similar morbidity and mortality to younger patients whom undergo surgery despite a greater number of co-morbidities. The post operative survival of octogenarians justifies an aggressive surgical approach.
