AU  - Tada, H.
AU  - Asamura, H.
AU  - Debruyne, C.
PY  - 2011
DO  - 10.1016/S0169-5002(11)70209-4
AB  - Introduction: Surgery is currently the standard treatment in early stage Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC). Lobectomy or greater resection (sleeve-lobectomy rather than pneumonectomy whenever possible) is the recommended surgical approach in patients (pts) with stage I IIIA NSCLC associated with radical or complete lymph node dissection (LND) or lymph node sampling (LNS) (Robinson et al., 2007; Scott et al., 2007; De Leyn et al., 2007). However, the extent of lymphadenectomy is still controversial in scientific literature, and currently no consensus has been reached on the more beneficial approach (Zhong et al., 2008). We report the surgical approach taken for pts in stage IB-IIIA NSCLC treated as part of standard of care in a global study evaluating investigational MAGE-A3 ASCI or placebo after complete tumor resection (Phase III trial, MAGRIT, NCT00480025). Methods: Disease's and pts' characteristics were collected and analyzed by descriptive statistics for all 1196 treated pts coming from more than 350 different centers worldwide as of 13 AUG 2010. Results: 62% of the pts come from Europe, 20% East Asia, 14% North America and 4% from the rest of the world.16% of the pts had pneumonectomy whereas 84% had some form of lobectomy as a minimum intervention. Similar percentages were observed in the different regions. Regarding the lymphadenectomy, globally LND has been performed in 53% of pts. This proportion is similar in all Regions (64% Europe, 55% East Asia and 48% rest of the world), except North America in which LNS is the preferred option (only 5% LND). Conclusions: Lobectomy (including bi and sleeve-lobectomy) is the most often used surgical approach for complete tumor resection in pts currently treated in the Phase III MAGRIT trial in agreement with scientific literature recommendations. Interestingly, we observed that nearly all pts from North America had LNS whereas in other regions LND is more represented. To ensure balance among the treatment groups, the different approaches of lymphadenectomy are one of the stratification factors in the study.
