TI  - Surgical management and outcomes of elderly patients with early stage of non-small cell lung cancer - A nested case-control study
AU  - Rivera, C.
AU  - Falcoz, P.E.
AU  - Bernard, A.
AU  - Dahan, M.
PY  - 2010
DO  - 10.1016/S0748-7983(10)00156-3
AB  - Background: The number of oncogeriatric patients with lung cancer is expected to increase in the next decades. The purpose of this study was to assess the modalities of the surgical treatment and post-operative outcomes of elderly patients with early stage of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), matching them to younger patients with similar characteristics. Materials and Methods: A nested case-control study over a 5-year period using EPITHOR®, the French Society of Thoracic and Cardiovascular Surgery database, created in 2002 and including more than 131 000 procedures from 92 institutions. We prospectively collected the data concerning 1969 patients 70 years or older with NSCLC stage I or II, from January 2004 to December 2008 and we matched them 1:1 to 1969 controls (<70 years old) for gender, American Society of Anesthesia (ASA) score, Performance Status (PS), Forced Expiratory Volume (FEV). The patients of the control group were randomized within 6310 patients with NSCLC stage I and II of the EPI-THOR® cohort. Lung cancer surgical treatment, hospital length of stay, postoperative morbidity and mortality rates were compared between groups. Results: The absence of radical lymphadenectomy was more frequent in patients 70 years or older (14%, n=269) than in younger patients (9%, n=170) (p<0.0001). There was no significant difference in video-assisted thoracic surgery modality (p=0.606) or in type of resection [pneumonectomy 8% (n=164) for 70 years or older vs 11%(n=216) for younger patients, lobectomy 79% (n=1559) vs 77% (n=1521), bilobectomy 4% (n=88) versus 5% (n=97), sub-lobar resection 7% (n=143) vs 6% (n=118) (p=0.076)]. Hospital length of stay was moderately longer for the elderly (13.0 days, CI[12.6; 13.4], vs 12.3 days, CI[11.9;12.7] for patients under 70 years, p=0.025). Difference in number of post-operative complications was not significant (p=0.073). Postoperative mortality rate was higher in elderly patients at every end point [30-days mortality: 3.56% n=70, vs 2.18% n=43 (p=0.010), 60-days mortality: 4.06% n=80 vs 2.39% n=47 (p=0.003), 90-days mortality: 4.72% n=93 vs 2.54% n=50 (p=0.0002)]. Conclusions: Elderly patients with NSCLC should not be denied pulmonary resection based on chronological age. Specific guidelines could be proposed for this population to improve post-operative mortality. Effective management of cancer in older patients is individualization of treatment with geriatric assessment.
