TY  - JOUR
TI  - Thoracoscopy vs Thoracotomy: Morbidity and mortality after lobectomy in patients with non-small cell lung carcinoma
AU  - Papiashvilli, M.
AU  - Bar, I.
PY  - 2010
DO  - 10.1097/IMI.0b013e3181e17b26
AB  - Objective: Until the last decade, lobectomy via open thoracotomy was the gold standard for patients with T1 lung carcinoma. Today, more and more thoracic surgeons perform less invasive techniques for early stage lung cancer. Our study analyzed morbidity and mortality after lobectomy to compare the thoracoscopic approach and thoracotomy in patients with non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC). Methods: Between 1998 and 2007 (a 10-year-period), we performed 331 lobectomies via open thoracotomy in patients with NSCLC. Since 2007, we generally perform thoracoscopic lobectomies and 62 cases of NSCLC patients have been operated on in our Department using this method. A comparison of postoperative complications, hospital stay, morbidity and mortality in these two groups of patients was the subject of our study. Results: During the 10-year period 1998 to 2007, 331 patients (mean 65.7 years) underwent surgery. Hospital stay (mean) was 10.8 days. Postoperative complications were: AF-62 (18.7%); lobar atelectasis-41 (12.4%); pneumonia- 30 (9.1%); prolonged air leak-28 (8.4%); empyema-19 (5.7%); mechanical ventilation-16 (4.9%); tracheostomy-14 (4.2%); sepsis-13 (3.9%); DVT-13 (3.9%); rethoracotomy-6 (1.8%); MI-5 1.5%); acute respiratory failure-3 (0.9%); CVA-10 (3.0%); paralytic ileus-4 (1.3%); bronchopleural fistula-2 (0.7%); pulmonary embolism-3 (0.9%); ischemic emboli-3 (0.9%); wound infection-2 (0.7%). Intraoperative bleeding occurred in 3 (0.9%) patients; intra-hospital mortality in 9 (2.7%); 6-month mortality in 10 (3.0%); and pulmonary insufficiency in 23 patients (6.9%). In 207 patients (62.5%), there were no postoperative complications. From 2007 until 2009 (2-year period), we performed 62 VATS lobectomies in NSCLC patients (mean 66.3 years). Three operations were converted to anterior mini-thoracotomy (bleeding-2, adhesions-1). There were no postoperative complications in 49 patients (78.9%). Intraoperative complications were: bleeding from the pulmonary artery (2 patients); bronchial stump leak (2 patients) (thoracoscopically repaired). No intraoperative deaths occurred. Late mortality occurred in 1 (1.6%) patient due to an acute MI. Other complications were: lobar atelectasis-6 cases (8.7%), air leak-6 (8.7%), empyema-3 (4.4%), pneumonia-3 (4.4%), CVA-1 (1.6%), pulmonary insufficiency-1 (1.6%). Hospital stay was 5.7 days (mean). Conclusions: Our clinical impression in this early retrospective study is that after thoracoscopic lobectomy, hospital stay is shorter and there is a lower incidence of morbidity and mortality, and fewer complications.
