TI  - Risk of recurrence of resected stage i non-small cell lung cancer in elderly patients as compared with younger patients
AU  - Goodgame, B.
AU  - Viswanathan, A.
AU  - Zoole, J.
AU  - Gao, F.
AU  - Miller, C.R.
AU  - Subramanian, J.
AU  - Meyers, B.F.
AU  - Patterson, A.G.
AU  - Govindan, R.
PY  - 2009
DO  - 10.1097/JTO.0b013e3181b6bc1b
AB  - Purpose: Half of all patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) are 70 years or older at the time of diagnosis. Surgery is an option for fit elderly patients with early stage disease, but rates of disease recurrence after surgical resection are not well described. We report the outcomes in elderly patients (70 years or older) with stage I NSCLC after surgical resection. Patients and Methods: We conducted a retrospective study of patients diagnosed with stage I NSCLC after surgical resection at Washington University School of Medicine-Alvin J. Siteman Cancer Center from 1990 to 2000. Demographic, pathologic, treatment, and follow-up data were collected. Recurrence rates and overall survival were calculated by the Kaplan-Meier method. Multivariate Cox proportional hazards models were used to detect associations between potential prognostic factors and survival and recurrence. Results: Of the 715 patients with stage I NSCLC, 286 were 70 years or older at diagnosis. In this elderly cohort, the median age was 74 years (range, 70-89 years) and 140 of them were women (49%). Lobectomy was performed in 237 patients (83%) whereas 43 patients (15%) had a wedge or segmental resection, and six patients (2%) underwent pneumonectomy. Clinical and pathologic characteristics were not statistically different between the elderly and younger cohorts, with the exception that older patients were more likely to be white (90% versus 80%, p = 0.0003) and less likely to be smokers (88% versus 95%, p = 0.019) compared with the younger cohort. With a median follow-up of 4.6 years, the overall 5-year survival rate was 52% with a 5-year recurrence rate of 24%. In comparison, the patients younger than 70 years had a 5-year survival rate of 67% (p < 0.001) and a 5-year recurrence rate of 24%. ConclusionS: Although overall survival was worse in elderly patients, estimated disease recurrence rates after resection were identical. © 2009 by the International Association for the Study of Lung Cancer.
ID  - 3504
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Advances in surgery for lung cancer
AU  - Shim, Y.M.
PY  - 2009
DO  - 10.1111/j.1440-1843.2009.01656.x
AB  - Surgery offers the best chance for cure for patients with stage I or II non-smallcell lung cancer (NSCLC). However, with advances in operative techniques and improvement of perioperative management, more aggressive surgical resection can be attempted, for instance, even in patients with stage IIIB. About one-third of NSCLC presents with locally advanced stage III disease, and about half of them had stage IIIB. Although patients with stage IIIB disease are typically treated with definitive chemoradiation, surgery can be offered to highly selected patients as a single modality or after induction chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy. Patients who are staged T4 due to satellite tumour nodules, carinal involvement or superior vena cava invasion can be a good candidate for surgical treatment as long as there is no evidence of N2 or N3 disease. On the other hand, as opposed to aggressive approach for locally advanced disease, video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) lobectomy has been performed with increasing frequency for early-stage NSCLC as a minimally invasive approach. Advocates of VATS lobectomy emphasize the benefit in terms of less tissue trauma, reduced cytokine release, decreased postoperative pain, lower complication rates, and shorter hospital stay. A recent meta-analysis of randomized and nonrandomized trials on safety and efficacy of VATS lobectomy demonstrated that VATS lobectomy is an appropriate procedure for selected patients with early-stage NSCLC when compared with open surgery. However, questions still remain to be answered as to whether VATS lobectomy reduces the local recurrence and improves the long-term survival in patients with NSCLC. Therefore, more evidence supporting the oncologic efficacy of VATS lobectomy for patients with NSCLC needs to be accumulated to gain its widespread acceptance in clinical practice. Nevertheless, many studies favouring VATS lobectomy in terms of oncologic efficacy are being reported in recent days. Therefore, VATS lobectomy can be considered as a feasible option for patients that are surgically resectable as long as there is no compromise of standard oncologic principles of thoracic surgery.
ID  - 3505
