TY  - JOUR
TI  - Factors associated with recurrence in patients with curatively resected stage I and II lung cancer
AU  - Koo, H.-K.
AU  - Lim, H.-J.
AU  - Lee, C.-H.
AU  - Yim, J.-J.
AU  - Kim, Y.T.
AU  - Yang, S.-C.
AU  - Yoo, C.-G.
AU  - Kim, J.H.
AU  - Shim, Y.-S.
AU  - Kim, Y.W.
PY  - 2009
DO  - 10.1111/j.1440-1843.2009.01658.x
AB  - Background/Purpose: Patients with stage I-II non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) show variability in recurrence after curative resection. Old age, diabetes, lymphovascular invasion, non-squamous histology, pneumonectomy, multi-stational N1 nodes, visceral pleural invasion, and adjuvant chemotherapy have been proposed as prognostic factors for recurrence in various studies. However, the results of various studies were not concordant. The aim of our study was to identify risk factors for recurrence in patients with curatively resected stage I-II NSCLC. Methods: We reviewed the medical records of pathologic stage I-II NSCLC patients after curative surgery, who had been followed more than 5 years to evaluate the recurrence, in a tertiary referral hospital (Seoul National University Hospital) from January 2002 to December 2004. Demographic factor, surgical technique, radiologic, histopathologic, and laboratory finding were analyzed. Patients with invasive cancer other than lung cancer before 5 years from surgery were excluded. Results: A total of 310 patients was included. Among them, local recurrence occurred in 27 patients (8.7%) while distant recurrence in 79 patients (25%). On univariate analysis, the factors associated with recurrence were adenocarcinoma histology, poorly differentiated carcinoma, invasion of visceral pleura, the number of positive lymph nodes, involvement of hilar lymph node, tumour size, preoperative carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) level (>2.3 ng/mL), and maximal SUV of tumour in PET (>4.5). On multivariate analysis, adenocarcinoma (HR, 2.35; P = 0.02), tumour size (HR, 1.77; P < 0.01), CEA level (>2.3 ng/mL) (HR, 1.70; P = 0.04) were significantly associated with recurrence. Hilar node involvement (HR 25.49, P = 0.001), CEA level (HR, 3.93; P = 0.009), and diabetes (HR, 2.87; P = 0.02) were related to local recurrences, while adenocarcinoma (HR, 7.49; P < 0.01), poorly differentiated carcinoma (HR, 3.30; P = 0.01), tumour size (HR, 2.24; P < 0.01) were associated with distant recurrence. Conclusions: Diabetes, CEA level, adenocarcinoma, poorly differentiated carcinoma, tumour size, and hilar node involvement could be considered prognostic factors for recurrence in the patients with curatively resected stage I-II NSCLC.
