TY  - JOUR
TI  - Robotic-assisted thorascopic surgery (RATS): The next evolution in minimal invasive thoracic surgery
AU  - Lee, K.A.
PY  - 2009
DO  - 10.1097/IMI.0b013e3181a5aa85
AB  - Objective: Lung cancer is the leading cause of all cancer related deaths in the United States with an estimated 215,000 new lung cancers expected for 2008, accounting for approximately 15% of cancer diagnosis. Minimal Invasive Surgery (MIS) introduced the video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) technology to the discipline of thoracic surgery. Procedures requiring thoracotomy incisions are now implemented through small port incisions, resulting in a reduction of complications after thoracotomy. Morbidity and mortality for VATS lobectomy are comparable or lower than the thoracotomy. Disadvantages associated with VATS are limited visualization and maneuverability of instrumentation. The next evolution in MIS technology is a four-arm robotic system, which redefines and enhances the VATS lobec- tomy. Methods: Eighty patients undergoing pulmonary lobectomy using the da VinciS robotic system (Intuitive Surgery, Sunnyvale, CA) were prospec- tively studied. Setup times, total operative times, extubation time, length of hospital stay, and complications were achieved. Results: Study comprises 32 men and 48 women (mean age 64 years). Indications: early-stage non-small cell lung cancer 69 patients, 2 small cell, 5 metastatic, and 4 benign lesions. Operative procedures included 11 RUL, 3 RU/ML, 9 RML, 25 RLL, 14 LUL, and 18 LLL. No operative mortality. Setup time 20 minutes. Total operative time averaged 165 minutes. Complications included air leaks, atrial fibrillation, and one C. Difficle colitis. All patients were extubated in the operating room. Length of stay within 2 days 31 patients (39%), within 3 days 49 (61%), and 70 patients (88%) were discharged within 5 days. Conclusions: Robotic-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (RATS) enhances VATS lobectomy with advantages as 3-D visualization, magnification, motion scaling, tremor filtration, and natural visual-spatial relationships contributing to increased surgical precision. The four-armed robot allows the surgeon to self-assist while imparting two free hands for surgical dissection. Complete endoscopic versus assisted thoracoscopic lobectomies for early- stage lung cancers showed no significant differences in analgesia requirements but shorter length of stay in the purely endoscopic approach with preservation of the immune system. RATS lobectomy is consistent as the VATS, with similar outcomes as the VATS lobectomy, and superior results in decrease pain and length of stay compared with conventional thoracotomy.
